        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Barton</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasFullName"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/person/0d81dce2-deb0-4663-af18-673f6d22ef86"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">C C Barton</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasGivenName"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/person/0d81dce2-deb0-4663-af18-673f6d22ef86"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">C C</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasFamilyName"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/person/0d8dbcc6-c142-40e2-aef3-10676d095e61"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Shoberg</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasFullName"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/person/0d8dbcc6-c142-40e2-aef3-10676d095e61"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">T Shoberg</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasGivenName"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/person/0d8dbcc6-c142-40e2-aef3-10676d095e61"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">T</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/000eeaac-8ab2-41fc-96e1-728aa430344f"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In this study we examine the processes that lead to the generation of debris flows from basins recently burned by wildfire.
Throughout the summer 2000 thunderstorm season, we monitored rain gages, channel cross-sections, and nine sediment-runoff
traps deployed in a steep, 0.1 km2 basin burned by the May 2000 Cerro Grande fire in New Mexico.  Debris flows were triggered
in the monitored basin during a rainstorm on July 16, 2000, when 25.6 mm of rain fell in 1.5 hours (return period of ~1.0
year).  Numerous storms with shorter return periods occurred before and after the July storm; these storms resulted in
significant runoff, but did not generate debris flows.
The debris flows generated by the July 16 storm initiated primarily in subtle swales and on a broad, open hillslope, at
distances between 15 and 25 m from the ridgecrest.  The debris-flow paths were delineated by continuous levees up to 0.25-m
high composed of gravel- and cobble- sized material in abundant fine-grained matrix.  A lack of soil-slip scars at the heads
of the paths, and observations of erosion of surficial material by overland flow and rilling, suggests that progressive
sediment entrainment by surface runoff, rather than infiltration-triggered failure of discrete soil slips, was the primary
mechanism of debris-flow initiation.  Ratios of sediment production to runoff volume collected in the hillslope traps during
the debris-flow producing storm ranged from 0.0015 to 0.002 kg/ml, and from 0.0008 to 0.001 kg/ml for storms that did not
result in debris flows.  These values suggest sediment concentration threshold conditions for debris-flow production.  The
debris flows traveled the length of the hillslope, increasing in width and volume, and entered a first-order channel.
Remnants of debris-flow deposits from this event up to 0.50-m thick were observed along the length of the channel.  A
resurvey of the channel cross-sections showed that no significant erosion occurred during this event.  Thus, the great
majority of material in the debris flows was derived from the hillslopes.  Rainstorms with longer return periods may result
in channel erosion and a significant contribution of material to future debris flows.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0026d12f-4bb6-43a3-b9c5-20f3ed695ec1"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Lake Baikal in Russia, the oldest and deepest continental lake in the world lies in south central Siberia.  The lake occupies
the deepest portion of the Baikal Rift Zone, one of the two major continental rift zones in the world.  The rift is over  9
km deep.  Lake Baikal was formed some 35-40 million years ago.  It contains one-fifth of all the fresh water on Earth.  The
lake is a self-contained aquatic system.  This isolated ecosystem is home to more than 1,500 unique endemic species.  These
are three-fourth of the plant and animal species living there.  The microbial diversity in this environment has not yet been
fully examined or harvested for products and processes of commercial interest and value.  Here we report on the collection of
sub-bottom sediment samples collected in the framework of the international Baikal Drilling Project (BDP) and on the
preliminary characterization of the microbial diversity present.
Under field conditions, aseptic sample collection methods were used as far as possible.  Up to 10 million-year-old sub-bottom
sediments were sampled.  Isolation efforts focused on bacilli, actinomycetes, and fungi for their potential biotechnology
application and value.  Biodiversity was characterized via fatty acid methyl ester (FAME) analysis, genomic DNA
fingerprinting and restriction enzyme profiling of the isolates.
A new sample treatment technique that separated viable microorganisms from dead cells and sample matrix particles in a
high-frequency electromagnetic field helped increase the ratio of culturable microorganisms by up to two orders of magnitude.
Over 2,100 strains were isolated and preserved.  They are maintained at $-$86 \deg C.  The number of colony forming units
depended on sampling depth, incubation temperature and media used.  FAME as a preliminary characterization tool proved to be
very helpful.  The method is simple, eloquent, and highly reproducible.  Genomic DNA fingerprinting showed no significant
decrease in microbial diversity with sampling depth.  DNA polymorphism of closely related strains was detected via
restriction enzyme expression patterns.  Beyond wide spread activities, rare combinations and novel restriction endonucleases
were identified.
Lake Baikal represents a unique niche in nature.  We undertook the first step in bioprospecting this untapped wealth for
exploring the microbial diversity and analyzing the microbial products of high commercial interest and value.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/002ef928-cb96-4558-833e-c491d3cac7f9"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Surprisingly little effort has been invested so far into exploring the risks and policy implications of climate change
impacts characterized by discontinuous climate impact response functions (CIRFs). There are several plausible explanations,
like the uncertainties about the underlying geophysical processes, the time scales involved, which make the assessment of
socioeconomic impacts highly speculative, and the difficulties of applying traditional decision analytical frameworks to
problems involving such spatial and temporal scales and complexity.
This presentation addresses one of the discontinuous CIRFs, the potential collapse of the thermohaline circulation (THC). Our
current understanding of these large geophysical systems is rather vague. The question here is whether, based on what we
know about them today, do these risks influence near-term climate policy or do we have the time to improve our understanding
of these processes, our estimates of thresholds and the possible implications of crossing them.
The presentation is structured as follows: first, it provides some background information about recent efforts to come to
grips with the THC problem. This is followed by presenting results from a scenario run prepared by using the CLIMBER (Climate
and Biosphere) model (developed at the Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research in Potsdam, Germany). The scenario
depicts the evolution of annual mean temperatures in the North-Atlantic region as a result of a fast increase of global
carbon dioxide emissions in the 21st century. The presentation then contemplates possible impacts of the fast temperature
increase (as a result of global warming) followed by the temperature decline (as a result of the shutdown of the THC) over
the next millennia.
Next, emission corridors of all future global carbon dioxide emission paths that would avoid crossing the estimated THC
threshold are presented. These model runs are based on the climate module of the integrated assessment model developed in the
ICLIPS (Integrated Assessment of Climate Protection Strategies) at PIK. The presentation closes by offering preliminary
conclusions about the policy implications of the risk of the THC collapse and by outlining interesting directions for future
research on this issue.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00331855-893f-4f7a-a9f4-2408c8df0b21"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The trapped electron flux in Earth&#39;s magnetosphere is highly dynamic, varying over many orders of magnitude on time scales
ranging from minutes to days to years.  This variability is driven by a complex chain of processes extending from the solar
wind into the inner magnetosphere.  The radiation belt electrons exhibit both abrupt flux decreases as well as increases,
driven by the changing solar wind conditions.  In this paper we present observations of abrupt flux decreases driven by
geomagnetic activity.  The particle dynamics are found to have significant local time and energy dependence that develop over
roughly a ten-hour period beginning with the onset of the activity.  The $andgt;$2 MeV electrons are found to drop fairly
abruptly at given local times, but not simulaneously at different local times.  It is shown that the flux dropout is due to
the development of local tail-like magnetic field stretching, rather than due to more global processes such as ring current
buildup or large-scale radial diffusion.  It is also found that while the lower energy electrons ($andlt;$300 keV) fully recover
by the end of the geomagnetic activity, the $andgt;$2 MeV electrons are lost from the magnetosphere and do not recover.  Various
particle loss mechanisms will be discussed in light of the specific spatial and energy dependencies of the observed particle
dropouts.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/003695c2-2cb7-474c-950a-94b3fd1b1f8c"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Large areas of the conjugate mid-Norway and East Greenland passive rifted margins are underlain by a high-seismic-velocity
lower-crustal body (HVB) commonly interpreted to represent a late-synrift to breakup-aged magmatic underplate. The geometry
of the HVB is constrained by industry and academic seismic data. Wide-angle reflection and refraction studies, including
ocean-bottom seismometer (OBS) profiles and expanded-spread profiles (ESP), delineate the body as a field of P-wave
velocities between 7.1 and 7.4 km/s. Multichannel reflection profiles in places clearly image the top of the HVB and
indicates internal structure. On the Norwegian side the geometry is constrained by ESP experiments and a grid of OBS lines
spaced 20 to 40 km apart. Coverage on the Greenland side is considerably coarser. Reconstructed to breakup (54 Ma), the HVB
measures 300 km normal to the margins, 450 km along the margins, locally reaches 8 km thickness, and has a volume of ca.
450,000 km$^{3}$. The base, defining the present Moho, lies at depths ranging from 18 to 25 km.
The history of the mid-Norway and East Greenland margins includes several episodes of crustal thinning beginning in at least
the Permian and ending with the &quot;successful&quot; Late Cretaceous through Paleocene rifting episode. Calculations of crust and
underplate geometry near breakup indicate that the underplate is thickest beneath the thinnest areas of Paleocene crust, yet
the crustal thinning largely occurred in earlier rifting episodes. Thus if the underplate is interpreted to be of breakup age
the distribution of the underplate primarily reflects the cumulative extensional history, rather than the distribution of
late syn-rift extension. This relationship implies considerable lateral magmatic flow.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/003f3c05-ea83-40f7-ae85-0b62af1884fb"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Evaluation of contributions of pyroxenite lithologies to basalt formation requires recognition of effects of pyroxenite
composition on partial melting behavior. Possible pyroxene-rich lithologies in basalt source regions range from refractory
ol-websterites to fertile basalt-like compositions. Partial melting experiments at 2.0-3.0 GPa and 1275-1525$\deg$C using a
composition similar to presumed recycled oceanic crust indicate that even small proportions of fertile pyroxenite can
significantly enhance melt production during pressure-release melting, but associated in situ fractionation of trace
elements, such as so-called garnet signatures, will be small owing to large extents of partial melting and low garnet modes
of the fertile pyroxenite.
At 3 GPa the solidus of our basaltic pyroxenite is 1310$\pm$12$\deg$C, nearly 150$\deg$C lower than that of peridotite at the
same pressure. Near the peridotite solidus the degree of pyroxenite partial melting is $\sim$60$\%$ at 3 GPa
($\sim$1475$\deg$C) and $\sim$77$\%$ at 2 GPa ($\sim$1375$\deg$C). Along the peridotite dry adiabat between 2 and 3 GPa, the
productivity of the fertile pyroxenite is $\sim$1.7$\%$/km.  Thus, fertile pyroxenite should begin to melt 35-45 km below the
peridotite solidus and melt almost completely during upwelling in a typical oceanic ridge environment. So, for triangular
(MORB-like) melting regions, partial melts of pyroxenite could be derived from much larger volumes of mantle than the volume
of peridotite that experiences significant melting and could greatly enhance the crustal thickness; e.g., for major
peridotite partial melting beginning at 65 km and generating 6 km of crust, crustal thickness increases to 7, 8 and 11 km for
1, 2 and 5$\%$ fertile pyroxenite, respectively.  We therefore infer that the abundance of this type of pyroxenite in MORB
source regions is unlikely to be $andgt;$1$\%$.
The high degree of fertile pyroxenite partial melting near the peridotite solidus indicates that melts contributed to MORB or
OIB from these lithologies will be basaltic, not dacitic as sometimes postulated. Also, maximum garnet modes in our
experiments are 18$\%$ at the 3 GPa pyroxenite solidus, much less than the 30-50$\%$ commonly assumed in geochemical models.
The combination of high melt fraction and low garnet mode prevents fertile pyroxenites from imparting strong garnet
signatures: e.g., they cannot explain the high Th-excess or elevated Sm/Yb observed in MORB.  If pyroxenites are responsible
for significant major element signatures in OIB or garnet signatures in MORB, they must be much less fertile than typically
presumed recycled oceanic crust compositions.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0045a4b3-98a7-43ec-bf39-a8cb3e39595e"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Trout Lake Water, Energy, and Biogeochemical Budgets (WEBB) project is one of five sites in the U.S. Geological Survey&#39;s
WEBB Program.  The Trout Lake WEBB site is co-located with the North Temperate Lakes LTER project in north-central Wisconsin.
The Trout Lake WEBB research, which is concerned primarily with watershed processes, was designed to complement the NTL-LTER
research focus.  Previous work has included: (1) modeling of the ground-water  system; (2) investigations of water sources
and flow paths; and (3) biogeochemical process research at the stream sediment-water interface.  Overall findings include:
(1) The combined use of a screening model with traditional hydraulic measurements allows construction of an improved,
simplified ground-water-flow model; (2) A reasonable understanding of water sources and flowpaths can be accomplished with
the combined use of water isotopes, strontium isotopes and traditional hydraulic measurements.  With this approach, reliable
prediction of flowpaths that range from several meters to nearly a kilometer can be made; (3) The sediment-water interface in
streambeds-where ground water interacts with stream water-plays a major role in the carbon cycling in a watershed.
Continuing research includes integrated ground-water  and surface-water modeling, refined flow-path investigations,
multidisciplinary work at the stream sediment-water interface, and modeling of watershed-scale carbon dynamics.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/004c440c-1398-4cbe-9b61-b854bf440e16"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Recent studies at a range of scales suggest the nature of fault growth may differ significantly between the slow- and
fast-spreading ridges, with the mechanics evolving through time as faults link together, accumulate strain, and potentially
reach a size sufficient to penetrate the brittle layer.  Mature fault populations on the flanks of both slow- and
fast-spreading ridges show exponential size-frequency distributions.  Fractal distributions, however, have been shown for
shorter faults (andlt; 5 km) in narrow zones within the axis of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and the Icelandic spreading center.  Such a
transition from fractal to exponential distribution is consistent with the evolution of a fault population growing in a
brittle lithosphere of finite thickness.  A similar transition at a lower level of strain is anticipated at the fast
spreading East Pacific Rise, but data are lacking.  Slow-spreading ridges have faults across a range of length scales
(0.15-50 km) that exhibit displacement to length (d/L) ratios similar to those typical of terrestrial normal faults
(~0.015-0.30).  Mature faults on fast-spreading ridges (data exist only for faults with length andgt; 5 km) exhibit d/L ratios at
the extreme low-end of terrestrial studies and systematically less than at slow ridges. Inferring that the mechanical
thickness of the near-ridge brittle layer limits the ability of a fault system to accrue displacement and impedes elastic
interaction between simultaneously growing faults, we view this thickness as a controlling factor on the variations in
observations and mechanical style.  A feedback mechanism likely exists between the processes of faulting, the longevity of
magma chambers, thermal structure, and brittle layer thickness. We expect a similar pattern of lower d/L in areas with higher
magma supply, even for a given spreading rate, because of the expected thinner brittle layer there.  We suspect a yes answer
to our question.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0057e6bf-0dcd-4f6f-be2d-dda1f9cf5844"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The lack of kinetic and product data on the reaction between mercury and halogens prevents from making any definite
conclusions about rapid transformations of atmospheric Hg$^{0}$ observed during the spring in the Arctic. Here we present our
results on laboratory kinetic and mechanistic study of the reactions involving gaseous mercury and molecular and atomic
chlorine and bromine. Reaction between mercury and Cl$_{2}$/Br$_{2}$ was studied by following the mercury concentration using
cold vapour atomic absorption spectroscopy, while relative rate kinetic method was used for investigation of Hg reaction
with Cl and Br atoms in air and nitrogen at different temperatures.  Atomic chlorine and bromine were generated by in situ UV
photolysis (240-250 nm) of CCl$_{3}$COCl and CH$_{2}$BrCOBr or CH$_{2}$Br$_{2}$; concentrations of mercury and reference
were monitored using GC-MS operating in SIM mode.  Reaction products in both gaseous and condensed phases were analysed using
MS, Raman, and UV spectroscopy.  The preliminary kinetic and mechanistic data obtained in this study will be discussed.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00595ecd-2022-4466-9905-c53aae67414c"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The ability of apatite to concentrate significant amounts of trace elements including rare earth elements (REE) that may
reflect the geochemical state of the crystallization medium makes it valuable geological indicator mineral. In order to
clarify the genesis of apatite and to evaluate its applicability as indirect biomarker in Early Archean supracrustal rocks of
Isua belt and Akilia enclave ($andgt;$ 3.7 Ga), a detailed REE study using laser ablation ICPMS has been carried out on
individual apatite grains extracted from metasedimentary units, mafic dykes/lavas and metasomatic veins.
The chondrite-normalized REE distribution patterns of apatite in most Isua cherts and banded iron formations (BIFs) are
distinctive from non-sedimentary lithologies, and display relatively flat profile with variable positive Eu anomaly. Such REE
distribution patterns are consistent with the REE data from several other Archean sedimentary formations, suggesting that
apatite in Isua sediments has sampled the REE composition of Archean ocean and that the metamorphic overprinting was largely
isochemical. Positive Eu anomalies are strongest in magnetite rich BIFs, while the cherts, that contain very little or no
magnetite, display weak positive Eu anomalies. Such a correlation between magnetite abundance and Eu anomaly is consistent
with BIF accumulation in immediate vicinity of hydrothermal venting sites that would have supplied both Fe and Eu. The weak
positive Eu anomaly in Fe-poor cherts suggests their precipitation in areas outside of direct hydrothermal influence or
during periods of low hydrothermal activity.
The &quot;hat-shape&quot;, strongly MREE enriched distribution patterns are typical for apatite in metasomatic, carbonate rich veins
that cut across both sedimentary and non-sedimentary units. The similarity of apatite REE signatures in spatially separated
veins cutting through different rock types suggests homogeneity of metasomatic fluids, unfractionated REE uptake and
insignificant influence of the host rock upon the REE composition of the vein apatite. The REE abundances in apatite from
mafic, chlorite rich rocks commonly display distinct negative Eu anomaly on the background of left-inclined, LREE depleted
distribution profiles.
The REE distribution patterns of apatite in a quartzite unit of the Akilia enclave that has been interpreted as
metachert/BIF, are characteristically Eu depleted, atypical of chemically formed Archean sediments. The observed negative Eu
anomaly may reflect the Eu reduction and selective removal of relatively large Eu$^{2+}$ during granulite facies metamorphism
that Akilia rocks have experienced. However, such extensive alteration would require exceptionally high fluid/rock ratios to
rework the protholith. If severe heterochemical alteration indeed has affected the Akilia quartzite then rock components
other than REE were likely also to have been displaced. This complicates reliable identification of the sedimentary
protholith on the basis of bulk rock chemical data. The amphibolite grade alteration of Isua metasediments has apparently not
substantially altered their REE distribution, which retain a signature characteristic for Archean ocean with enhanced Eu
concentration.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00601e4c-193b-4e9a-8cba-37426379acb8"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We used the 1999 Chi-Chi earthquake and its aftershocks to model the 3D geometry of the Chelungpu fault and of several other
faults in the area. We then combined the resulting faults with those we obtained using background seismicity from the Chi-Chi
area all the way to offshore eastern Taiwan. Fault geometries are constrained by a combination of hypocenter locations and
geological data from surface, wells, and seismic profiles.
We applied a &quot;collapsing method&quot; to both the Chi-Chi earthquake aftershocks and to the background seismicity in order to
obtain clustered sets of earthquakes that better define fault surfaces. Then we used Gocad to generate the fault surfaces,
combining seismicity with the other kinds of data, where available.
The model shows the presence of a major surface (about 11,000 square km) at 10 km depth beneath central Taiwan, spanning 3/4
of the island width. Several thrust faults, among which the Chelungpu thrust, ramp up from this surface at its western end,
where it is subhorizontal. At its eastern end instead, starting around 10 km W of the Longitudinal Valley, the surface
steeply dives down to a maximum depth of about 60 km. Most of the faults are located in the hanging wall of this large
structure; there are however indications of some active deformation occurring in the footwall too.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0060232d-8738-4998-aecb-52851db40072"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The NASA Langley Atmospheric Sciences Data Center produces, archives and distributes data from the Multi-angle Imaging
SpectroRadiometer (MISR) instrument aboard the Terra Spacecraft.  MISR provides radiometrically and geometrically calibrated
images at each of nine widely-spaced angles with a spatial resolution of 275m to 1100m.
Level 1 data products from June 1, 2000 onward are publicly available.  These data include the Level 1B2 product, which
contains conditioned radiance values that are coregistered and geolocated in each of four spectral bands: blue, green, red,
and near infrared.
The MISR Level 2 products, first available in the Fall of 2000, provide derived atmospheric parameters for aerosols and
clouds, as well as retrieved surface bidirectional reflectances and albedos.
These data sets may be ordered from the NASA Langley Atmospheric Sciences Data Center at http://eosweb.larc.nasa.gov@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0066cb02-46d2-4734-acf5-010a4128b10f"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Traditional transport models have failed to provide adequate
descriptions of contaminant distributions in the vadose zone of Hanford&#39;s Tank Farms. Tank fluids leaked to the vadose zone
are hypersaline with normality near 10 and
specific gravity of 1.4. In this study, it is hypothesized that deviation between observed and predicted plume distributions
is related to the fluid constitution, its effect on surface tension, equilibrium contact angle, and
ultimately the soil water retention function. A thermodynamic model that describes interfacial tension and equilibrium
contact angle, as functions of aqueous phase salt concentration, is incorporated into the STOMP numerical
simulator and used to predict the infiltration of hypersaline fluids through the vadose zone. Specific wetting fronts
features, which are absent in the pure fluid case, are identified and quantified. Laboratory observations and numerical
simulations suggest the elevated surface tensions observed in hypersaline fluids may play a fundamental role in water and
contaminant migration through unsaturated soils.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/007db1bb-5714-431d-af8a-d4a87046a099"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Dry air-surface exchange processes for a given substance are controlled by both meteorological and surface conditions.  Over
regional scales, meteorological models can be used to estimate local wind speed, air temperature, water vapor mixing ratio,
solar radiation, and soil moisture content, all of which can be important in the simulation of dry deposition of trace
chemicals for a particular time period and location.  In this study, dry deposition velocities (downward fluxes divided by
concentration at a specified height) are calculated by using Argonne&#39;s Dry Deposition and Emission Model.  The surface
meteorological inputs to this model were obtained with the Mesoscale Meteorological Model (version 5, MM5).  The surface
characteristics are inferred from the normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI, resolution of about 8 km by 8 km) derived
from satellite data and land use information (1 km by 1 km) from the U.S. Geological Survey.  A test case involved
computation of the deposition velocities for ozone, sulfur dioxide, and nitric acid vapor over the eastern United States for
May 1997, for several times of the day (0900, 1200, 1500, and 1800 hr local standard time).  For deciduous forest, which
covers much of the domain, the largest deposition velocities seen for ozone were 0.8-1.0 cm/s, at 1200 hr; and much smaller
deposition velocities (near 0.1 cm/s) occurred at 1800 hr.  The large diurnal variation in deposition velocity for ozone is
caused by changes, driven mostly by solar radiation, in canopy stomatal resistance.  By contrast, the deposition velocity for
nitric acid vapor was much larger, typically 2-4 cm/s, because the surface resistance to uptake of nitric acid vapor is
negligible.  The deposition velocity for sulfur dioxide over vegetated land surfaces is similar to that for ozone in
magnitude and diurnal variability.  Over bodies of water, the deposition velocity for sulfur dioxide behaves similarly to
that for nitric acid vapor, because of the strong solubility of sulfur dioxide in water.  For sulfur dioxide, ozone, and
other substances whose uptake by vegetation is controlled by canopy stomatal resistance, soil moisture stress can
significantly decrease the deposition velocities.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/009fe02b-655b-4442-a662-7dfa96a077d5"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Data assimilative models typically minimize a penalty
functional that measures model-data misfit and the size of
model adjustments. The penalty functional builds statistical
assumptions about model error into the assimilation
procedure. Usually, errors from different parts of the model
(e.g., dynamics and boundary conditions) are presumed to
be uncorrelated. But such assumptions are clearly not valid
in regional models where uncertain large-scale forcing
affects the open-ocean boundary conditions.  Cross terms can
be added so that the penalty functional allows for correlated
model errors.  While this clutters the inversion, there are
realistic scenarios where such terms are necessary for
reasonable results.  The example here uses realistic
bathymetry in a coastal setting. The detrimental effects of
neglecting model-error correlation become evident when the
assimilation problem is restated in its equivalent formulation as an exercise in objective analysis. This well
known technique was introduced to oceanography by (in
alphabetical order) Bretherton, Davis, and Fandry (1976).
The specific question: If one has a limited number of
current-meter moorings with which to estimate coastal
wind-driven currents in a regional data-assimilative model,
should the moorings be placed near the open boundaries so
as to directly influence the uncertain open boundary
conditions, or should they be placed in the interior of
the domain and allowed to influence the open boundary
conditions from afar?  The answer is highly sensitive to
the formulation of the data assimilative model.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00a43777-35c0-42a5-aa90-0e129bffc402"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The spring transition from wintertime horizontal thermal stratification to the summertime vertically stratified state on a
wide, sloping mid-latitude continental shelf is investigated.  The Sigma Z-Level Model (SZM), a three-dimensional primitive
equation numerical ocean model employing a hybrid sigma coordinate and z-level coordinate in the vertical, simulates the
continental shelf region.  The response of continental shelf water forced by realistic surface fluxes is examined by running
a pseudo two-dimensional (x-z) form of the SZM with daily heat and wind stress records from the West Florida shelf region.  A
term-by-term evaluation of the depth-averaged time-integrated temperature equation quantifies the significance of the
physical processes that can alter the horizontal temperature gradient.  A simple model that describes the heating rate of a
homogeneous water column appears as the dominant balance in the integrated temperature equation, and agrees to the SZM model
results from the simulation with realistic forcing to within 31\% error over the shelf.  It is shown that two processes
describe the spring transition.  The first is the erosion of the wintertime horizontal thermal gradient and occurs on a time
scale of about two to three months, dependent upon the heat input, when the water column is being alternately subject to
periods of stabilizing heat flux and periods of vertical mixing.  The second process is the formation of the seasonal
thermocline following the last strong mixing event.  This occurs on a time scale of less than a week under a stabilizing heat
flux and light winds completing the spring transition from horizontal to vertical thermal stratification.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00a7eef2-5834-431c-9b28-a5564658f1f6"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Among John Sass&#39; many contributions to understanding crustal heat flow has been his critically-important work along San
Andreas fault system. While it is well known that the San Andreas is a major lithospheric weakness in a transpressive plate
margin setting , the markedly different styles of strain partitioning observed along strike in Central and Northern
California cannot be adequately explained in terms of relative plate motions  and fault geometry. From San Francisco bay area
northward (Northern California), strike-slip motion is accommodated on several sub-parallel strike-slip faults and
shortening associated with transpression is accommodated on widely distributed reverse faults. In contrast, in Central
California, strike slip faulting is concentrated on along a single fault (the San Andreas) and reverse faulting is
concentrated along the margins of the adjacent deforming belt (the Coast Ranges). Using a simplified 3D FEM analysis in large
strain, we have investigated how interactions between the upper and lower crust, lateral fault strength variations and the
amount of convergence affect crustal deformation and stress in Central and Northern California. We find that the difference
in tectonic style between Central and Northern California can be explained by the manner in which heat flow varies with
distance from the fault and modest variations  of fault friction along strike. Our modeling appears to provide a good
conceptual framework of first-order aspects of an actively deforming transpressive plate margins such as the SAFS and point
to the overall importance of crustal heat flow as part of comprehensive understanding of lithospheric deformation.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00af96d7-0aa8-4691-81aa-5f3a4ec3f4b9"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">During May and June, 2000, the USGS and the University of Puerto Rico, Mayaguez, coordinated a joint deployment of both land
and ocean bottom seismometers (OBS) to study the crustal and upper mantle structure beneath Puerto Rico and the region to the
north and west of the island.  An accurate determination of the pattern of tectonic stresses is helping to determine seismic
hazard potential in and around Puerto Rico.  Following an extensive revamping of the instrument design, the USGS now
operates 15 OBS capable of recording data continuously from a hydrophone and 3 geophones for at least 6 weeks and to a
maximum depth of 5000 meters. The configuration consists of an external geophone package enclosed in a bag full of sand,
which  is designed to mimic the burial of the geophone package in sediments to provide better seafloor coupling.  The Puerto
Rican experiment marks the first complete deployment of all external sensor OBS.  The current permanent land station
configuration of the Puerto Rican Seismic Network (PRSN) was upgraded to 4 broadband stations and 12 short period
seismographs.  An additional three temporary short-period land stations, provided by USGS, Menlo Park,  were added in western
Puerto Rico to fill gaps in the PRSN.  A total of 12 OBS were deployed for 45 days in two staggered lines extending away
from the coast, one to the north along the Mona Canyon, and another to the west past Mona Island.  The north trending line
continues across the island with land stations, and extends south of the island with 3 OBS sites.  This area of the
northeastern Carribean marks the mostly left-lateral strike-slip boundary between the Caribbean plate and the obliquely
subducting North American plate.  During the 45-day deployment, over 100 local events were located within the Caribbean and
North American plates as well as the Puerto Rico / Virgin Islands Platform.  These highly resolved events, combined with
previous seismicity as deep as 250 km, support the idea of a small component of subduction, resulting either from a small
degree of north-south compression along the northern Caribbean-North American plate boundary or west-facing subduction of the
North American plate along the eastern edge of the Caribbean plate.  A preliminary 1-D crustal velocity model has been
constructed to aid in improving these event locations.  Other anticipated results of the experiment are receiver function
analysis of lateral crustal heterogeneity, the assesment of individual raypath attenuation, regional S wave anisotropy, and
possible initial tomographic modeling of velocity and attenuation.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00d695c0-c175-4ed7-9d92-d7d766451ed1"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">One of the most important issues facing environmental scientists today and in the future is the extent to which human action
has influenced past species distributions and ecosystem processes, such as disturbance, and how ecosystems recover or change
as human activities subside or change.  The history of human impact in New England over the past 300 years is among the most
extensive in North America.  We know from historical records that more than half of all forested land had been converted to
open fields and pastures by the 1800s and that by the mid 1900s much of these open lands were abandoned and have become
reforested.  Ecosystem conditions before written history can only be understood through paleoecological studies, which
provide both the status of ecosystems before human impact as well as how they have changed through time along with human
activities.  We are studying the past 2000 years of change in pitch pine-oak vegetation of Cape Cod in order to establish
pre-European vegetation composition and fire occurrence and to document how these have changed over the past 300 years.
Current pressures from increasing human development and the potential importance of fire in this ecosystem call for a
thorough understanding of this long-term history for management and conservation decisions.  Our study sites show that
charcoal increased dramatically throughout most of Cape Cod at the time of European settlement, probably a result of land
clearance and burning.  Presettlement forest composition of the western portion of Cape Cod was influenced by glacial
landforms with oak more common on moraines and pine more common on outwash.  This mirrors the pattern of fire occurrence,
which was higher in pine forest on the outwash plains.  Although vegetation composition is still somewhat related to landform
today, variation among sites is not as great as it was before European settlement.  Furthermore, sites with greater levels
of human impact during the 1800s show greater change over the past 150 years.  The resulting contemporary landscape is
difficult to interpret without this long-term perspective.  Our results demonstrate the importance of paleoecological studies
for describing ecological conditions before and during extensive human impact on Cape Cod as well as the utility of
combining paleoecological and historical reconstructions.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00e528cc-6bb0-4af2-891a-a6403422873f"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Measurements from the GLAS laser altimeter onboard NASA&#39;s ICESAT satellite
and from the NASA-DLR dedicated gravity satellite mission GRACE, both
scheduled for launch in 2001 and both with expected lifetimes on the order of 5 years, can be combined to learn about ongoing
changes in polar ice mass and viscoelastic rebound of the lithosphere under the ice sheet. GRACE will map the Earth&#39;s
gravity field orders of magnitude more accurately and with considerably higher resolution than any existing satellite.  GLAS
will monitor changes in ice-sheet topography, and can provide estimates of secular changes in the total polar ice sheet mass
over the lifetime of the mission.
ICESAT/GLAS and GRACE can be combined to give better estimates of the ice sheet mass balance and post glacial rebound (PGR)
than is provided by either observable by itself, but the improvement obtained by combining data would be substantially
greater for multiple, successive missions.
We will examine spatial resolution properties of the ice mass balance estimate, and the improvement of PGR estimates that
would be provided by GPS measurements of vertical velocities.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00f644e6-afd9-4fcf-8407-8dbf68342a91"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Three dimensional plasma observations from the TAUS plasma instrument aboard the PHOBOS spacecraft have detected
non-gyrotropic alpha particle distribution functions in the solar wind. We present a study of non-gyrotropic alpha-particle
distribution functions in the presence of a spectrum of low frequency electromagnetic Alfv\&#39;en waves in an electron-proton
background plasma medium using a 1-1/2D and 2-1/2D hybrid simulations. The simulation results indicate that alpha particles
can be selectively heated and gyrophase bunched by the gyro-resonant absorption of Alfv\&#39;en waves. In the nonlinear stages of
the process, protons are also gyrophase bunched and heated by the waves. This study provides of a simple method to model
non-gyrotropic distribution functions in the absence of a closed-form analytical representation. To study the wave spectrum
generated by the non-gyrotropic alpha particle distribution functions, we initiated a hybrid-simulation experiment in the
absence of wave fluctuations and with isotropic protons and electrons. The results of such and experiment indicates that the
wave spectrum generated have a different spectral signature when compared to the Alfv\&#39;en waves &#39;ab initio&#39;. The results of
all these simulation experiments are compared with satellite TAUS observations of proton-alpha particle distribution
functions in the solar wind.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00fe6219-98cc-4253-aae9-553e9b2341b5"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The overall spatial spreading of a tracer plume, the temporal evolution of average solute fluxes across a reference surface
and the dilution of local concentrations due to local mixing processes are important output variables of transport modeling
studies. In this presentation it is illustrated how local concentration measurements in an aquifer can be used to identify
aquifer characteristics, i.e. spatial heterogeneity of the hydraulic conductivity and the local scale dispersion tensor, that
determine spreading and dilution. A first-order Lagrangian description of transport is used to predict the spatial
variability and spatial covariance of local concentrations. The first-order Lagrangian theory is verified using Monte Carlo
simulations of convective-dispersive transport in generated heterogeneous conductivity fields. In a sensitivity analysis,
parameters that unequivocally determine the spatial covariance of local concentrations are identified. The correlation scale
of the conductivity in the direction transverse to the mean flow and the local scale transverse dispersivity (local scale
transverse dispersion divided by the magnitude of the advection velocity) can be inferred unequivocally from the spatial
covariance of local concentrations. The variance and correlation scale of the conductivity in the direction of the mean flow
cannot be inferred independently since similar concentration covariances are obtained for different combinations of the
variance and correlation scale. In another application, the Lagrangian theory is used to interpret time series or
breakthrough curves (BTCs) of locally measured concentrations during a tracer test in a fluvial aquifer at Krauthausen
(Germany). A locally measured BTC is characterized in terms of an equivalent dispersivity that corresponds to the
dispersivity of an equivalent perfectly homogeneous aquifer in which the same BTC would be observed. The equivalent
dispersivity can be interpreted as a measure of dilution. Equivalent dispersivities of local BTCs are predicted by the
first-order Lagrangian theory as a function of the spatial variability of the hydraulic conductivity and the local scale
dispersivity. The local scale transverse dispersivity is estimated by comparing predictions of the equivalent dispersivity by
the Lagrangian theory with equivalent dispersivities that are derived from measured local BTCs.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/00fe9145-badf-44d1-90f9-0fdea060fbce"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">During the last decade a series of geophysical experiments, among them
the INDEPTH experiments, were
carried out in Tibet in order to elucidate the processes
associated with continental collision and the uplift of the Tibetan
plateau.  We have measured $P$ wave travel time residuals for teleseismic earthquakes
recorded by stations of the INDEPTH III array, a NNE-SSW trending
array in central Tibet, which crosses the Banggong-Nujiang suture, and of the 1994 INDEPTH II
deployment, which crosses the
Indus-Yarlung suture, extending the coverage southward. We inverted these data for a tomographic image of
the mantle beneath central and southern Tibet.
In order to minimise
the bias introduced by heterogeneity in EW direction and by
anisotropy, we constructed a two-dimensional profile using only those arrivals
whose propagation paths were
similar to the strike of the array.  This ray set
allowed the resolution of structures larger than $\sim$40 km
horizontally and $\sim$100 km vertically between latitudes
29\deg N and 34\deg N (corresponding to longitudes 89\deg E--91\deg E) and between
depths 100-400~km.
The most striking feature in the tomographic image is a fast zone whose vertically dipping northern boundary is at $\sim
32\deg$N and
whose thickness varies, as a function of depth, between 100 and 200 km,
such that its southern boundary is between 30 and 31\deg N.
The velocity contrast
across its northern boundary is 2--3\% and across its southern
boundary just over 1\%. This feature becomes well defined
below $\sim$180 km, whereas no large-scale structure is retrieved in the
uppermost mantle above $\sim$180 km.
We interprete the fast vertically-dippping structure
as the continental Indian lithosphere, which is sinking into the mantle. The
dip-angle is close to 90\deg, so it is probably misleading to
view this vertical boundary as the edge of a rigid Indian slab;
instead the anomaly is more likely to be related to
downwelling caused by a temperature-induced gravitational instability.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01017526-6887-40fe-878a-028077969f29"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Based on two high-resolution (20 to 300 years/sample) sediment records from the northern (core 17940-2, off the Pearl River)
and southwestern (core 18252-3, off the Mekong River) South China Sea (SCS), we assess the timing and phasing of major
changes in monsoonal climate during the last deglaciation, and differentiate between local and regional climate variability.
The detailed correlation of both cores is based on an AMS radiocarbon chronology and, more importantly, on the assumption of
synchroneity of major planktonic foraminiferal $\delta$$^{18}$O and/or U$^{k}$$_{37}$-SST events at both sites.
To monitor changes in fluvial and/or aeolian delivery of detrital components to the SCS, which should be a direct reflection
of changes of the monsoonal climate in the hinterland, we analyzed the major/minor element geochemistry of the two cores.
Elements that reside in the lithogenous fraction (Al, Si, K, Mg, Rb, and Zr) are used to identify major shifts in the
siliciclastic sediment supply. These records are complemented by $\delta$$^{13}$C$_{org}$ data that reflect variable
proportions of terrigenous/marine organic matter supply at the two sites.
Major changes in the geochemical composition of detrital input to the SCS lead the major deglacial events in the planktonic
$\delta$$^{18}$O and U$^{k}$$_{37}$-SST records, corroborating earlier interpretations based on grain size distribution by
Wang et al. 1999. This phasing is in contrast with records from the Arabian Sea (core 74 KL; Sirocko et al. 1993), where the
largest event of atmospheric and hydrographic reorganization appears to be synchronous in both the planktonic oxygen isotope
record and the indicators of lithic sediment supply. Taken together, these records hint at regional differences in the
temporal evolution of monsoonal climate during the last deglaciation.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01170a59-4b77-4740-b90b-2b300f5a7fb0"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Ionospheric perturbations due to geomagnetic storms and substorms are prevalent at high latitudes.  During storms and
substorms, the convection electric fields, particle precipitation, and ionospheric currents intensify, and as a result, a
large amount of energy can be deposited into the ionosphere-thermosphere system.  In response to this energy input, there is
an increase in the ionospheric electron density, the ion and electron temperatures are elevated, large O$^{+}$/NO$^{+}$
composition changes occur, localized ionospheric holes appear, and the ion velocity distributions can become non-Maxwellian.
In addition, neutral composition changes occur, wind speeds increase, and equatorward propagating waves drive the F region
plasma toward higher altitudes, which can result in ionization enhancements (positive ionospheric storm).  At later times,
the storm enhanced meridional neutral winds lead to upwellings and decreases in the O/N$_{2}$ density ration.  The latter, in
turn, results in decreased electron densities at F-region altitudes (negative ionospheric storm).  These storm and substorm
effects will be discussed.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/012452d0-7b5a-4a08-8bdd-8a6d9ea3a08a"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">To investigate basic characteristics of mantle flows within the earth, we conducted numerical simulation on the unsteady
dynamic response due to instantaneous supply of thermal anomaly mass.  The simulation code used was developed with the finite
difference method.  The basic three equations are for the continuity, the motion with the Boussinesq (incompressible)
approximation, and the (thermal) energy conservation.  The viscosity of our model is sensitive to temperature.  We used the
Newton method, to get time integration with high precision.  For solving the Jacobian matrix of the constitutive five finite
difference equations associated with the three scalar components of the velocity vector, pressure, and temperature, we
followed the preconditioned &#39;Bi-CGSTAB&#39; (Bi-Conjugate Gradient Stabilized) method.  The matrix-matrix multiplication and
incomplete LU factorization are included in the precondition.
We tried cases of instantaneous input of a local cool mass (hereafter, case A) and great-circle cool ring (case B) as a
negative buoyancy source at the base of the upper mantle, 670 km.  Also cases of initial input of a local warm mass (case C)
and great-circle warm ring (case D), as a positive buoyant source at the core-mantle boundary (CMB), are studied.  The mantle
temperature for the initial state is monotonic increasing with depth from 300 K, through approximately 2000 K at 670 km, to
4000 K at the CMB, and is laterally homogenous except the portion of the thermal anomaly mass inserted initially.  We assumed
thermal boundary layers at the surface mantle and just above the CMB.  In this study we neglected, for simplicity, the
effect of horizontal motion of the surface lithospheres, mineral phase changes, and internal heating.  We describe the result
of calculation derived for cases A to D.  Due to the negative or positive buoyancy of the thermal anomaly inserted initially
at 670 km or the CMB, respectively, the mantle drives itself a large-scale flow system to attain thermal relaxation.  We,
then, observed numerically major upwelling (for cases A and B) or downwelling (for cases C and D) due to the initial input of
thermal anomaly, as well as the secondary reverse flow cells at more distant regions from the site of the initial input.  It
seems that, for cases A and B, the plume heads of the major upwelling, rising from the thermal boundary layer above the CMB,
due to the downgoing cool mass, are the most distinct and well developed at a certain period after ten Ma.  The angle
distances for sites of the major upwelling measured from the locus of the initial input are approximately 30 deg, for cases A
and B.  Whereas, for cases C and D, the angle distance measured from the initial input site to the place of the major
downwelling is approximately 25 deg.  The simulation result presented here reveals fundamental nature of Green function-like
response associated with the dynamics due to downwelling of cool mantle and/or upwelling of hot material.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0131230b-64bc-4917-ba88-89b86578adaf"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Analysis of all the existing magnetic field data from Saturn&#39;s magnetosphere is carried out. This data arises from the three
spacecraft encounters with Saturn (Pioneer 11, Voyager 1 and 2, in 1979, 1980 and 1981 respectively).
The inbound observations of the magnetic field from Pioneer 11 and Voyager 2 (in the noon sector) show a spin-periodic
perturbation of a few nanoTeslas in the azimuthal component of the field, from the inbound magnetopause crossing to closest
approach. The outbound observations present an equivalent feature only in the case of Pioneer 11 (in the dawn sector), where
the radial and azimuthal components of the field are perturbed throughout the whole outbound pass.
It was previously shown that a dipole tilt and/or current sheet crossings are unlikely to cause these perturbations. Other
possibilities are considered, such as an outflow of plasma giving rise to a ballooning of the magnetic field. This structure
would keep rotating with the planet, hence perturbing the observed magnetic field in a periodic manner (even though the
outflowing plasma is surely sub-corotating). The successive Pioneer 11 outbound magnetopause crossings are indicative of the
boundary motions, which can be interpreted as a result of the periodic passage of the ballooning at dawn, pushing the
boundary away from the planet. As this occurs once per rotation, it therefore drives an internal modulation of the
magnetopause position. We also take the solar wind into account (since it is one of the factors determining the size of the
magnetosphere and may be the trigger of the large scale restructuring described here), and gather information from an
analysis of complementary data sets, including the energetic particles and plasma data.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01513dc2-b538-4834-a98b-8b30e3a8f55a"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Crystal structures of phase A, Mg$_{7}$Si$_{2}$H$_{6}$O$_{14}$ synthesized at 6 GPa, $900\deg$C, were refined with the single
crystal X-ray method under high-pressure conditions.  The X-ray diffraction intensities were collected at ambient, 3.6, 4.5,
6.2, 9.1, 9.4 and 10.8 GPa using an AFC-7s four-circle diffractometer (Rigaku) equipped with Mo X-ray sealed tube.  Also
synchrotron radiation at KEK (BL-10a), Tsukuba, Japan was used for collecting the intensity data of phase A at 4.5 and 9.4
GPa.  The data at 4.5 and 9.4 GPa were obtained by another run that changed the crystal orientation with the same sample.
The lattice parameters of phase A refined with synchrotron radiation are in good agreement with the compression curve of
phase A determined by Kuribayashi et al. (1999).  The isothermal bulk modulus of phase A, recalculated using sealed tube data
by third-ordered Birch-Murnaghan equation of state, is 106.2(1.6) GPa with pressure derivative K&#39;=3.7(4).
Characteristic points of phase A structure are that the directions of all tetrahedron are equal to and the non edge-shared
tetrahedra (T1) are existence.  The behavior of this tetrahedron (T1) with increasing pressure is characteristic.  Si1-O5
distance, which corresponded to apical direction of T1 tends to be increasing with pressure, in contrast, Si1-O3 distances,
which formed basal plane of T1 are shortened.  In other tetrahedron (T2), each Si-O distance is decreasing with pressure.
According to Kagi et al. (2000), hydrogen positioned near the O3 site and formed hydrogen bonding.  These hydrogen, which
formed hydrogen bonding, would influence on this characteristic behavior of T1 tetrahedron.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01695f44-f583-4ec1-ad27-f5cb30274e24"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We have developed a Spectral Finite-Element Method
(SEM) for the accurate modeling of elastic wave propagation
in realistic 3-D Earth models. The method is based upon a weak
formulation of the equations of motion and lends itself
well to massively parallel computations.
The goal is to be able to assess seismic risk in complex
3-D geological structures such as sedimentary basins.
Typical examples include densely populated regions
such as the Los Angeles or Tokyo basins, as well as Mexico City.
Strong ground motion at the surface of these models
is computed and recorded in the form of time traces (synthetic seismograms)
that can be compared to data recorded in the field.
Understanding complex phenomena such as local amplification due
to subsurface gelology and/or topography, basin edge effects,
and increased seismic risk due to the presence of low velocity and
often poorly consolidated sediments, turns out to be a challenging
issue that requires powerful 3-D visualisation tools.
Our spectral finite-element approach has been
carefully benchmarked against existing solutions, such as those
based upon either the reflectivity method, or normal-modes in the case
of global Earth models. The software is implemented based upon
the Message Passing Interface (MPI) on a large PC cluster with 156 nodes,
312 processors, and 156 Gb of memory.
The technique can accommodate anisotropy, attenuation, free surface topography,
and fluid-solid boundaries.
Very accurate absorbing boundary conditions are implemented
based upon the Perfectly Matched Layer (PML) methodology first developed
by Berenger (1994) for electro-magnetic waves and extended
to the first-order velocity-stress wave equation by Collino and Tsogka (1998).
We have extended the method to incorporate second-order systems in time.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/016a0256-8576-4bb2-8307-9bf294972306"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Based on stratigraphic studies and K/Ar dating, three major volcanic episodes have been recognized (cone forming, caldera
forming and post caldera eruption episodes). The data indicate initiation of a trachytic volcanism at 0.089Ma and wide spread
episodes of rhyolite lava flows at about 0.048Ma. The caldera forming eruption took place at about 0.008Ma. This eruption
changed the mechanism of eruption from effusive dominated to explosive producing plinian and ignimbrite flows that ended with
caldera collapse. Post caldera activities have continued to present from central but local satellite vents from the flank of
the volcano and from local fissure eruptions inside the caldera along newly developed cracks resulted from the caldera
collapse. These eruption products range in composition from trachyte to rhyolite. A rhyolitic pitchstone sampled from this
series yielded 0.003Ma.
Petrological and geochemical investigations  show that rocks from Fentale volcano consists of peralkaline rhyolites and
trachytes with few benmorite lava flows. Basalts are trasitional type displaying homogeneous incompatible element content.
Major and trace element data indicates that the magmatic history of Fentale volcano is related to successive alimentation of
shallow magma chamber or zoned magma chamber. Three magma refilling processes, that correspond to the three more mafic
eruption episodes have been recognized principally on the base of major and trace element evidences.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0173ace9-03b8-4639-a763-55dd5fec3441"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A large stockpile of plutonium has been manufactured for nuclear weapons and during nuclear power generation since the
element was first synthesized in the early 1940s.  Much of this plutonium is no longer needed and will find its way into
geologic repositories.  A portion of the inventory will inevitably be released from these repositories to the environment
where it may threaten human health or cause other adverse effects.  The potential for plutonium to be released and move to
receptors is controlled by its environmental chemistry.  This chemistry has been studied but is complicated, and
uncertainties remain concerning the environmental behavior of plutonium. One source of this uncertainty is the small number
of case studies documenting the behavior of plutonium under actual environmental conditions.
The Idaho Nuclear Technology and Engineering Center provides a case study for the behavior of plutonium in fractured basalt
aquifers.  The facility was used to reprocess spent nuclear fuels from a variety of sources, and during reprocessing volumes
of low-radioactivity waste were injected directly into the Snake River Plain Aquifer.  The waste included tritium and
plutonium (primarily relatively short-lived Pu-238).   Injection was suspended during 1982 for repair of the injection well,
and a relatively large inventory of waste was injected as a slug once the repair was completed.  Both tritium and plutonium
were subsequently detected in the two nearest monitoring wells (USGS-47 approximately 211 m east of the injection well and
USGS-40 approximately 199 m south of the injection well).
USGS-47 and USGS-40 are roughly down-gradient to the regional groundwater flow, but injected water mounded and flowed
radially outward from the injection well.  Tritium appeared in both monitoring wells less than 30 days after injection.
Plutonium arrived in USGS-47 less than 60 days after injection and moved at least 3.6 m/day.  Plutonium did not appear in
USGS-40 until approximately 360 days after injection and moved less than 0.55 m/day.  Retardation factors for plutonium
moving through the aquifer may be as low as 1 for movement into well USGS-47 and greater than 12 for movement into well
USGS-40.  The difference in plutonium migration into the two wells shows that the fractured basalt aquifer is inhomogeneous
on scales greater than 200 m.  Preliminary estimates of the amount of plutonium moving into the monitoring wells suggest that
the portion of the plutonium migrating rapidly represents approximately 5 percent of the inventory injected into the
aquifer.   The 95 percent of the inventory held near the injection well has not been detected in the monitoring wells.  The
mechanism responsible for formation of the rapidly moving plutonium fraction in the aquifer is not clear, but work by others
at the laboratory scale suggested that plutonium complexes with compounds, such as EDTA, citrate, and fluoride, or
plutonium-bearing colloids may play a role.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/018feb1a-b0c0-48ae-a13b-6e1a1a1c1772"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE) will return high
resolution measurements of the Earth&#39;s gravity field for five years
beginning in 2001. This mission will produce dramatic increases in the
accuracy of both the static geoid and monthly observations of the
time-varying geoid. The spatial resolution of these measurements
suggests that new mathematical techniques may be better suited for
geophysical investigations using GRACE.
For certain applications multiresolution representations of geoid
require much less computation than traditional spherical harmonic
analysis. For example, they are especially useful and efficient for
preserving the accuracy of local geoid information. Constructing
special wavelets for spherical geometry appear to be complicated
and the benefits quite limited. We will present a multiresolution
decomposition of the EGM-96 geoid using B-splines and appropriate mappings to deal with the spherical geometry.
Love numbers are often used to estimate the regional geoid response to
time-varying mass loads (e.g. hydrological changes). Because Love
numbers vary smoothly and asymptotically with increasing order, a
multiresolution representation of Love numbers is compact, increasing
the efficiency of calculating local geoid responses. We will present
Love numbers calculated in a wavelet basis using the PREM Earth Model.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01a478c2-fd6f-43c1-acb5-e4c2624061ad"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The stress shadow hypothesis states that earthquakes are less likely in regions where the Coulomb failure stress has been
decreased.  The hypothesis was developed after examining the effects of two great earthquakes (1906 San Francisco, California
and 1857 Ft. Tejon California) on the pattern of subsequent large earthquakes.  We test the application of the stress-shadow
hypothesis to aftershocks of large magnitude mainshocks by examining the case of two nearby earthquakes separated in time by
7 years, the 1992 Mw7.3 Landers earthquake and the 1999 Mw7.1 Hector Mine earthquake.
We use a simple model of dislocations in an elastic halfspace and calculate the Coulomb stress-change due to the 1992 Landers
earthquake at the hypocenter of the Hector Mine earthquake.  Because there is currently no consensus model of the Hector
Mine fault plane(s), we examine a range of possibilities for the hypocenter.  For the Landers earthquake, we test a number of
published slip models, including Wald and Heaton (BSSA, 1994).  We find that the answer of whether or not the Hector Mine
hypocenter occurred in the stress shadow of the Landers earthquake depends on the Landers model, on the record of earthquakes
that occurred between Landers and Hector Mine and on the poorly resolved hypocentral depth of the Hector Mine earthquake.
Therefore we conclude that it is not possible to resolve if Hector Mine occurred in a stress shadow until more precise
information becomes available.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01a878e3-2071-4088-b117-6562fed41814"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The occurrence of climate change due to increasing anthropogenic emissions of greenhouse gases is now generally accepted, but
its nature is still under debate.  Recent interpretations of this change propose that it will manifest itself as a
projection onto the pre-existing natural modes of variability of the climate system.  One possibility is that the change will
project linearly onto the modes, corresponding to a simple trend in their mean states.  A second possibility is a nonlinear
projection, corresponding to a shift of the residence frequency of the climate system in the quasi-stationary regimes
associated with the modes.  These two interpretations are evaluated using sea level pressure (SLP) and surface air
temperature (SAT) fields obtained from integrations of the Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory coupled general circulation
model forced with varying concentrations of greenhouse gases.
In the Northern Hemisphere the change in SLP projects partially onto the Arctic Oscillation-like mode.  In the Southern
Hemisphere the change initially projects almost entirely onto the Antarctic Oscillation-like mode, but once the ocean has
warmed and the sea ice has retreated the change projects negligibly.
The dominant modes of SAT unrelated to variations in sea ice remain dominant in warmer climates; however, the climate change
projects negligibly onto these modes.  Changes appear to project onto the El Ni\~{n}o/Southern Oscillation-like mode, but in
fact most of this projection relates to the mean global warming associated with the mode and not the pattern itself.
With both SLP and SAT the climate change manifests itself as a linear trend in some of the modes.  In the warmer climates the
modes are in states not frequently visited by the climate system in the present climate, and thus their change cannot be
interpreted as a shift in the residence frequency in quasi-stationary regimes.  These results suggest that recent
observational studies supporting the nonlinear interpretation may instead be more indicative of the earlier stages of the
linear interpretation.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01a8dcb2-7514-470a-b9a5-4290032f3d10"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The distributions of natural and anthropogenic inorganic carbon and CFCs in the Labrador Sea and adjacent area during
1995-1997 were studied extensively. A three-dimensional view of these distributions is presented. The Labrador Sea is a
highly dynamic region.  Labrador Sea Water (LSW), which is produced in late winter by deep convection, occupies the central
part of the Labrador Sea and contributes to the ventilation of the North Atlantic thermocline. The West Greenland Current
flows northward along the west coast of Greenland.  The Labrador Current flows southward along the Labrador Shelf and Slope
and the North Atlantic Current flows north-eastward across the mouth of the Labrador Sea.  At depth, North East Atlantic Deep
Water (NEADW) and Denmark Strait Overflow Water (DSOW) flow into the region.  The salinity and temperature of LSW, as well
as the depth to which the water mass is ventilated in the winter, have varied significantly over the last decade.  Variable
lateral mixing of slope water also modifies LSW.  These variations influence the sequestration and transport processes of
anthropogenic gases.  High precision total inorganic carbon and alkalinity measurements along with CFC-11, 12 and 113 have
been made as a part of the WOCE program.  In the central Labrador Sea, three water masses are clearly identified by
anthropogenic CO2 (CTant) and CFC distributions.  NEADW has its core around 2800m and is characterized by a salinity maximum
and low concentrations of CTant and CFC due to its relatively old ventilation age (10-15 years) compared to the underlying
DSOW (6-9 years).  DSOW is found at depths $andgt;$3500m and is characterized by lower salinity and higher concentrations of CTant
and CFCs than those found in NEADW.  LSW has low salinity and shows higher CTant and CFC concentrations than the underlying
waters.  Cold and fresh water on the Labrador Shelf transported by the Labrador Current, which has high concentration of
CFCs.  On both the east and west sides of the basin, warm and saline water masses exist at the slope regions transported from
the Irminger Sea, and relatively low concentrations of CFCs are observed.  CFC distributions also demonstrate well-defined
deep convection plumes, which showed higher concentrations of CFCs in the central Labrador Sea in 1995 and on the south-west
side of the Labrador Sea in 1997.  LSW with the elevated concentrations of CTant and CFCs spreads to the east into the
Irminger Sea and to the south into the North Atlantic.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01ae386a-3e63-4e56-a18a-4aa37973f9a6"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Increasing attention is being paid by geophysicists and geologists to the use of geomorphic data to constrain surface uplift
histories. In particular, reconstructions of palaeotopography, based primarily on traditional denudation chronology involving
the identification and correlation of erosion surfaces, have been used in a range of recent studies to infer changes in
surface elevation over geological time. In spite of the severe criticisms of traditional denudation chronology that have been
advanced since the 1960s, such reconstructions are being regarded by some researchers as key evidence for the evaluation of
geophysical models of phenomena such as mantle dynamics, orogenesis and continental rifting. We assess this problem with
particular reference to attempts to use the inferred surface uplift history of south-east Africa to test models of tectonic
uplift . We show that although reconstructing past elevations of the landsurface is generally highly problematic, the
application of techniques such as low temperature thermochronology and cosmogenic isotope analysis is significantly enhancing
our ability to reconstruct regional denudational histories over geological time spans. Such denudational records, in turn,
enable estimates of flexural isostatic uplift to be calculated which can be compared with the elevation of well-constrained
palaeo-elevation proxies, such as preserved shoreline or shallow marine deposits, to assess isostatic uplift as a component
of total surface uplift. We quantify the post-break-up pattern of denudation for the south-east margin of southern Africa,
the type area for the classic denudation chronology approach of L.C. King, using apatite fission-track thermochronology and
cosmogenic isotope analysis.  These estimates of long-term denudation are then used to constrain a model of landscape
evolution which predicts the amount of flexural isostatic uplift that has occurred in response to denudational unloading.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01d36d4d-e249-4c5d-ad47-90150e905f2f"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Hydrothermal deposits in most terrestrial impact craters are the product of relatively minor, short-lived hydrothermal
circulation.  In contrast, hydrothermal deposits in the 200-250 km diameter Sudbury crater in Canada include low temperature
diffuse regional alteration zones and discordant higher temperature zones focussed along faults near the crater center.  The
latter fed a long-lived subaqueous vent complex (Vermilion Formation). The Sudbury vent system is similar to modern deep-sea
vent systems, known for their proliferation of thermophilic bacteria, and it is an established source of precious metals.
Therefore, large impact hydrothermal deposits may be important economic resources, as well as niches for early life on Earth
(and perhaps Mars).
The Sudbury hydrothermal system was driven by heat from the 2.5-km-thick coherent melt sheet (melt mostly free of unmelted
clasts). Data from terrestrial craters indicate that there is an exponential relationship between crater size and coherent
melt sheet thickness, which in part explains why most craters smaller than Sudbury have small hydrothermal systems. This
relationship reflects not only the greater melt production in large impacts, but also the greater mixing of melt and unmelted
clasts in small craters. When the ratio of melt volume to transient crater volume is small (about 0.1), the melt forms a
thin sheet along the transient crater walls, which is completely mixed with unmelted clasts upon crater collapse. When this
ratio reaches 0.4-0.5, as at Sudbury, the mixing zone is a fraction of the melt and a coherent melt sheet forms.
Therefore, we might expect that other large craters would have thick coherent melt sheets and hydrothermal systems similar to
Sudbury&#39;s. However, the relationship between crater size and melt sheet thickness is complicated by the fact that the ratio
of melt volume to transient crater volume is greater in high-velocity comet impacts compared to low velocity asteroid
impacts. The 200-km diameter Chicxulub crater in Mexico is similar in size to Sudbury. Hydrothermal activity has been
documented at Chicxulub, but limited coring has produced no evidence of a Sudbury-like hydrothermal vent complex.  The
presence of such a hydrothermal system at Chicxulub may depend upon whether or not it has a thick coherent melt sheet
(characteristics of its melt sheet are not well constrained). If a hydrothermal vent system and a thick melt sheet are absent
at Chicxulub, it will support the hypothesis that Chicxulub was an asteroid impact and Sudbury was a comet impact.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/01ed212d-bd04-4623-8f93-778c4f051a8d"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Hawaiian volcanic chain is commonly believed to have formed by magmas fed by a mantle plume that reaches to the Lower Mantle
or at least the Transition Zone. Therefore, there has been strong interest in the geochemistry of the Hawaiian lavas and
mantle xenoliths with the goal of understanding the nature of the deep mantle and the plume itself. Although a variety of
mantle xenoliths occur on Oahu, Hawaii, previous studies concluded that all of these xenoliths came from the shallow mantle -
no deeper than about 90 km (Sen,1987).A re-evaluation of the garnet bearing xenoliths from Hawaii indicates the presence of
a significant dissolved pyroxene in some of the garnets.The presence of exsolved pyroxenes in these garnets also suggest that
they are majoritic. Comparison of compositions of these garnets with those produced in high-pressure experiments suggests
that these majoritic garnets may have come from 9-10 GPa (~300 km).We suggest that these majoritic garnets were transported
to the surface in two stages: they (and probably some clinopyroxene)were first moved from ~300 km to the bsae of the
lithosphere by some medium (the plume?). here they resided for some time and mixed with other silicate minerals derived from
the lithosphere and asthenosphere. Finally, the mixed rocks (xenoliths) were brought up to the surface by the post-erosional
Hawaiian lavas.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/020aa7d2-c4a7-49af-a8a8-c50dba329edc"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Chinese Holocene vocanic rocks mainly distribute in Hainan, Wudalianchi, Longgang, Jingbohu, Changbaishan, and Tengchong. We
carry on U-Th disequilibrium studies of these young volcanic rocks in order to provide a better understanding of the mantle
processes in these areas. The basalts from Hainan, Wudalianchi, and Longgang have (230Th/238U)$andgt;$1.2, and the Jingbohu
basalts and Chnagbaishan vocanic rocks have (230Th/238U)$andgt;$1.10. Wide spread 230Th enrichments in these young volcanic rocks
exclude the possibility of fluid addition from subducted slabs to produce these volcanic rocks. In addition, significant
230Th enrichments indicate that melting starts in the presence of garnet and/or high-Na clinopyroxenes.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/020d45b2-1ed9-4635-981c-7e0fca4621fe"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We investigate the dynamics of the India-Eurasia collision zone using a thin viscous sheet approach on a sphere. We first
solve for the deviatoric stress field associated with gravitational potential energy (GPE) differences within the lithosphere
using a finite element type approach. GPE values are inferred using topography (assuming local isostatic compensation),
seismically defined crustal thickness estimates, and the EGM96 geoid model. We then solve for stress boundary conditions
associated with plate interaction (India-Eurasia, East Asia-Pacific, and SE Asia-Philippine) using a 9 parameter inversion.
Solutions are an estimate of the absolute magnitude of the vertically averaged deviatoric stress and the stress values are
relatively insensitive to the actual power law rheology of the lithosphere. We also estimate the vertically averaged
effective viscosity distribution by dividing the magnitude of stress by the magnitude of strain rate, inferred from
Quaternary fault slip rate estimates. Our estimates of the absolute magnitude of the vertically averaged viscosity
distribution provide a starting point for dynamic forward modeling of the internal velocity, strain rate, and associated
stress fields, assuming different power-law rheologies. Velocity boundary conditions for the dynamic forward model were
obtained from the joint inversion of Quaternary fault slip rates and 364 GPS observations for an internally consistent
kinematic model. We compare our dynamic forward model results with GPS measurements and strain rate fields from kinematic
studies, using different power-law exponents. For example, using a power law exponent of n = 3 the fit of the dynamic
velocity field to GPS observations (sum of squares misfit divided by number of degrees of freedom) is 2.1 for the case where
topography data are used to infer GPE values, 2.2 for the geoid case, and 2.4 for the seismically defined crustal thickness
case. The dynamic model produces pervasive E-SE extension within southern and central Tibet, NNW-SSE extension in eastern
Tibet, as well as NW-SE extension in Lake Baikal region. Regional values of vertically averaged effective viscosity are 2.3 x
10$^{22}$ Pa s in Tibet and 1.5 - 9.2 x 10$^{23}$ Pa s in the more rigid block-like regions of Tarim Basin, South China and
Amurian Block. Published strength profiles, combined with our results for the vertically averaged deviatoric stress, argue
for the majority of the strength of the continental lithosphere in Asia to reside within the seismogenic portion of the crust
where stress levels are expected to be 1 - 3 kbars.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02165160-ec01-4f36-b3e0-36f1527b1e3e"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Along the Egyptian coastline, the Bay of Aboukir, located 25 km east of the City of Alexandria promises to yield important
archaeological discoveries. The accounts of ancient writers about what was then called the Canopic region are numerous. There
are also papyrus sources and Greek inscriptions found on land, which give interesting details about this region.
Taking into account all the information given by those texts, and the positions of the various ancient remnants already
discovered and excavated alongside the coast since the 19th century as well as thanks to chance discoveries made around
1934-1940 by divers, it was obvious that a large land area, which carried cities and monuments, had disappeared under the
Mediterranean sea during the first millennium. Specifically we now know that the eastern suburb of the city of Canopus (east
of Alexandria) with the nearby city of Menouthis as well as the city of Herakleion was missing.
Canopus had an important temple dedicated to the god Serapis. In Menouthis where the celebrated temple of Isis and the later
church of Evangelists together with the sanctuaries of Saint John and Saint Cyr. Herakleion was a port at the Canopic mouth
of the Nile and the entrance to Egypt for all foreign vessels during the New Kingdom. Its temple to the god Herakles was
famous in the ancient world.
It was decided in 1996, in coordination with the Egyptian Supreme Council for Antiquities, that the European Institute for
Underwater Archaeology will perform an exhaustive archaeological survey of the East part of the bay of Aboukir, where these
missing cities probably were originally situated on land but are now under water.
The task was challenging, as the area concerned is about a hundred square miles. Taking into account our experience in the
Eastern Port of Alexandria where we have discovered the submerged Royal Quarters in 1996-1999, we recognized that the work
would be only successfully achieved if a team gathered from diverse disciplines could be formed. A geophysical survey was to
be performed first, which would give us data concerning the underwater topography of the area as well as relevant magnetic
data geological data. We also needed to understand what caused the land to become submerged as well as the changes in the
geographic positions of the Canopic branch of the Nile in ancient times.
The necessary team had to involve specialists in geophysics, archaeologists, historians and specialized divers. The survey
was performed with depth sounders, nuclear resonance magnetometers, side scan sonar and sub bottom profiler. The various maps
obtained were then interpreted using visual reconnaissance, spot under water archaeological excavations and taking into
consideration the historic information given by ancient texts and inscriptions.
The resulting effort revealed the location of the missing cities in the Bay of Aboukir and enables us to next undertake an
exhaustive archaeological excavation in the very near future. The results to date highlight two big questions: Why did the
cities, indeed the entire bay rapidly subside and what caused their structural collapse? The archaeological evidence together
with the geophysical data will be used in the near future to explain the complex pattern of repeated destruction of this
region during at least 18 centuries.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02313388-7a58-43d6-bd15-1e1525363db2"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Meaningful numerical modeling of geodynamic processes requires an accurate description of the rheological behavior of the
relevant rock materials. Constraints for such modeling may come from estimates of paleostresses in naturally deformed rocks,
applying experimentally calibrated paleopiezometers such as the relationship between dynamically recrystallized grain size
and flow stress. Most piezometric relationships used to date relate stress directly to the mean recrystallized grain size
without taking into account any effect of temperature. However, available microphysical models for dynamic recrystallization
show that dynamically recrystallized grain size may depend on temperature as well as stress. Furthermore, the role of strain
in the evolution towards a steady state microstructure has generally not been systematically explored. Hence, straightforward
extrapolation of experimentally derived piezometric relations to natural conditions which differ from those in the
laboratory may not be justified.
In this study, the influence of strain and temperature on the grain size distribution developing during dynamic
recrystallization is systematically investigated for experimentally deformed samples of Lorano Marble (Carrara, Italy). The
samples are uniaxially shortened to strains of 15-35\% in a gas-medium deformation apparatus, at stresses of 18-60 MPa,
strain rates of 10$^{-6}$-10$^{-3}$ s$^{-1}$, temperatures of 700-$1000\deg$C and a pressure of 300 MPa. Microstructures are
analyzed using quantitative image analysis techniques.
The results show that with increasing strain at the same stress, at least up to strains of 30\%, the median recrystallized
grain size of the deformed Lorano marble decreases and the characteristics of the grain size distribution are progressively
modified. In addition, the grain size distribution that develops is found to be dependent on temperature. From this, we
conclude that existing dynamically recrystallized grain size versus stress relations used in paleopiezometry are in need of
revision to account for the influence of strain and temperature. We present an updated relationship for calcite.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/023f59c8-5397-4de9-8289-b9fc020d2f27"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The results of structural analyses at high pressures on chondrodite up to 6.0 GPa (Kuribayashi et al., 1998) and phase A up
to 10 GPa (Kuribayashi et al., this meeting) showed that the mean Si-O distances remain almost constant with pressure. The
compression of the unit cell is governed by the compression of MgO6 octahedra. This situation is similar to the high pressure
behavior of forsterite (Kudoh and Takeuchi, 1985). In this study, we found that the structural modules of crystal structures
of phase A and chondrodite can be derived from the crystal structure of forsterite with substituting Si by 4H, rather than
the combination of forsterite and brucite. The brucite-like portion of the structure can be regard partly as the MgO6
octahedra and partly as Mg(OH)6 octahedroa. Density and bulk modulus of chondrodite and phase A can be estimated by the
weighted average of those of forsterite, Mg2SiO4, brucite, Mg(OH)2 and periclase,MgO. The estimated values (3.07 gcm-3, 115
GPa for chondrodite and 3.00 gcm-3, 103 GPa for phase A) are in good agreement with observed values (3.06 gcm-3, 118 GPa
(Sinogeikin and Bass, 1999), 129 GPa (Kuribayashi et al., 1998) for chondrodite and 2.96 gcm-3, 106 GPa for phase A
(Kuribayashi et al., this meeting)), supporting this view. It is interesting to note that the unit cell contents of the
phases along the forsterite-brucite join can be written to be the formula equivalent to forsterite.
Clinohumite:9xMg2Si0.89H0.44O4. Humite:14xMg2Si0.86H0.57O4. Chondrodite: 5xMg2Si0.8H0.8O4. Norbergite:6xMg2Si0.67H1.33O4.
Phase A: 7xMg2Si0.57H1.71O4. This implies that hydrous phases along the forsterite-brucite join can be the possible
candidates for hydrous forsterite.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/025b8cae-545e-4e9a-aae4-54d7258a4afe"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Laboratory column experiments have historically under predicted field-scale transport distances of bacteria.  Analysis of a
recent field injection indicates that a novel methodology utilizing colloid filtration theory may be successfully used for
scaling bacterial transport.  Results from laboratory column experiments were compared to a field scale injection that
utilized the same indigenous groundwater bacterium ({\it Comamonas} sp. strain DA001).  The laboratory experiments were
performed using 0.5 m intact sediment cores and real groundwater, both taken from the field site.  The field-scale injection
was conducted at the Narrow Channel Focus Area of the South Oyster bacterial transport field site near Oyster, Virginia.
Colloid filtration theory was utilized to delineate the collision efficiency distribution for DA001 from the breakthrough
curves of the field experiment and the sorbed bacterial concentrations from the laboratory experiments.  The field-scale
injection, which sampled greater 90% of the injected bacteria, indicated that collision efficiency was log-normally
distributed.  The column experiments sampled a smaller percentage of injected bacteria, because only 30 to 40% of the
bacteria sorbed to the sediment in the 0.5 m column.  Since the column experiment data represented the 30% to 40% of the
population with the greatest collision efficiencies, a log-normal distribution fit to this data was used to generate the
overall distribution of collision efficiency of the injected bacteria.  Utilizing the distribution of collision efficiencies
obtained from the cores in colloid filtration theory reproduced the concentration profile observed during the field
experiment.  It is believed this methodology could be successful in predicting concentration profiles in future field-scale
studies.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/025e7d14-12ca-47e3-9e9c-629d7b35f662"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We present dynamics of the dusk side Jovian magnetopause during the period of the Ulysses encounter. It is well known that
energetic ion anisotropy near the magnetopause is produced by the spatial gradient of ions depending on the distance from the
spacecraft to the magnetopause. Thus, anisotropies of energetic ions are useful to study the behavior of the magnetopause.
In this study, we analyzed the ion anisotropies measured by the COSPIN instrument onboard the Ulysses spacecraft. The
spacecraft observed magnetopause crossing 5 times during the outbound pass of Jupiter and provided a good opportunity to
study dynamics of the dusk side magnetopause.
During the magnetopause crossings, energetic particle instruments observed strong anisotropies of MeV protons. We showed that
the observed anisotropies were produced by the sharp flux gradient at ``trapping boundary&#39;&#39; just inside the magnetopause.
Distance between the trapping boundary and the magnetopause were comparable with gyro radius of energetic ions. It indicates
that energetic ions that hit the magnetopause were absorbed by the magnetopause and might escape toward the magnetosheath. We
constructed a model to reproduce the observed anisotropies. By comparing the model anisotropies with the observations, we
derived the magnetopause distance from Ulysses, and then the velocity of the magnetopause motion. From the analyses of the
five-magnetopause crossings, we found a dynamic magnetopause motion, where the magnetopause had an inward speed of about
150km/s at first, and then showed a rapid change to outward velocity with 10km/s within 2 hours. During this period, there
was no rapid or strong change of the solar wind condition, which was measured just outside the magnetopause. This suggests
that the observed rapid motion of the magnetopause was caused by the rapid change of plasma condition in the Jovian
magnetosphere.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/027afffe-2a87-4fd1-af1f-1f5d7321619a"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A relocation procedure that uses arrival times from first-arriving
regional and teleseismic P and S phases, the PKPdf branch, and depth
phases (pP, pwP, and sP) is applied to earthquakes reported by the
ISS, ISC and NEIC during the last century. The results of this
reprocessing are used together with a high-resolution,
tomographic P velocity model of the mantle to investigate the principal subduction zones of
the world viewed in a
curvilinear projection system based on the centers of curvatures of
the relevant volcanic arc or trench. Within the resolution of the
hypocenters ($andlt;$ 15 km) and the model (100-200 km) the resulting 805
one-degree wide cross sections, better resolve earlier results or
reveal several new findings: (1) Typically, Wadati-Benioff (W-B)
zones are sharply resolved with a thin, smoothly curvilinear
morphology for the main seismic zone. (2) A discontinuous lower
zone of earthquake activity, extending to depths of as much as 300
km and separated by 10 to 40 km from the upper zone, typically occurs
in old Pacific plate subducting in the western Pacific or in places
where slab curvature changes sign in flat slab localities. (3)
Reduction in W-B zone dip, outboard earthquakes and flat lying
velocity anomalies occur throughout the depth range of the
transition zone (not just near the bottom) suggesting that the
resistance to slab descent cannot be solely caused by possible
increased viscosity at the 660km discontinuity, but by buoyancy
effects due to differences in mineralogy and/or composition between
deep slabs and the surrounding mantle. (4) In regions that have
had a complex history of changes we observe plate reorganization
or reversals in subduction direction (e.g., in Melanesia and the
Philippines); detached slabs defined by inclined zones of seismicity
lacking shallow earthquakes are commonly observed. (5) Most of the
subduction zone segments that lack arc volcanoes also lack W-B zone
earthquakes deeper than about 100 km, consistent with recent models
of warm-slab subduction.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02910329-fbf1-46ee-b5e4-ec506b82f2f2"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Structural and geochronologic constraints in the central East Greenland Caledonides (72.5 ï¿½N) highlight the importance of
syncontractional tectonic denudation in the unroofing and exhumation of middle crustal rocks. Broad exposures from upper to
middle crustal levels reveal the evidence of progress in unroofing of a large-displacement, low-angle, ductile, extensional
shear zone. A second, complicated synorogenic detachment with large displacement lies approximately 15 km structurally below
the first. In addition to any erosion during orogenesis, the combined movements along these two faults were responsible for
tens of kilometers of synorogenic exhumation over tens of millions of years. The upper shear zone, called the Tindern
Detachment, juxtaposes amphibolite facies, migmatitic gneisses of the Krummedal Sequence (maximum P ~10 kbar, T ~ 750ï¿½ C) and
greenschist facies schists of the Lower Eleonore Bay Supergroup. Contact metamorphism of these schists to lower amphibolite
facies occurred during local intrusion of garnetiferous pegmatite/aplite dikes. A survey of this E-dipping ductile
detachment&#39;s footwall revealed a 2-3 km thick region marked by increasing upward migmatization and deformation capped by a
synextensional sillimanite-cordierite-bearing granite pluton injected along the detachment itself. Fabric asymmetries and the
relative age relationships among these fabrics and metamorphic porphyroblasts suggest that the Tindern detachment is a
rooted structure that has exhumed middle-crust (ca. 25 km depth) rocks. The presence of an essentially complete 14 km
stratigraphic succession above the fault indicates a maximum omission of ca. 11 km of section by displacement on the
detachment. Upper crustal blocks preserved in the hanging wall of the Tindern Detachment were thinned by way of many,
smaller-displacement (10s to 100s of m), antithetic west-dipping brittle faults, which were in turn overprinted by subsequent
north and south-dipping brittle faults. U-Pb monazite ages for the pre-extensional anatexites (ca. 425 Ma) and the
syn-extensional pluton (ca. 424 Ma), constrain the age of initiation for the Tindern Detachment.
Rapid cooling of the synextensional pluton through the closure temperatures for 40Ar diffusion in muscovite (~400ï¿½ C) and
biotite (~350 - 300ï¿½C) by 424-423 Ma. Multi-domain diffusion modeling of 40Ar in K-feldspar from the same granite indicates
later rapid cooling from 380 to 360 Ma possibly due to reactivation of the fault. The structurally lower shear zone, the
Hogedal Detachment, juxtaposes amphibolite/granulite facies footwall gneisses of the Gletscherland Complex and the hanging
wall amphibolite facies rocks from the footwall of the Tindern Detachment. Though we do not yet have absolute age constraints
on the duration of movement on this fault, multi-domain diffusion modeling of 40Ar in K-feldspar from the footwall of the
detachment indicates rapid cooling from 380 to 360 Ma, which may indicate coincident slip on both the Hogedal and Tindern
Detachments. Kinematic indicators along the Hogedal Detachment indicate top-to-the-east sense-of-shear. Although the Hogedal
Detachment places lower grade rocks on top of higher grade rocks, this relationship is deceptively simple. Existing
geochronologic data suggests that footwall metamorphism is Late Archean-Paleoproterozoic in age while hanging wall
metamorphism is Caledonian. This suggests that the Hogedal Detachment transects or reactivates a pre-existing Caledonian
thrust. Such complexity illustrates the importance of understanding both the timing of deformation and metamorphism for
interpretation of synorogenic extensional structures.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02a58f35-bcd2-499c-87e9-50fe27966f17"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Methane oxidation in anoxic environments is microbially-mediated and of global significance.  This process occurs in
geological environments where sulfate and methane coexist, and couples methane oxidation to sulfate reduction.  Evidence
indicates that the mechanism of anaerobic methane oxidation involves methane oxidizing archaea and sulfate-reducing bacteria,
acting in a close syntrophic association.  Hydrogen appears to be a key intermediate, but the specific mechanism is not
known.  Recent physiological, phylogenetic, biogeochemical, and molecular-isotopic studies provide further insight into the
possible mechanism of anaerobic methane oxidation.  These studies have led us to propose a novel mechanism for this
syntrophic process in which methane is oxidized to acetate and hydrogen by an archaea, with syntrophic consumption of
hydrogen and acetate by sulfate-reducing bacteria:
2CH$_{4}$ + 2H$_{2}$O $\rightarrow$ CH$_{3}$COOH + 4H$_{2}$           (archaea)
4H$_{2}$ + SO$_{4}$$^{2-}$ + H$^{+}$ $\rightarrow$ HS$^{-}$ + 4H$_{2}$O          (bacteria)
CH$_{3}$COOH + SO$_{4}$$^{2-}$ $\rightarrow$ 2HCO$_{3}$$^{-}$ + HS$^{-}$ + H$^{+}$ (bacteria)
The proposed mechanism is consistent with previous field and laboratory studies and is advantageous over previous hypotheses
as it allows for more energy for each organism involved, and better explains observations of isotopically-depleted bacterial
lipids.  Thermodynamic calculations indicate that 15-20 kJ is available to each organism for each round of catabolism at
conditions typical of high-methane marine sediment.  These thermodynamic yields are more feasible than those calculated
assuming other mechanisms.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02aa733a-9805-4e16-bbfb-97a934d4a6ac"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">One of the first scientific applications of AMS measurements was the determination  of Be-10 in polar ice (Nature,
275,731,1978). Such measurements, which were totally impractical before the development of AMS, can be used to obtain
information on the rate of Be-10 production in the past, and thus on the three parameters which influence this rate, namely
the primary cosmic ray intensity, the geomagnetic field intensity and the solar activity. This latter parameter can be used
to estimate variations in solar irradiance (Tellus B 52,985,2000), and is thus relevant to studies on the causes of climatic
variations in the past. Be-10 can also be used to help constrain chronologies of ice cores, either by permitting  estimates
of past ice accumulation rates, by using distinctive &quot; events &quot; such as the Be-10 peak 40,000 years ago as stratigraphic
markers, or by correlating the observed production pattern with that of C-14 in independently dated reservoirs such as tree
rings. For more than 20 years, we have been involved in measuring Be-10 in a number of Arctic and Antarctic ice cores. We
present here the current status of our measurements in the Vostok ice cores, which contain one of the most valuable
paleoenvironmental records presently available. Our Be-10 profile extends from the surface down to 3620 m, and includes more
than 2000 measurements.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02c55ddd-e85d-4a50-aca9-88068c733fbe"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The suggestion that thunderstorm electrical structures such as the electric field polarity or the vertical stacking of charge
regions can be inverted has been around awhile.  It has been part of some proposed explanations for positive ground flashes.
More recently the finding that inverted-polarity cloud flashes not only exist in deep convection, but may occur frequently
in certain regions or types of storms, has raised the issue of whether such lightning is produced by storms whose electrical
structure is inverted, either totally or in a part of the storm.  Looking for inverted structure must be done in the context
of typical, noninverted thunderstorm structures.  We start with the previously published electric field profiles commonly
found in deep convection, such as those presented in Stolzenburg et al. (JGR, 1998, pg14059).  These are compared with the
balloon-borne electric field soundings obtained this season in the tristate region around northwest Kansas during the Severe
Thunderstorm Electrification and Precipitation Study (STEPS).  In this report, we limit the discussion to convective regions
of storms.  Soundings exist for nonsevere storms, severe nontornadic thunderstorms, and supercell/tornadic thunderstorms.
Difficulties in deciding if there is an inversion of electrical structure include uncertainties in determining cloud top and
base.  A first look at the electric field profiles reveals a few soundings that seem to be of inverted polarity.  Because
previous studies have found that the electrical structure in updrafts differed systematically from those outside of updrafts,
our comparison of structures takes into account sounding location relative to updrafts.  The upper portions of the storm
structure will receive particular scrutiny owing to the apparent initiation of inverted-polarity flashes high in the storm.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02dccf17-7e18-43c2-a32a-2bb4b0b4efba"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The survval of blobs more viscous than the surrounding mantle is an important issue :
the subducted lithospheric material at depth might be more viscous than the primordial
mantle and this may affect its properties as a geochemical reservoir and also its gravi
tational segregation.
As emphasized in previous publications, the deformation of a blob
may depend on two parameters : the initial shape of the blob (i.e. its aspect ratio $A
_R$) and the viscosity contrast $\lambda$ between the blob and the mantle. In order to
quantify the effect of these two parameters, we perform three types of two-dimensional
numerical simulations : a pure shear simulation, a simulation of steady-state convectio
n and a simulation of time-dependent convection. In a pure shear flow, the rate of defo
rmation of a viscous blob is, in first approximation, proportional to the ratio $A_R/\l
ambda$. An extrapolation of these results may suggest that, once submitted to a large s
train, all blobs may have acquired a large aspect ratio, so become easily stretched and
mixed. For a viscous blob in a steady-state flow, a periodic deformation is superimpos
ed on the slow long term deformation. The strains of the periodic deformation obey the
laws found for pure shear. The long term deformation is very slow and seems influenced
by the fact that the flow is no longer perfectly steady. The mixing of more viscous blo
bs in this type of flow is rather inefficient. In a time-dependent flow, more represent
ative of the mantle flow, we find numerically that the distance $l$ between two tracers
situated within a viscous blob obeys the relationship : $\overline{ln(l)}=A.t/(1+\lamb
da)^2$, and that it is independent of the aspect ratio of the blob. These two results a
re well explained by a simple model of successive strains in arbitrary directions. The
viscosity contrast controls the mixing efficiency, and it has an effect much larger tha
n predicted by earlier studies. Blobs of matter more viscous than the average mantle ca
n therefore persist unmixed for a long period of time in the mantle, stirred by time-de
pendent convection.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02eabee7-4056-43a7-b91f-ca1ebf1a0164"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Large Eddy Simulation (LES) data of neutral atmospheric boundary layer (ABL) turbulence is analyzed to examine the range of
justifiable application of Taylor&#39;s hypothesis.  The paper addresses specifically the use of Taylor&#39;s hypothesis in field
experiments that aim to measure subgrid-scale (SGS) stresses and fluxes in order to address open issues in SGS modeling.
From the simulations the SGS shear stress is evaluated from spatial filtering in horizontal planes and, similar to the field
measurements, from a combination of temporal filtering in the streamwise direction and spatial filtering in the transverse
direction.  The analysis is repeated for a range of heights above the ground and filter sizes.  The simulations show that
time filtering provides a good approximation to streamwise spatial filtering even down to low measurement heights.  When
Taylor&#39;s hypothesis is used to interpret temporal signals of SGS stress as spatial signals, a height-dependent distance below
which Taylor&#39;s hypothesis holds, is identified.  With certain limitations, the results suggest that, for typical heights of
interest in LES and in field experiments, Taylor&#39;s hypothesis is applicable to measure SGS stresses in ABL field experiments.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02f940d6-c539-47a3-87d4-f2a20a4cc813"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">During the past 5 years CMIP, the Coupled (ocean - atmosphere - sea ice) Model
Intercomparison Project, has collected output from 20 models run in 8 countries.
The CMIP data base currently contains output from both unforced (``control run&#39;&#39;)
and perturbed (1\% per year increasing atmospheric carbon dioxide) simulations.
Here we present an overview of results from the most recent phase of CMIP.
The CMIP models are about equally divided between those employing and
those not employing ad hoc flux corrections at the ocean-atmosphere
interface.  The new generation of non-flux-corrected control runs are
nearly as stable and agree with observations nearly as well as the
flux-corrected models. This development represents significant progress
in the state of the art of climate modeling since the Second (1995) Scientific
Assessment Report of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change
(IPCC).  Comparison with observations of the total spatial and seasonal
variability of surface temperature, sea level pressure and precipitation shows
that the models simulate temperature best and precipitation worst, in accord
with conventional wisdom.  For precipitation and surface air temperature,
however, the r.m.s. difference between alternate ``observed&#39;&#39; data sets is
more than half the smallest r.m.s. model error.  This result implies
that in terms of variance and space-time pattern correlation, the models
nearly agree with observations to within the observational uncertainty.
In the increasing-CO$_{2}$ runs, differences between different CMIP models, while
substantial, are not as great as would be expected from earlier
assessments that relied on equilibrium climate sensitivity.
A major expansion (from Gigabytes to Terabytes) of the CMIP data base is
currently underway.  All interested parties are invited to contribute to and/or
make use of the data base.
ACKNOWLEDGMENT: This work was performed under auspices of the U.S.
Department of Energy by University of California Lawrence Livermore National
Laboratory under contract No. W-7405-Eng-48.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02fa385a-353c-4428-ba01-729ce4bb90e3"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Perennial springs located on west central Axel Heiberg Island in the Canadian High Arctic occur in a region with a mean
annual air temperature of -17ï¿½C and flow through continuous permafrost over 600 meters thick.  The outflowing spring water is
a low temperature (up to 6ï¿½C) brine  that maintains constant discharge temperatures and flow rates throughout the year.
Here we report on observations of the thermal regime associated with these springs and develop a combined flow and thermal
model of the subsurface flow using the measured geothermal gradient.  We also consider the implications these springs have
for the search for similar environments, past or present, on Mars.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/02fdfe21-df95-4b06-b77e-f94bd7f4a040"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Grand River basin is a large sub-watershed in the Great Lakes system draining an area of approximately 6,800 square
kilometres into Lake Erie.  The basin is home to over 715,000 people and is on the western fringe of the rapidly growing
Greater Toronto Area.  Unlike many areas within the Great Lakes basin, groundwater represents the major source of potable
water within the Grand River basin.
A multi-disciplinary study of the potential impact of climate change
on the groundwater resources of the Grand was carried out by Environment Canada in cooperation with the Grand River
Conservation Authority. The groundwater resources of the Grand River basin were characterized through the analysis of 85,000
water well construction records purchased from the Ontario Ministry of Environment. These records were edited and assembled
into a relational database management system (RDBMS) and GIS.
Water use was characterized from Environment Canada&#39;s Municipal Water Use Database (MUD), the Ontario Ministry of
Environment&#39;s Permits to Take Water (PTTW); and, census data on population and agriculture. Base flow indices, (BFI&#39;s), were
determined determined for gauged sub-catchments using turning point analysis of stream hydrographs.  A relationship was
developed between sub-basin Quaternary geology of gauged sub-catchments and BFI&#39;s derived from hydrograph analysis.  This
relationship was used to estimate BFI&#39;s for each watershed division.  A 2-dimensional finite differences model was developed
to determine groundwater response to changed water use and recharge conditions.
Observation well data were analyzed to determine historic groundwater level response to climatic and anthropogenic factors.
A current climatic and hydrologic conditions were developed from  1971-1995 meterologic and hydrometric data. Potential
climatic scenarios were developed from transient runs of the coupled atmosphere-ocean global climate model (AOGCM) called the
Canadian Global Coupled Model (CGCM1) developed at the Canadian Centre for Climate Modelling and Analysis (CCCma).
Precipitation and temperature values for the basin for each scenario were developed based on ratios of current to future time
period values.
The impact of the scenarios of climate conditions on groundwater recharge was derived using hysterisis plots of monthly
runoff efficiency versus temperature based on spatial analogues from  similar but warmer regions in the United States.
Comparative estimates of groundwater recharge under changed climatic conditions were developed using the Guelph All-Weather
Sequential Events Runoff (GAWSER) hydrologic model.
Preliminary modelling of the climate scenarios shows that significant reductions in water tables and stream base flows will
likely occur in the Grand River basin as a result of climate change.  Short-term impacts (year 2030) will likely be moderate,
but longer term impacts (year 2090) will be more significant.  If the predicted impacts of climate change on groundwater
resources occur, they will have significant implications on water management in the Grand River basin with reduced
groundwater supplies available for municipal and rural water use and impacts on wetlands and fisheries resulting from
diminished water tables and base flows.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/031ec0cc-d26f-4028-9ab3-c73ccf222065"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">It is now well known that suborbital-scale climate events recorded in Greenland ice cores also have manifestations in low
latitude marine records.  In the Arabian Sea, this linkage has appeared as productivity variations recorded by sediment
organic carbon content.  Plausible forcing is likely via the intensity of the southwest  monsoon and resulting upwelling of
nutrient-rich water.  We have examined high accumulation rate cores from the Oman Margin for nitrogen isotopic ratio which
records denitrification intensity within the oxygen minimum zone (OMZ) found at intermediate depths.  Denitrification
requires suboxic intermediate waters which can arise from intense local productivity.  With temporal resolution approaching
50 yr., nitrogen isotopic ratio was found to vary with a pattern of events very similar to the oxygen isotopic record from
the GRIP and GISP2 ice cores.  Given a general temporal setting known from  foram isotopes, a series of tie points were used
to correlate the Arabian nitrogen isotope and ice core records.  The resulting correspondence is striking and statistical
comparison shows a very high index of similarity.  Chronology for the first 20 kyr. is confirmed by radiocarbon dating.
Arabian Sea denitrification  is evidently very closely coupled to high latitude climate at the 100 year time scale and up and
is amongst the best comparisons between the ice core and marine records.  The high quality of the nitrogen isotopic record
holds much potential for refining our understanding of high and low latitude linkages.  The poorer comparison with
productivity indices may result from their extrinsic nature with influence  from sediment accumulation rate and possible loss
from diagenesis.
Alteration in denitrification intensity may produce a global feedback through changes in global ocean nitrate inventory
and productivity in non-HNLC regions.  Such feedback  would occur, though, on time scales similar to the residence time of
marine nitrate, about 3 kyr. at present.  Filtering the nitrogen isotopic record to examine variability on time scales of 3
kyr. and greater, produces a series of oscillations between 20  and 70 ka that are well correlated with a recent
high-resolution atmospheric CO2 record from Taylor Dome, Antarctica.  A possible mechanism relating high frequency N.
Hemisphere climate change to lower frequency global change is suggested.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0324108f-70ba-4546-9fa2-068af663dd06"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Since the late ancient period Alexandria played an important role in the Mediterranean culture.  As a result, there is an
abundance of documentation referring to the most important seismic events by which it was struck. In this talk the author
present the results of a research carried out for the Istituto Nazionale di Geofisica regarding the whole Mediterranean area
from the eighth century BC. till the fall of  Constantinople (1453).  The data referring to the period of the eleventh till
fifteenth centuries are still unpublished.
For Alexandria, the examined sources provide data for about one thousand years: from 320 till 1303 (the city was founded in
the fourth century B.C).  Within the time frame for which we have historical records, Alexandria belonged to different areas
of political and cultural power, always having an important role, an element which has allowed to preserve the memory of the
seismic events.  The sources thus belong to different cultures and languages: from the Greek-Latin era during the late
ancient period and later, till the Byzantine area until the first part of the seventh century. From 642, the year of the Arab
conquest, Alexandria was a great city of Islamic culture and economy.
The authors of the historical sources recording the seismic effects at Alexandria or of a nearby sites are in total 17,
reporting mostly of direct testimonies. They are thus precious data, examined in a critical way. The effects of the
earthquakes which memory has been preserved are in total eight: 320, 365, 551, 796, 951, 956, 1202 and 1303.
The most destructive effects are those generated by earthquakes located on the island of Crete. In particular, the earthquake
of 21 July 365 AD generated a strong tsunami at Alexandria which caused permanent effects on the coastal environment. Also
the earthquake and tsunami of 8 August 1303 caused destruction. Less damage was also caused by the earthquakes located in the
Syria-Lebanon area, of which however exists less information regarding the epicentral area.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0324617b-357d-438b-af20-ee67ca605b51"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Radon flux is monitored since 1992 in geogas at a 15 km sector along the western boundary fault of the Dead Sea Transform
(DST), in the northwestern sector of the Dead Sea area. A first study (1992-94) revealed a temporal association between Rn
Events (RnEv - radon flux signals 2-15 days long) and earthquakes (EQ) in the adjoining sector of the Rift, indicating a
relation between them (Steinitz et al., 1999, Israel J. Earth Sci. 48, 283-299). High-resolution ($andlt;$1 hour) electronic
measurements at one monitoring site (gravel; depth 1.5m) in the years 1995-1999 showed a similar result. The relation between
RnEv and EQ was investigated by testing for clustering of EQ, occurring in distinct sectors, around 215 RnEv. The clustering
of EQ within a window of and#61617;1 day around all RnEv is higher for all earthquakes (25.1% versus the expected value of
20.7\%) which occurred in the 200 km long DST sector adjacent to the monitoring site, particularly for EQ with ML $and#61619;$
2 (30.2\%) and for EQ with ML $and#61619;$ 3 (50\%). The associated confidence levels (c.l.) are andgt; 98.5\%. This again implies a
connection through a common cause between the RnEv and EQ in the sector of the Rift and#61617;100 km from the Rn sensor. The
increase of EQ (relative to the expected number in the investigated DST sector; ML $and#61619;$ 2) occurring within 2 and 4
days after the initiation of a RnEv is, respectively, 46\% and 38\% (c.l. 97\%-99\%). The results serve as a firm indication
as to the potential use of RnEv as a precursor of EQ in the DST system.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/032b893a-76cd-4728-82a4-2650626aec41"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Parameters associated with a hydrological model (or land surface model) are generally calibrated to achieve better model
performance through optimization methods with certain objective functions. For a complex system in the real world, such as
the land-atmosphere system, that is highly nonlinear and operates over multiple scales, the traditional objective functions
are usually unable to represent the characteristics of the observed quantities at multiple scales. Therefore, optimization
methods with the traditional objective functions would have a difficult time in obtaining the optimized parameters that have
a good property of predictability. A new approach for better calibration of model parameters is presented in this study. The
new approach employs the multiresolution analysis of wavelet theory to capture the characteristics of the observed quantities
that possess properties over multiple scales. The new approach together with the Shuffled Complex Evolution (SCE) method is
applied to the hydrologically based three-layer variable infiltration capacity (VIC-3L) land surface scheme for model
calibration studies. The results show considerable improvements both for the calibration and validation periods when compared
with the results when the SCE method is used alone with the traditional objective function.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/035b01fe-dca6-4e14-92e6-1a2a1778ffa6"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">During geomagnetic storms relativistic electron fluxes undergo large drop-outs and enhancements. Various theories have been
proposed to account for the observed changes in flux including the &quot;Dst effect&quot; (Dessler and Karplus, 1961; McIlwain, 1966;
Rinaldi et al., 1994; Kim and Chan, 1997; Li et al., 1999), ULF wave resonance (Elkington et al.,1999; Hudson et al., 1999),
VLF wave resonance ( Thorne and Kennel, 1971; Summers, 1998; Horne and Thorne, 1998), and simultaneous VLF and ULFwave
interaction (Rostoker et al., 1998, Liu et al., 1999)
The &quot;Dst effect&quot; states that relativistic electron fluxes observed at constant energy will mimic changes in the magnetic
field as measured by Dst as the relativistic electrons move to conserve all three invariants. This effect can cause order of
magnitude changes in flux which will obscure enhancements due to other acceleration processes. Using the POLAR HISTe data for
January to June 1997, we produce a data set of phase space densities at constant invariants which are unaffected by slow
changes in the magnetic field allowing us to identify enhancements due to other processes.
We used the 210 meridian magnetometer data in order to investigate the correlation of ULF wave power with relativistic
electron phase space densities. Many enhancements correlated with ULF wave power at the resonant frequency. However, several
clear increases in wave power showed no following enhancement in phase space density indicating that ULF wave power alone can
not account for the enhancement.
Here we explore the possibility of VLF wave interaction. We calculate pitch angle distributions using the POLAR HISTe
particle data along with the POLAR MFE magnetic field data for the same time period of January to June 1997. The pitch angle
distributions give an indication of how the particles interact with VLF waves. The orbit of POLAR allows us to describe
changes in the pitch angle distributions as a function of time and L shell.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0369d0ee-66c9-4737-9427-bed2549984d3"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">IMPACT (Integrated Massively Parrallel Atmospheric Chemical Transport) is a global model with prognostic troposphere and
stratosphere. It is being used to study the influence of natural and anthropogenic activities on the distribution of species
important to the sulfur cycle on global and regional scales, particularly for climate/chemistry interaction studies. IMPACT
runs with assimilated meteorological fields to drive the model as well as GCM fields. IMPACT is fully parallelized and
implemented on a wide range of computer platforms (both parallel and non-parallel) using two-dimensional domain decomposition
and message passing.
In climate/chemistry interaction studies, models frequently use diurnally or even monthly averaged concentrations of chemical
species to speed up computation time.  Using average concentrations may cause biases in the results due to the non-linearity
of certain reaction pathways. We will analyze the sulfur cycle and the non-linear effects due to the diurnal cycle by
comparing full diurnal chemical mechanism (~100 prognostic species) with compact averaged chemical mechanisms (~12 prognostic
species). We will present results including sulfur species concentrations and resulting aerosol distributions.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03787555-230e-4376-9d68-8ad66347e781"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The snowball earth hypothesis proposes that a series of global glaciations occurred 750-580 million years ago, each lasting
for millions of years and ending in scorching heat caused by extreme enrichments of atmospheric greenhouse gases. Evidence
exists in pre-glacial rocks for red, green and xanthophyte algae as well as ciliates and testate amoebae. In conjunction with
phylogenies, this requires that all major extant groups of protists (~70 phyla) diverged before glaciation began and
survived the deep-freeze and the aftermath. The existence of multicellular benthic algae implies that that refugia included
places where sunlight reached the sea floor. Candidates include volcanic islands like Iceland or Hawaii, and areas beneath
equatorial ice cover thinned by crystallization of new ice at the base (McKay, 2000) or by solar heating of entrained dust.
The survival of early metazoans - if they existed - is less problematic, as it is likely that such organisms could exist in
association with populations of eukaryotic algae. The abrupt deglaciations may have been more devastating to Neoproterozoic
biota than the prolonged glaciations themselves. Sea surface temperatures would have changed rapidly from near-freezing to as
much as 30 to 50 degrees in less than a few hundred years. Abrupt changes in ocean chemistry including oxygen levels would
occur with the removal of sea ice. In the high-CO2 aftermath of the glaciations, ocean pH would drop to between 6 and 7,
although the transition would have occurred gradually during the glaciation due to slow air-sea gas exchange. By itself,
biology does not allow us to differentiate between the snowball earth hypothesis and other alternative theories for climatic
change. However, the broad diversity of survivors favors less extreme variants with the range of possible scenarios predicted
by the snowball earth hypothesis. Although there is some turnover associated with each of the glaciations, a challenge is to
explain why major radiation follows only the last of several ice ages.  This radiation affects many groups of eukaryotes,
discouraging clade-specific explanations. We explore the possibility that the aftermath of a snowball earth led to a rise in
oxygen levels, stimulating the diversification of large metazoa as well as algae.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03a34029-52ce-4214-b3e4-61153ffc7e33"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Eruption of the Soufriere Hills Volcano on the island of Montserrat, Lesser Antilles volcanic arc, started in 1995 and has
continued intermittently to the present day. The geochemical evolution and the timescales of petrogenesis of andlt; 300 ka
deposits from the South Soufriere Hills (SSH) basalts and from the Soufriere Hills (SH) andesites and their mafic inclusions
(MafI) were studied. WR major and trace elements were determined by XRF and ICP-MS, and U-Th isotopes by TIMS.
Major and trace element variations indicate that the SSH basalts and the MafI have different source regions. The lower
Fe$_{2}$O$_{2}$$^{t}$/MgO ratios of the SSH basalts suggest that they may be generated at shallower depths than the MafI.
Furthermore, the SH magma cannot be produced by simple fractional crystallisation or AFC processes from either of these magma
types. ($^{238}$U/$^{232}$Th) and ($^{230}$Th/$^{232}$Th) activity ratios of the SH deposits and their MafI range from
$\sim$ 0.8 to $\sim$ 1.0 and are close to secular equilibrium, although some show significant Th excess. Mineral separates
are analysed to constrain the timescales of crystallisation and to test current models of magma remobilisation beneath the
Soufriere Hills volcano.
Nomarski imaging in combination with EMP profiles across plagioclase crystals reveal multiple resorption events. Trace
element variations across selected phenocrysts are studied by LA-ICPMS and IMP analysis in order to elucidate the geochemical
evolution of the magma through time, with the potential use of trace element diffusion modelling to determine crystal
residence times in the Soufriere Hills magma chamber.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03a733c4-2e12-4de6-be67-0d96d03b4743"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">To image the Nazca Ridge and its intersection with the Peru Trench, bathymetric and wide-angle seismic data were collected
off Peru at $15\deg$S during the RV &quot;Sonne&quot; cruise SO146-GEOPECO from March to May 2000. An $1\deg$ by $0.5\deg$ large area
at the southeastern flank of the ridge, where its leading edge enters the Peru Trench, was covered by swath bathymetry. The
survey revealed a rather smooth surface, volcanic structures and trenchparallel lineaments near the intersection with the
trench. A seamount of about 600-700 m height and a diameter of about 20 km is located in the center of the mapped area. To
the west, the survey revealed another seamount with a caldera-like top structure, conical peaks and several ridge-shaped
linear structures.
Two refraction seismic profiles were shot, one along the crest of the Nazca Ridge continuing on the continental slope and one
perpendicular to the axis of the ridge. The OBH/OBS data is of very good quality and refractions up to 140 km offset were
recorded. Modelling the profile across the ridge by ray-tracing indicates that it is covered by only several hundred meters
of sediment and shows a topographical high at the ridge axis. The velocity model consists of four layers which comprise
velocities in accordance with typical oceanic crust. Furthermore, the Nazca Ridge is characterized by an asymmetrical crustal
root: the depth of the Moho as indicated by clearly recorded events increases from 16 km in the north to 19 km at the root
of the Nazca Ridge, whereas the boundary between crust and mantle is located a depth of 15 km beneath the southern flank of
the ridge. The according thickness of the crust varies along the profile from north to south from 12.5 km over 16.5 km to
11.5 km.
Modelling of the profile perpendicular to the continental slope revealed that there is no accretionary wedge and that the
lower slope is characterized by an angle of $9.5\deg$. The Nazca plate which can be followed to a depth of 27 km subducts at
an angle of about $12\deg$. The resulting taper of about $21\deg$ indicates high basal friction and, as the steepness of the
wedge is  higher than on other profiles acquired during the GEOPECO cruise north of the Nazca Ridge, this can be interpreted
as a consequence of ridge subduction and erosion of a former wedge. Concerning the sediment cover of the oceanic plate, the
model shows thickening towards the trench. There seems to be no active accretion of the incoming sediments, so it is assumed
that they are completely subducted.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03ac10f7-d6e1-4984-af1d-21c9f26361ed"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Over the past 25 years, a large and diverse body of literature has addressed the sources, fate, and transport of DOC in
catchments around the globe.  These studies show that at the hillslope and small catchment scale, soil adsorption largely
controls DOC flux, while changes in flow path are typically responsible for most temporal variability.  At the landscape
scale, changes in land use or disturbance history can result in differences in DOC flux among catchments.  Within a region,
percent wetlands or soil C stocks can be good predictors of variability in DOC fluxes among catchments.  Finally, at the
global scale, soil C:N is an excellent predictor of DOC flux.  These seemingly incompatible results, based on studies
conducted at multiple spatial scales, present a dilemma for global change modeling.  Accurately predicting changes in DOC
resulting from changes in the global environment will require a more mechanistic understanding of the factors controlling
global patterns in DOC flux.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03ae0ceb-1d83-45f3-8b0e-4b850e4bf4f3"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Minimizing forecast error requires accurately estimating the initial state from which the forecast is made by optimally using
the available observing resources.  The Kalman filter accomplishes this but requires computation of the time dependent error
covariance matrix, which presents a daunting computational burden. However, the dynamically relevant dimension of the
forecast error system is far smaller than the full state dimension of the forecast model suggesting the use of reduced order
error models to obtain near optimal estimators.  A method is described for implementing a Kalman filter on a reduced
dimension representation of the forecast error system.  The reduced dimension system is obtained by balanced truncation of
the Hankel operator representation of the error system. A reduced order Kalman filter is implemented in a time-dependent
quasi-geostrophic storm track model to illustrate the theory.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03c692a8-7346-484f-9eb8-474e56f6a5a6"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A negative cloud-to-ground flash is made of various discharge components: a stepped leader, 3 or 4 return strokes, K and J
processes and dart leaders. The most luminous of these is the return-stroke that lasts about a millisecond and is separated
by tens of milliseconds from subsequent strokes. The general view is that the return-stroke is responsible for the formation
of NOx by lightning.  However, an unexplored question is whether the products from the first return-stroke are able to
diffuse out from the lightning channel prior to the formation of a subsequent return-stroke.  To address this problem we have
experimentally designed a double return-stroke flash by laser induced plasma (LIP) breakdown in air at atmospheric pressure.
The aim if this work is to study the temperature and electron density evolution of LIP in a single and double
return-strokes.
LIP was obtained by focusing with a 5 cm focal length lens of two time synchronized beams from Q-switched Nd:YAG laser
sources operated at 10 Hz. The first and second plasmas were formed by 300 and 150 mJ pulses, respectively.  The plasmas were
situated in one arm of a Mach-Zehnder interferometer which was illuminated with an expanded beam from two He-Ne lasers
emitting at $\lambda $=632.8 nm and
543.5 nm. These probe beams were sent one at a time to a ICCD 1024 $\times $
256 camera and the signal was stored in a PC. The incident and transmitted
energy were measured with two power energy meter.
The transmitted energy after the plasma shows that the discharge scatters most of the second pulse energy when the delay
between the lasers is less than 20 $\mu $s. Time resolved shadowgrams reveals two or more shock waves produced by the
reflection of the first one
with the interface plasma-surrounding air. The temperature evolution was derived by interferometry from the microsecond to
the millisecond time scale.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03ca2267-b2e1-4797-a81b-d9341f2611c3"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Laramie River in southeastern Wyoming is a small, low gradient, meandering river.  During winter when discharge is low
($\sim$1 m$^{3}$ s$^{-1}$) the river consists of alternating pools and riffles.  We report on observations of anchor-ice
formation and ice rafting along a 500-m reach of the river during the 1999/2000 winter.  There were 36 observed diurnal
anchor-ice formation and release events between 11/20/99 and 3/14/00.  These events were restricted to freezeup and thaw
periods when portions of the river surface were ice free.  The amount of anchor ice formed during events was highly variable.
Anchor ice formed along the entire study reach during some events on sand, pebble, cobble and bedrock outcrop substrates.
It formed most often in pebble- and cobble-bedded riffles.  Mean current velocities in areas of anchor-ice deposition ranged
from 18 to 60 cm s$^{-1}$.  Clay- through cobble-sized material was incorporated into anchor ice when the ice formed, but
coarse material predominated.  When released from the bed on mornings following formation events, anchor ice preferentially
rafted coarse-grained material downstream.  The buoyancy of the ice allowed transport of pebbles and cobbles through river
reaches with low flow velocities where normal hydraulic transport did not occur.  These coarse-grained materials were
transported near the top of the water column and were deposited when released anchor-ice masses melted or broke up in
turbulent eddies.  Eddies that broke up floating anchor-ice masses were located at stream riffles.  It appears that ice
rafting is an effective process for moving coarse material from riffle to riffle.  During freezeup and thaw periods, when
river discharge and bed shear stresses are relatively low, anchor ice rafting is an important, but subtle, process that moves
significant amounts of sediment downstream.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03ccfb50-1a8d-4462-b59d-06e840fbca85"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Invasions and range expansions of non-native plant species are among the gravest threats to natural ecosystems and the
maintenance of ecosystem.  Given the interplay among physical, human, and biological aspects, the problem must be studied
using an integrated approach.  The key to understanding this issue is a system simple enough to disentangle and one complex
enough to reveal the intertwined biological, physical, chemical, and social phenomena.  The massive invasions of Spartina
alterniflora, an Atlantic cordgrass, into Pacific estuaries provide an ideal system for delving into the complex
ramifications of alien species in ecosystems.  We will investigate these linkages through the development of a predictive
model of the dynamics and selected impacts of S. alterniflora invasions. The core model of S. alterniflora spread will
include key feedbacks with the environment.  We will use an overall modeling and conceptual approach describing our system
that includes the spatial extent of the invasion, and the biological and physical aspects of the environment.  We are terming
the modeling approach an &quot;l-state, r-state approach,&quot; where the &quot;l&quot; stands for the &quot;local&quot; state of the system at a
particular location, and the &quot;r&quot; stands for the overall &quot;regional&quot; description of the system.  This discrete-time,
continuous-state framework has substantial mathematical and computational advantages over reaction diffusion models, and
matches the yearly reproductive cycle of Spartina.  In our general model, we will have a density (on location and system
state) function describing the local state of the system.  For particular aspects (physical, biological, human) of our
modeling approach, we will use different formulations based on this overall concept, but a typical example will include
position along a linear along shore transect, tidal height, age of Spartina, and the densities of two other species in the
system.  The local dynamical part will include the effect of Spartina on the physical environment, and the local changes in
density of all species, and changes in size and age of Spartina.  To this age dependent part, we would add a dispersal
kernel.
The full generality of this modeling approach may not at first be apparent.  The nonlinearities, such as density dependence
and species interactions, can enter into the kernel.  Stochasticity is explicitly included because the kernel can include the
possibility of a single state in year t being `transferred&#39; to a distribution of states in year t + 1.  Year to year
variation in the physical environment, such as ENSO events, is included in the dependence on t.  Dispersal is included
because the population at one location x* affects the population at another location x.  Feedback with the most important
physical aspect of the environment, tidal height, is included through the inclusion of the z variable in the kernel.
Descriptions of flow, and modification of flow, which will affect survival and dispersal, are included.  Thus our modeling
efforts will be focused on developing mathematical descriptions of the interactions, such as density dependence or physical
feedback, that will be used to produce the kernel.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03ce81c8-df93-4b8c-9809-14d8a9a2a34d"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">By studying the detailed properties of coronal loops, we can learn
a great deal about both the structure of the magnetic field and the
spatial distribution of coronal heating.  This in turn provides
important clues about the physical origin of the heating and its
likely relationship to coronal electric currents.  Existing
single-view observations from missions like {\it Yohkoh} and {\it TRACE}
suggest that loops have a cross section that is circular, fully
filled on resolvable scales, and uniform along the loop.  {\it STEREO}
observations are needed to confirm these results and to provide
important additional details.
We here describe our attempt to explain some of the observed
properties of loops with a twisted coronal flux tube model.  The
model is a numerical solution of the fully three-dimensional
force-free magnetic field equation.  To resolve the internal
structure of an individual flux tube embedded within a much larger
dipole configuration, we use a nonuniform grid of size $609 \!
\times \! 513 \! \times \! 593$, the largest ever applied to a
solar problem, to our knowledge.  We discuss our results with
particular emphasis on some ambiguities of interpretation that
should ultimately be resolved by {\it STEREO}.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03cf5c18-218e-481e-9fd3-c28886cb4462"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Deep-sea core PS2644 from the western Iceland Sea was dated by $andgt;$ 150 AMS $^{14}$C ages with a focus on marine isotope stage
3. The high-resolution climate records of this core reveal millennial-scale oscillations in conjunction with the
Dansgaard-Oeschger cycles in the Greenland ice cores. This enabled us to obtain 80 marine $^{14}$C calibration points for the
interval 11.4-53.3 calendar kyr BP by correlating the marine climate records and $^{14}$C ages with the annual-layer
chronology of the GISP2 ice core. Between 27 and 54 cal. kyr BP the continuous record of $^{14}$C / cal. age differences
reveals three intervals of highly increased $^{14}$C concentrations coincident with low values in paleomagnetic field
intensity, two of which are attributed to the geomagnetic Mono Lake (33.5-34.5 cal. kyr BP, up to 550$\permil$ marine
$\Delta$$^{14}$C) and Laschamp excursions (40.3-41.7 cal. kyr BP, up to  1215$\permil$ marine $\Delta$$^{14}$C). A third
maximum (marine $\Delta$$^{14}$C: 755$\permil$) is observed around 38 cal. kyr BP and attributed to the geomagnetic intensity
minimum following the Laschamp excursion. During all three events the $\Delta$$^{14}$C values increase rapidly with maximum
values occurring at the end of the respective geomagnetic intensity minimum. During the Mono Lake event, however, the
$\Delta$$^{14}$C values of core PS2644 seem to underestimate the atmospheric level, if compared to the $^{36}$Cl flux
measured in the GRIP ice core (Wagner et al. 2000) and other records. As this excursion coincides with a meltwater event in
core PS2644, the underestimation is probably caused by an increased planktonic reservoir age. Some first results of $^{14}$C
dating between 53 and 59 cal. kyr (Heinrich event 6) will also be discussed.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03d1c9ac-ceb3-4962-bcba-c8ac3b5be790"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Physical changes in the coastal environment are both caused by, and are an indicator of, changes in the underlying
ecosystems. For this reason, coastal scientists have long been interested in the application of remote sensing
technologies for change detection studies. Most previous efforts have utilized coarse resolution spaceborne data
and multispectral data processing techniques. However, the recent deployment of imaging spectrometers in low altitude
aircraft has made it possible to conduct land cover mapping at resolutions as fine as 2 meters. Consequently, coastal change
mapping can now be accomplished using the vector space methods that are commonly employed when processing hyperspectral data.
Researchers at the University of Florida and at the NOAA National Geodetic Survey have conducted studies to quantify the
performance of imaging spectrometers for this purpose. Important considerations are: the geometric fidelity of the images;
image resolution;and accuracy of
vehicle navigation. We have also investigated the potential of spectral mixture analysis (SMA) algorithms applied to the
coastal change problem. In our work, we have used repeat track data from the 1998 and 1999 low altitude deployments of the
AVIRIS sensor, to monitor change in coastal land cover at a resolution of 4 meters.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/03e96b88-0407-452a-8fd7-f34e0b324966"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We develop a numerical simulation model based on the discrete element method (DEM) in order to clarify the geophysical
phenomena which accompany with the fracture of rocks and three-dimensional simulation of uniaxial compression of rock is
carried out using our code.  Model rock is assumed to consist of spherical elements with a same radius.  Each element obeys
equations of motion of both translation and rotation.  Elements interact with viscoelastic forces, if they connect or contact
with each other.  In this study, we introduce cohesion and restricted rotation, that is, elements in connection acts as if
they were a single continuum.  Results of simulation of uniaxial compression of rock show that the surfaces of the fracture
are oblique and that the fracturing begins at the corners of the upper and bottom compressional surfaces and propagates into
inside the model rock.  The fracture zone is cone-shaped, which is similar to those observed in laboratory experiments.
Stress-strain curves also show agreement with those observed by laboratorical uniaxial compression tests using natural rocks.
After rupture, the model rock is divided into various size of fragments.  The distribution of fragments size of model rock
statistically show that fracturing does not depend upon the size of the cluster of the elements.  When the stress drop takes
place, the number of connection of elements rapidly decreases.  As mentioned in the above, our model seems to be successful
in simulating rock fracture.  To clarify the effects of the rotation of elements, which is a feature of our model, we compare
our model with the models that does not allow the rotation of elements and that allows to rotate even in connection.  They
show unrealistic results for uniaxial compression test on ether shape of model rock or stress-strain curves.  This suggests
that the rotation of elements plays an important role for the realizing the fracturing process by numerical simulation.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/040ff889-a4da-41d3-b537-cd8b936d960a"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">During that portion of the heliomagnetic cycle when the sun&#39;s magnetic field in the northern hemisphere is outwardly directed
(qA$andgt;$0), (1990-2000), positively charged cosmic rays flow into the heliosphere over the solar poles and out along the
heliospheric neutral current sheet (HNCS).  Near solar maximum there is a polarity reversal of the field which reverses this
drift imposed flow pattern.  These effects are studied over a complete heliomagnetic cycle (1980-2000) using the data from
cosmic ray experiments on the Voyagers, Pioneer 10, Ulysses, Imp 8, Wind and SAMPEX spacecraft at 1 AU and in the distant
heliosphere out to 77 AU.  It is found that drift effects are clearly present over the recovery, solar minimum and onset of
the new cycle (22) for positively charged cosmic rays over the 1980-1990 qA$andlt;$0 epoch.  However for the 1990-2000 qAandgt;0 epoch,
the latitudinal gradients are positive but small even at solar minimum and the inclination of the HNCS appears to have a
negligible effect.  Moreover the recovery period of cycle 22 (1991-1997) suggest there may be significant modulation in the
region of the heliosheath.  In the distant heliosphere the intensity levels are very different over the cycle 21 and 22 solar
minima.  At 1 AU the onset of modulation for cycle 23 and the modulation levels through 2000.5 are remarkably similar to
those of cycle 20 some 20 years earlier.  However these disturbances are just reaching the Voyagers and it is not clear at
this time what their effects will be in the distant heliosphere.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/042a60c9-e763-4a3d-b920-52f102b3b0b6"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">It has long been posited that at statistical turbulent equilibrium vertical heat (or buoyancy) flux is proportional to
temperature (or buoyancy) variance dissipation.  This relationship has been criticized as being unrealizable because in any
practical attempt to approximate ensemble averages (by time means, for example) of second-order fields (variances, heat
fluxes, variance dissipations, etc.) the contributions from cross-interactions between scales above and below the cutoff
scale of the average are not negligible. We examine this difficulty by first considering the practical averaging, rather than
ensemble averaging, of the evolution equations for buoyancy and buoyancy squared over small scales (of order meters, or
order 100 sec).  We find that this generates equations perfectly analogous to the ensemble-averaged equations, with
practically averaged variances and fluxes to which cross-scale interaction terms do indeed contribute.  The solution to the
difficulty posed above is not to partition arbitrarily the contributions of these interactions, but to retain them.  Only for
the estimator of variance dissipation is it justified to neglect cross-interactions, because the spectra of scalar gradients
are peaked at high wavenumbers and roll off at low wavenumbers.  By further averaging the equations over larger scales,
though on isopycnal surfaces rather than at fixed vertical levels, estimates of fluxes, variances, and dissipations can be
made with greater statistical confidence. The contributions to buoyancy variance are still cut off at the small averaging
scale; longer scales do not contribute to this variance, so that statistical equilibrium can be approached.  In this case,
the buoyancy variance dissipation furnishes the diapycnal buoyancy flux, whose divergence in turn gives the material flow
across isopycnals.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04324357-8a29-4c2b-b4ca-c6c9a08520ee"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A cross-shelf transect of six Datawell Directional Waverider buoys and a coherent array of bottom pressure sensors were
deployed during September-December 2000 on the North Carolina continental shelf as part of the SHOaling Waves EXperiment
(SHOWEX).  The buoys (1 m diameter) are surface following buoys that measure horizontal and vertical orbital displacements at
the sea surface from which wave height spectra and directional wave properties can be determined. The array of pressure
sensors, deployed on the inner shelf in 24 m depth, consisted of five autonomous instruments that were arranged in a linear
array configuration with 360 m aperture.  This array provides high-resolution directional spectra of swell on the inner
shelf.  In addition to wave measurements, sediment samples were collected using a Shipek sampler, and the small-scale sea bed
morphology was surveyed during three cruises with an EdgeTech DF-1000 Digital Side Scan Sonar system.
The data set spans a wide range of conditions including Hurricanes Floyd, Gert, Irene, and Jose. A preliminary analysis of
the observations is presented here.  Frequency spectra, frequency-directional spectra, mean propagation directions, and
directional spreads were estimated from
the buoy and array measurements to examine the variability of wind seas and swell on the continental shelf.  In energetic
swell conditions a strong decay of wave energy levels across the shelf (about a factor 4 between the shelf break and the
inner shelf) is observed, consistent with earlier studies.  Widespread sand ripples observed in the side scan sonar images of
the sea floor support the hypothesis that the damping is caused by enhanced bottom friction over rough bed forms.  A strong
decrease in wave energy levels near the coast was also observed in active wind sea conditions associated with the passage of
hurricanes and nor&#39;easters. Estimates of directional spectra show the expected refraction of waves to shore-normal directions
on the inner shelf.  Variations in directional spread across the shelf are generally small, with the exception of
directionally narrow swells generated by Hurricane Gert.  The sharp increase in directional spread observed on the inner
shelf is possibly caused by wave scattering from bottom irregularities.
This research is supported by the Office of Naval Research.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04468eba-d0cf-4150-a1bb-56fdcdc18177"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In the island-arc/ocean transition zone of the northeast Japan, the crustal thickness and the upper mantle velocity are
considered to change abruptly, although the detailed seismic crustal structure of this area is poorly known, and we need to
conduct a large scale artificial seismic exploration that covers the island-arc/ocean transition zone. In Aug. 1999, an
offshore seismic exploration using an airgun-array was made by JAMSTEC in the central part of the NE Japan arc. The line of
the experiment has length of about 270 km, from about 16 km off the coastline to beyond the trench axis. During the
experiment, we deployed 17 seismic stations with about 5 km spacing along the landward extension of the marine seismic
profile. On the records of these temporal land stations, there are several prominent later arrivals, interpreted as
reflections from the deeper part beneath the coastal regions. Our preliminary travel time analysis shows that the arrival
times of two branches of the later arrivals are explained as the reflections from the Moho and as those from a reflector
within the upper mantle, respectively. Furthermore, we also observed another later arrivals whose travel times are explained
as the reflections from the plate boundary. The depth to the Moho of the arc crust is about 20 km in the offshore area, while
those beneath the coastal line was estimated to be 27 km by the previous study, indicating that the Moho shows steep ascent
toward the trench. The depth to the deeper reflector is estimated to be about 40 km, which is significantly shallower than
the depth to the plate boundary. Wide-angle data obtained by an explosion seismic survey previously made in the land area
shows existence of a reflector at a depth of 40 km within upper mantle of the NE Japan arc. Our reflector may correspond to
this reflector, if it extends beyond the coastline. The airgun shots were also recorded by many land seismic stations. We
expect that these data enable us to determine detailed three-dimensional seismic structure of the island-arc/ocean transition
zone, since the seismic network covers almost entire part of the NE Japan arc.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04485bdc-3ec3-48b3-8545-8da8d68a1139"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Field, microstructural, and geochemical studies of exhumed traces of the San Andreas fault (SAF) in southern California
indicate that slip along the fault is accommodated over zones only 10&#39;s cm thick embedded in a damaged zone of fractured and
faulted rocks up to 200 wide. In order to infer the likely structure and processes at greater depths, we compare our work on
the SAF-system with our studies of exhumed brittle strike slip faults in the Sierra Nevada.  Here, faults that range from
10&#39;s m length to 13 km long exhumed from 4-15 km can be examined.  The longest faults have up to 140 m of net slip.
Analysis of microstructures of faults with 10 cm to 100+ m of show that faults with as little as 10 cm of slip consist of
zones of chlorite-quartz-epidote cataclasite 2-3 mm wide.  Brittle and reaction-induced grain-size reduction of 10 to 100x is
common, and foliation is well developed, defined by chlorite and sericite, stringers of Fe-oxides, and brittly deformed
quartz and feldspar grains.  Biotite and chlorite deformed by intracrystalline slip and cataclasis. The microstructures in
the small faults are remarkably similar to those previously documented in the Punchbowl and San Gabriel faults with up to 40
km of slip.  The primary difference is that the fault cores of the latter faults are 10-20 cm thick; however at the
thin-section scale many of the microstructures and deformation mechanisms are the same. The striking similarity in
microstructures across five orders of magnitude of slip suggests that deformation mechanisms responsible for slip
localization develop very early in the fault history, and may continue to operate along extremely narrow surfaces over the
history of the fault.  Models for fault growth thus should emphasize the mechanics of fault linkage, as the dominant
processes of slip along an individual fault may be set in at the very early stages of fault development.  These narrow slip
horizons are likely the loci of earthquake nucleation and propagation, and thus the source zones and the slip propagation
regions lie in narrow zones that have significant microstructural and thus, rheological, contrasts with the damaged zones.
These observations support models that call on large material property contrasts to maintain the slip zones along seismogenic
faults.  The larger fault structures indicate that this slip localization generates large contrast slip zones that may
maintain the same slip vector over much of the slipped patch, with decrepitation of slip at the fault terminus.  Corrugated
fault surfaces and/or streaks of fault composition may reveal the cause of streaking of earthquakes along the SAF, as
numerous long asperities are displayed on the larger faults.  Our data extend to depth our understanding of the structure of
strike-slip faults and its resulting geophysical signature.  The narrow slip surfaces are likely the regions of narrow bands
of earthquakes, and are created by focused alteration that accompanied slip.  In places where the SAF roots into crystalline
rocks, we anticipate that the fault zone is very narrow, and not a broad region of deformation and alteration.   Damaged
zones do occur at the tips and linkage regions of fault segments, which are likely zones of slip termination.  Such zones
should create a geophysical signature 100-200 m on a side, and may be regions of significant fluid movement.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0453a198-46bb-4787-a3b9-558d55a567db"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The western part of Okinawa Trough is characterised by an active rifting of the eastern margin of the Asian continent and is
one of the key areas to describe the tectonics and dynamics of the evolution of backarc basins. The area was surveyed by R/V
YOKOSUKA and DSV SHINKAI6500 in July-August 2000 (&quot;Lequios&quot; Cruise: Cruise ID = YK00-06 Leg2) after the succession of the
previous l&#39;Atalante cruise in 1996 in the SPOT Area (= east of Taiwan; Sibuet et al., 1998), in order to detect (i) the
formation and evolution of the central rift axes, (ii) the volcanic activity in the trough area, (iii) hydrothermal activity
in the trough area, and (iv) regional tectonics and dynamics of the study area. Two active hydrothermal sites were newly
located through 10 dives in the study area. One is located in the SPOT area and the other on the axial zone of the Okinawa
Trough off northern coast of Miyako Island. Maximum temperature was 170 degrees centigrade in the former case and 150 degrees
in the latter case. Both sites are characterised by active chimneys associated by sulphide deposits and chemosynthetic
communities. The central graben of the trough was found to be no more active and the the E-W-trending depressions and knolls
derived from faults are covered with thick sediment. Regional topographic survey by the SEABEAM2112 multibeam bathymetric
survey system shows the clear segmentation of the central graben. The eastern hydrothermal site is located on the junction of
the two different segment edge. On the other hand, there were no signs of the active magmatism nor hydrothermalism in the
segment centres. The central knoll on the Yaeyama Graben seems to be rather old and the seafloor surrounding the knoll
deforms like elastic bending due to the load of the knoll. It shows that no more active rifting is taking place at the axis
of the segment of the graben. Offset and segmentation structure of the axes of the graben are rather predominant in the study
area. For example, there locates one more graben between Yonaguni Graben and Ishigaki Graben forming en echelon graben
structure. One potential graben is also located in the south of Miyako Seamount the north of which Miyako Graben is located.
Largely meandering channels were located off the northern coast of Yonaguni Island and Ishigaki Island. These are similar to
those observed in the SPOT area. All these are apparently derived from onland fluvial erosion, which suggests that this area
was deformed down to 1000m corresponding to the rifting of the Okinawa Trough.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/046dcfaf-a3ca-49ce-8dc7-36f1e7b24ea4"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Ionospheric ions of about approximately 100 eV, injected into the magnetosphere at the dayside cusp, are studied using the
TIDE (Thermal Ion Dynamic Experiment) data on Polar spacecraft. The low altitude cusp region (1.8 to 3.0 +/- 0.3 Re,
geocentric) is targeted by a large number of Polar perigee passes between 70 to 80 MLAT (degree) and between 0900 to 1500 MLT
(hour). The cusp region is among the most active source regions participating in injecting ions into the magnetosphere and
has a much larger area at high solar wind pressure (p  andgt; 4 nPa) than at low pressure (p  andlt; 2 nPa). In this study, we will
show that the solar wind kinetic pressure dramatically affects not only the outflowing ion flux but also the area in latitude
and local time over which the increased flux occurs, in agreement with an expanded cusp (degree-hours). The outflowing ion
rate in the cusp region is the product of two parameters: the ion flux and the size of the area of the cusp. We study the
dependence of both these factors on the solar wind pressure, to determine the total supply of ions to the magnetosphere from
this region.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0481722c-e27e-474e-bc58-4d4b31f4fc36"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DIAS, Dublin, the University of Hawaii, Honolulu, and the GFZ, Potsdam, operate since summer 1999 twelve seismic stations on
the Hawaiian Islands. Eleven stations are equipped with Guralp 40T seismometers and one with an STS-2. In addition, data from
two IRIS stations and three stations of the USGS are available. The stations are distributed over
the largest islands with concentration on the Big Island.
The aim of this project is an attempt to map in greater detail than in an earlier study (Li et al. 2000) effects of the plume
conduit on the upper mantle discontinuities and the dimensions of an asthenospheric zone of strongly reduced velocity, also
observed by Li et al.(2000). The technique used is the receiver function method. First results indicate that the
asthenospheric low velocity zone is extended further south underneath the Big Island than observed by Li et al.(2000).
Li, X., R. Kind, K. Priestley, S. V. Sobolev, F. Tilmann, X. Yuan, and M. Weber, 2000. Mapping the Hawaii plume conduit with
receiver functions.
Nature 405, 938-941.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/048518b5-7871-42a5-9198-61a3f011573b"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Anthropogenic and natural production of methyl bromide (CH$_{3}$Br) are a potential source of stratospheric ozone depleting
bromine (Br). It is necessary to determine the magnitude of the sources and sinks of tropospheric CH$_{3}$Br to ascertain the
strength of this threat. Recent research suggests a significant discrepancy between the quantity of sources and sinks in the
budget with sinks outweighing sources by 59 Gg. Current publications have pointed to flooded soils (rice agriculture, salt
marshes, and freshwater wetlands) as significant sources of atmospheric CH$_{3}$Br. We have focused our most recent research
on the exchange of CH$_{3}$Br at two freshwater wetlands (Sallie&#39;s fen and Angie&#39;s Bog) and one brackish salt marsh (Chappy&#39;s
Landing) in the Northeastern United States.
CH$_{3}$Br exchange measurements occurred weekly at all three sites from April through November of 2000. Flux measurements
were made by placing a clear, Lexan chamber (0.198 or 0.397 m$^{3}$) on an aluminum collar (0.397 m$^{3}$) imbedded in the
soil. Samples were collected in stainless steel canisters from the sealed chamber headspace at timed intervals. The sample
cans were returned to the laboratory and analyzed for CH$_{3}$Br, methane (CH$_{4}$), carbon dioxide (CO$_{2}$) and the inert
tracer sulphur hexafluoride (SF$_{6}$) using ECD, FID and TCD gas chromatography. Temperature of the air, soil surface and
-10 cm as well as pH were monitored during measurements. Automated meteorological stations supplied hourly averaged
waterlevel and temperature profiles at the freshwater wetland sites for the entire season.
Fluxes of CH$_{3}$Br from the three sites ranged from -4.03 to +2.55, -1.22 to +4.90 and -1.58 to +9.22 $\mu$g m$^{-2}$
d$^{-1}$ (Sallie&#39;s Fen, Angie&#39;s Bog, and Chappy&#39;s Landing respectively) for the 2000 sampling season. Our measurements made
at the freshwater wetlands in 1999 fall within these ranges even though the two years were significantly different
climatologically with 1999 being a large drought year and 2000 being a very wet and cool compared to the 30 year average.
Our measurements at Chappy&#39;s Landing are consistent with previous research that salt marshes are a significant source of
CH$_{3}$Br to the atmosphere. The emission of CH$_{3}$Br appears to be related to temperature as well as tidal height
therefore any extrapolation to a global source estimate must include these parameters.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/049fe8e6-d344-48ca-a1a3-eb4ede1f018c"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Two principal heliospheric phenomena have been studied using the Voyager plasma wave instruments:  (1) heliospheric 2 to 3
kHz radio emissions, and (2) dust impacts.  Two intense heliospheric radio emission events have been observed, the first in
1983-84 and the second in 1992-93.  Strong evidence now exists that these radio emissions are generated in the outer
heliosphere.  Both events occurred about 400 days after a system of strong shocks, magnetic field and plasma disturbances
were detected propagating outward through the heliosphere.  These disturbances produced the two strongest cosmic ray Forbush
decreases ever observed at Earth, the first (21\%) in July 1982, and the second (30\%) in June 1991.
The current leading hypothesis is that the heliospheric radio emissions are produced when the shock associated with the
outward propagating disturbances interacts with the steep plasma density gradient at the heliopause. The radio emission
frequencies are consistent with the currently estimated plasma densities near the heliopause.  From the propagation time and
the speed of the shock the distance to the heliopause can be estimated, and is in the range from about 110 to 160 AU.  Using
nominal parameters for the relative distance between the termination shock and the heliopause, the distance to the
termination shock is estimated to be in the range from about 80 to 115 AU.  It is anticipated that strong locally generated
plasma waves will be detected when Voyager reaches the termination shock.
In addition to radio emissions and plasma waves, the plasma wave instrument also detects small dust particles striking the
spacecraft.  A small but steady level of dust impacts has been observed throughout the outer heliosphere.  Although the
particle size is difficult to estimate (probably micron size), the impact rate can be measured very accurately, and is about
4 impacts per hour.  From the velocity and cross-sectional area of the spacecraft the number density of these particles is
estimated to be about 3 x 10$^{-8}$ m$^{-3}$. These particles are most likely of cometary or interstellar origin.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04b5dba2-0876-4f0a-8aca-b1d6701d6a12"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Global biogeochemical models need land cover change estimates annually at a 0.5 degree resolution. One potential source of
this annual product is an interpolation between the decadally spaced maps derived from high resolution (30-m) TM imagery.
Another option is to use annual coarse resolution (8-km) AVHRR imagery. We report initial results using aggregated 48-km
AVHRR data (0.5 degrees), calibrated with Landsat TM data, to evaluate historical disturbance patterns (1981-1994) for an
area in the Amazon region.  Optical measurements from AVHRR almost certainly contain artifacts that mask subtle year-to-year
changes in reflectance associated with the removal of some small fraction of forest from a 48-km grid cell. Despite this, we
have developed a model based on NDVI and TM which appears to capture some details of the spatial and temporal patterns of
disturbance. Though tropical rainforest monitoring is in many ways a remote sensing worst-case scenario because of the
region&#39;s high cloud frequency and the dominance of other atmospheric constituents in the signal, this study suggests that
AVHRR data contain information about disturbance patterns and rates.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04bb098f-4a35-41ba-9cec-6e922763c0b6"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Endemic Antarctic diatoms are incorporated into downwelling Antarctic Bottom Water (AABW) during shelf and open-ocean
formation, becoming effective tracers of this watermass in the worlds oceans. Significant quantities of allochthonous
Antarctic diatoms are recovered in core-tops along the known flow-path of the AABW in the SW Pacific. In contrast, core-tops
bathed in overlying watermasses such as the North Atlantic Deep Water (NADW), are devoid of such taxa.  The AABW/NADW
interface at 3735-4097m depth is clearly defined in the SW Pacific by this criteria, and closely matches that obtained by
physical observations (3800m).
Tracer diatoms in cores located within the flow-path are used to infer changes in AABW production for the last 190 kyrs.
Absolute abundances (valves per gram dry weight) provide semi-quantitative information on the volume of AABW entering the SW
Pacific. The diatom signature is complicated by likely fluctuations in productivity at the source. Interpretations based on
reworked taxa from Antarctic sediments may go some way to eliminating this problem but this signal is noisy.
Significant amounts of tracer diatoms are recovered from late Quaternary sediments in the SW Pacific (of the order of
10$^{5}$-10$^{6}$ valves per gram dry weight). A periodicity of 21.5 - 23.2 kyrs is apparent in both this signal and in the
reworked Antarctic material. Both the value and amplitude of the tracer diatom signal is enhanced during times of ice growth
prior to the last and penultimate glacials. In contrast, their abundance is reduced during glacial maxima. At these times
extended sea-ice suppressed productivity at the source, making inferences about shelf-AABW flow by these methods
inappropriate for glacial periods. However, relatively low amounts of reworked material support a reduction in flow during
the LGM, compared to the periods of ice growth. During the present and last interglacials, when tracer diatoms are presumed
to have been available for incorporation, their reduced abundance is also suggestive of a relatively reduced flow. This is
supported by a very low amount of reworked material.
The diatom data are compared to the carbonate and oxygen isotope signals in an attempt to assess the suitability of this
method. If care is taken in selecting meaningful taxa, there is significant potential for the use of displaced Antarctic
diatoms as a supplement to geochemical and sedimentological techniques in AABW flow studies. Areas of refinement lie in
further quantification via the application of statistical techniques, and the identification of different source areas in
Antarctica perhaps through contrasting the signals of different taxa.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04cb619c-3c0c-4ff9-ae23-43c9ca6ef86b"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Data from a novel tiltmeter, which is better suited to sensing the shape of the earth than usual instruments, is being
studied primarily by means of autocorrelation.  Preliminary findings suggest that coherent oscillations borne of
geocomplexity are to be seen roughly every other day.  These oscillations vary from short-lived long period types that are
consistent with the known free-earth eigenmodes, to oscillations with much shorter periods, measured in tens of seconds.
Data from the tiltmeter are complemented by simultaneous recordings from a Sprengnether vertical seismometer that has been
outfitted with a novel patented capacitive sensor.  Example results from a four month period
will be provided.  Additionally, some modelling ideas will be provided, drawing parallels between the earth as a complex
system and much smaller systems that exhibit catastrophe due to mesoanelasticity.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04d66f77-14a8-47a3-ad6e-5d625cf0b769"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Stratigraphy of some lava complexes in Serenitatis Basin of the moon was reexamined by Clementine UV-VIS spectral images to
understand spatial and temporal variation of chemical composition of the magmas filling the basin. Lucey et al. (1998) and
Giguere et al. (2000) showed that voluminous low to middle Ti basalts fill in most of the basins while occurrence of high Ti
basalts is limited mainly in eastern and western parts of whole of mare. Difference of titanium content of the mare basalts
indicates they were produced from different source materials, so that spatial and temporal distributions of the mare basalt
would clarify horizontal and vertical heterogeneity of the lunar mantle due to density overturn of cumulate pile mantle
during late solidification stage of the lunar magma ocean. The lava complexes of Mare Serenitatis and Tranquillitatis show
drastic changes of the chemical composition. And, some precise chemical and age data of the rock samples from these basins
which were sampled by Apollo 11 and 17, are available. Therefore temporal change of internal thermal structure in the moon
can be discussed by extrapolation of the age data from the Apollo samples to staratigraphy of the lava complexes.
Mare Tranquillitatis is mainly composed of high Ti basalts, but classification of the basalts into some units was difficult.
However the lava complexes of Mare Serenitatis are divided into six units by Clementine geochemical mapping. Although fifth
unit is composed of Dark Mantle Deposits (DMD) which are not considered to be lava flow units but pyroclastic deposits due to
rare explosive volcanism on the moon, other units are composed of a number of lava flow. And, chemical composition of these
units systematically changes, that is TiO2 contents of these units decreases from first unit to sixth unit. Especially,
change of TiO2 content between first unit and second unit is notable (more than 10 % to about 6 %). Among the second unit to
the sixth unit (except for fifth unit), TiO2 content decreases about 6 % to less than 1 % (very low Ti basalts). Although the
first unit is considered to be a flow unit which overflowed from Tranqullitatis basin, other units would flow from northern
side of the Serenitatis basin. The sixth unit can be seen only in the most northern part of the basin. These features are
important, because the change of chemical composition was accompanied with a change of the position of the eruption. These
would suggest the magmas having extremely different chemical composition were not produced in same area, reflecting chemical
heterogeneity of the source mantle. And, These igneous activities in Serenitatis basin would occur for about 100 million
years.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04dfb5e9-a164-46f1-8e10-b6f1a3d6b40c"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Beginning on 15 May 1998, a large storm system formed in Central Texas and traveled north into Canada.  The average ground
speed of this storm was approximately 27 m/sec (60 mph) throughout its lifespan.  During the storm, over 50 thousand CG
lightning flashes were recorded by the National Lightning Detection Network (NLDN) and 122 severe weather reports were
observed in association with this storm including 31 tornadoes of ntensities ranging from F0 through F3.  Of the lightning
flashes, nearly 60 percent lowered a net positive charge to the ground compared to an annual overage of less than 10 percent
for the continental United States. Examination of the first stroke peak currents for the positive flashes indicate an
increase in the peak current strength during tornadic events.  This was not demonstrated in association with the other forms
of severe weather, namely hail or episodes of flooding. Negative flashes did not show the rise in peak currents.  The rise in
the positive peak currents seem to occur about 90 minutes prior to the highest concentration of tornado sightings.
While this type of investigation in preliminary in nature and research is being conducted on the occurrence of intracloud
lightning associated with tornadic storms, it is still worthwhile to examine the CG lightning as this data is readily
available at this time.  As the total lightning data becomes available on a regular basis, the mystery of the lightning and
tornado connection will hopefully be resolved@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04dfe469-0936-4535-8c67-89ce17ddc1a9"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The glacially-influenced Cenozoic continental margin of Antarctica shows a large-scale subsurface stratigraphy consisting of
flat-lying &#39;topsets&#39; recording episodic aggradation of the continental shelf, that rest on seaward-dipping, wedge-shaped
&#39;foresets&#39; formed by progradation of the continental slope. Ocean Drilling Program (ODP) Leg 178 (February-April 1998)
drilled two sites (1097, 1103) through the outer Antarctic Peninsula Pacific shelf into strata no older than late Miocene or
early Pliocene ($andlt;$ 4.6 Ma). &#39;Topset&#39; stratigraphy comprises multiple units (up to 100 m thick) of deformation till recording
episodes of ice expansion to the shelf edge and subglacial reworking of marine sediment. Till is interbedded with intervals
of stratified and graded diamictites that contain in situ marine biofacies typical of proglacial marine settings recording
ice retreat from the shelf. During ice expansion, shelf sediments are reworked and transported downslope by debris flows
allowing the continental slope to prograde as &#39;foresets&#39;. ODP Leg 188 (January-March 2000) drilled in Prydz Bay at the
downstream end of the Lambert Graben which has served as the principle drainage outlet for the East Antarctic Ice Sheet
during the Cenozoic. Site 1166 drilled the mid-continental shelf and recovered the transition, as yet undated, from
pre-glacial to glacial conditions. The slope site (1167) in the Prydz Channel Trough Mouth Fan is characterized by debrites
separated by thin ($andgt;$ 1 m) intervals of hemipelagic and contourite sedimentation. Cenozoic facies and overall stratigraphy
of the Antarctic Peninsula and Prydz Bay continental margins are very similar and directly comparable with Neoproterozoic
glaciated continental margins of Rodinia after 750 Ma now preserved in Australia and western North America.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04e20655-55b1-4317-8cd3-bd36ef51815f"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The CERES (Cloud and the Earth&#39;s Radiant Energy Systems) instrument aboard the TRMM satellite made top-of-atmosphere
radiative measurements   in the three broadband scanning radiometer channels (0.2 - 200 micron, 0.2 -   4 micron and 8 - 12
micron), during the period Jan to Aug 1998. This channel   configuration permits us to split the entire spectrum of the
observed   outgoing longwave radiation into contributions from the window (8 - 12   micron) and non-window spectral regimes,
which in turn enables us to assess   the relative importance of the continuum, vibration-rotation and pure   rotational
absorption regimes on the overall radiative budget for the   first time.
Several thousands of sonde measurements collected over both ocean and   land stations collocated with the instantaneous CERES
single satellite   footprints are used in a model to derive the top-of-atmosphere longwave   fluxes. The subset of simulated
and the coincident observed flux data   are then used  in producing a seasonally-averaged regional   distribution of the
pure water vapor   greenhouse effect (without the effects of CO$_{2}$, O$_{3}$, CH$_{4}$ and other trace   gases) in the
window and non-window from observations for the first   time.  We next examine how the satellite-measured water vapor
greenhouse effect in the continuum, vibration-rotation and pure rotation   bands vary with water vapor by employing
appropriately scaled water   vapor amounts. Results also provide us with an observational insight   into how the   water
vapor absorbs radiation in the continuum, vibration-rotation and   pure rotational bands. This observational validation of
the physics of   water vapor absorption in the atmosphere has further implications to the   study of the natural variability
of humidity in the troposphere and how   the height-dependent changes in the moisture field can affect the water   vapor
greenhouse effect and  the crucial feedback in the atmosphere.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04f48fd3-a7e8-4998-ace5-88f713c01ad6"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Turbo-Rogue Global Positioning System (GPS) receiver
on-board the {\O}rsted Satellite is able to measure the
phase, pseudo-range and amplitude of the GPS satellite
signals, as well as give an initial measure of the
{\O}rsted orbit ephemerides. These data are processed
into profiles of the atmospheric refractivity,
temperature, and pressure, using advanced radio
occultation and precise orbit determination processing
software. More than 1,000 radio occultations have been
collected and processed into atmospheric profiles with
relatively high accuracy and vertical resolution. Using
additional temperature profile information from the
European Center for Medium-range Weather Forecast (ECMWF)
analyses, water vapor profiles in the lower troposphere
have also been obtained.
Orbit determination accuracies are of the
order of 2 m for the position and 1 cm/s for the
velocity, but are expected to improve. The first comparisons with ECMWF
analyses data show that the so-called dry temperature can
be obtained with a mean accuracy better than 1 K between
5 and 25 km. The standard deviation in the same interval
varies between 2 and 4 K, being smallest at tropopause
altitudes. Specific humidity and water vapor pressure
profiles are retrieved with an accuracy of about 1 mb between 2 and 8 km,
when compared to the ECMWF water vapor. Below 5 km a
large negative refractivity bias of several percent is
observed in the statistics. This is thought to be due
to spurious tracking in the lower troposphere where the
signal to noise ratio is reduced because of defocusing,
resulting in loss of lock on the signal in some cases.
A procedure for detection and removal of such errors are
being developed. The results of the
processing improvements and validation activities will be presented.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04f99e90-fcfe-40a2-bfdb-d235ecf51efc"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Arid regions in North Africa are an important source to the atmosphere of mineral aerosols, which can be transported to long
distances depending on the wind configuration in altitude. North-eastern Spain is often affected by African dust intrusions.
This dust can be deposited in wet form (often called red rains when particle deposition is identified visually) or in dry
form. Such a deposition has an important role in terrestrial and marine biogeochemical cycles. Several authors have found an
increase of the Saharan aerosol mobilization to the atmosphere in recent years, and have related this with changes in the NAO
(North Atlantic oscillation) patterns.
In this work, the frequency of African events during the period 1983-1999 is analysed at a rural site in Montseny (Barcelona,
Spain) with the aim of defining its temporal pattern, as well as to interpret the meteorological mechanisms associated. A
discriminant analysis has been applied to the chemical database of the rain at Montseny to obtain the annual African rain
frequency. The discriminant function has allowed us to distinguish the events with a clear African fingerprint,
(characterised by high alkalinity, pH and calcium content) from the rest of rain events. Next, the relation between the
number of African episodes and the NAO and SO (El Niï¿½o-Southern oscillation) index has been analysed. Results indicate that
the annual frequency of African episodes is inversely correlated with the NAO index (r=-0.63, n=16), but it is not
significantly correlated with the SO index (r=0.16). A negative correlation has also been found between the annual
precipitation and the NAO index (r=-0.6), which is consistent with the results obtained by other authors for the
South-eastern Spain.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/04fbfd28-de57-4b97-bc05-71f337a66436"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Radon-222, a naturally-occurring radioactive gas with a 3.8 day half-life, is produced by the decay of Ra in the sediments.
The residual of a Rn mass balance constructed for Catalina Harbor, CA requires an additional Rn input into the shallow head
of the harbor after accounting for benthic inputs, mixing, and evasion.  The suspected Rn source is the tidal wedge, the
volume of beach sediments saturated and drained due to tidal fluctuations.  The residence time of water in the tidal wedge
can be estimated based on Rn in-growth.  The residence time is important for calculating nutrient budgets for beach sediments
and  identifying the fate of particle-reactive pollutants flushed into the beach.  To test this calculation, 10 Rn samples
were collected along with hourly measurements of the tidal height and water table during a 25 hour period in a shoreline
normal transect of 7 borehole wells spanning 61m of beach.  Four wells were set every 4 meters behind the beach-berm break
and three were between the lower-high water stand and the lower-low water stand.  The mixed diurnal spring tides had a
maximum range of 1.7m.  Water in wells behind the berm had been in contact with the sediments for 24 hours.  The water table
in these wells oscillated only once each day, rising during the flood of the higher-high tide and then dropping throughout
the day.  The tidal wedge volume was calculated by extrapolating the range between the daily minimum and maximum that
occurred in all wells just before and after the flood of the higher-high tide, amounting to 7.3 m$^{3} per meter of beach.
Seaward of the berm, the water table also fluctuated with the lower-high tide.  This produced a smaller tidal wedge 2.2
m$^{3} per meter of beach.  The water draining out of the aquifer, sampled in wells just after the tide dropped below the
beach surface, had a concentration of 30-50 dpm/L, correlating to a residence time of 6-8 hours.  The average Rn flux from
draining the tidal wedge was twice the estimate required by mass balance.  The difference in fluxes is attributed to the
large tidal range and that the beach slope was less than the average beach slope in the harbor.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/05137def-4eba-4d7f-96dd-e620886239d1"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">One of the major issues in assessing the performance of a deep repository in crystalline geologic media concerns the ability
of the rock matrix to delay the transport of radionuclides because of its storage capacity.
From the modelling point of view, the strong contrast in the values of flow and transport parameters between the fracture and
the rock matrix leads to classical numerical difficulties and awkward computer simulation time for the transport problem.
In order to face situations involving a dense and well connected network of fractures,  dual porosity, and dual permeability
approaches have been developed in the hydrogeological field during the last two decades. In these studies, the flow problem
has been extensively studied but the transport problem has only been treated for a limited range of geometries.
This observation led us to study the different operational dual porosity models proposed in the literature, i.e. which can be
implemented in a 3D performance model. The approaches developed by Bibby 81, Huyakorn et al. 83 serve here as a basis and
are further extended to 2D and 3D matrix blocks geometries. These approaches are implemented and compared to reference
calculations for which the explicit geometry is taken into account. The validation is performed for a large range of
transport parameters values.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/052f9758-815b-4632-bacc-e279e4c45ecf"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Paleoseismologic observations of slip histories and slip rates of faults that break the surface are available at an
ever-increasing number of sites, but the nature of blind thrust faults has kept paleoearthquake information on these faults
out of reach. One complex network of blind thrust faults beneath the Los Angeles metropolitan region includes the Puente
Hills thrust fault (PHT) extending from downtown Los Angeles southeastward into northern Orange County.  This thrust is
active, as demonstrated by the 1987 Mw 6.0 Whittier Narrows earthquake (Shaw and Shearer 1999). Despite our awareness of the
hazard posed by this fault, we do not know its current slip rate or its earthquake history prior to the 1987 event. To
determine these critical data, we have begun a two-phase project in which we will acquire high-resolution seismic reflection
data and excavate paleoseismologic boreholes and trenches across the zone of active folding associated with major earthquakes
on the PHT.
During April 2000 we acquired high-resolution seismic reflection profiles across two transects of the PHT that image the
locus of active folding between 300 and $andlt;$10 m depth.  These data effectively bridge the gap between the available industry
seismic reflection data used to interpret the deep geometry of the PHT, and near-surface growth strata that have been
deformed by recent folding associated with slip on the PHT.  Our high-resolution data demonstrate that the zone of active
folding extends to $andlt;$10 m depth as a discrete ($andlt;$50-m-wide) zone that will be amenable to paleoseismologic excavations.
For example, in our western transect, across the Santa Fe Springs segment of the PHT in Bellflower, we observed an upward
narrowing growth triangle that can be traced to within 7 m of the surface. Total width of the dipping panel at this site is
only 50 m at 15 m depth. Total structural relief on this buried &#39;fold scarp&#39; is 2.75 m at 15 m depth.  These dipping strata,
which we interpret as having been deformed during earthquakes generated by the PHT, are on-lapped by flat-lying, undeformed
strata that have been deposited since the most recent large PHT event. Thus, we believe that we will be able to document
onlap relationships (i. e., angular unconformities) that record individual paleoearthquakes in this floodplain setting. Given
the narrow width and shallow depth of this deformed zone, we believe it will be accessible to our planned paleoseismologic
excavations, a transect of large-diameter (&quot;bucket-auger&quot;) boreholes, followed by conventional paleoseismologic trenches. We
anticipate that these observations will constitute some of the first documentation of individual paleo-earthquake ages ever
obtained from a blind thrust fault.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/054df4cc-9849-461e-bd8e-929e62c764a8"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Transmission of subinertial Kelvin Wave energy through a gap between semi-infinite seas is investigated.  Linear, inviscid,
1-1/2 layer dynamics are used, implying a deep channel and steep boundaries.  Existing analytical solutions, limited to
channel widths much smaller than the Rossby radius, are extended using a numerical model.  The amount of transmitted energy
is seen to depend not only on the ratio of channel width to Rossby radius, but also on the geometry of the channel mouths,
the channel length, and the wave frequency.  The wave transmission can be quite sensitive to these parameters, so that the
amplitude of the transmitted wave may be significantly different than one might expect from consideration of only the channel
width.  One interesting result is that at low enough frequency, virtually the entire wave can be transmitted through a gap
regardless of the channel width, as long as the linear and inviscid approximations hold.  General results will be discussed,
and an algorihm will be presented for approximate predictions of wave transmission through gaps with arbitrary coastal
geometries.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/056ab214-c378-44c5-905f-2ce45d1b5b6d"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">In order to characterize organic matter in a tropical mangrove ecosystem, stable carbon and nitrogen isotope analyses were
made for various organic samples including land plants, mangrove plants, soils, particulate organic matter (POM), and bottom
sediments at the southwestern coast of Thailand. The average \delta$^{13}$C of land plants and mangrove plants were -28.2 \pm
2.2 \permil (n=8) and -27.0 \pm 1.3 \permil (n=6) respectively. The mean \delta$^{13}$C value of POM in river water was
-26.1 \pm 1.2 \permil (n=10), which can be explained by a greater influence of C$_{3}$ plants rather than C$_{4}$ plants. The
mean \delta$^{15}$N value of mangroves (2.7 \pm 2.1 \permil) was approximately 3.2 \permil heavier (p=0.008) than that of
land C$_{3}$ plants (-0.5 \pm 1.1 \permil, n=7). Nitrogen utilized by mangroves was shown to be enriched in $^{15}$N compared
with those for a land plant. It was presumed that some specific nitrogen diagenesis such as denitrification near
water-bottom interface was suggested as a possible cause for this enrichment. The POM and sediments from the Trang River and
Ko Talibong area also showed systematically heavier \delta$^{15}$N than those from Ko Muk and other coastal areas. Organic
matters in the Trang River might be influenced by nitrogen released from agriculture or human waste, and consequently affect
the isotopic composition of POM and sediment in its estuary and the coast off the river. The \delta$^{13}$C of surface
sediments from coastal sea (-21.0 \pm 2.0 \permil, n=17) showed 1.7 \permil higher \delta$^{13}$C than that of POM of the
seawater (-22.7 \pm 1.7 \permil, n=16). In contrast, the \delta$^{15}$N of coastal sediments (2.8 \pm 1.2 \permil) showed 3.1
\permil lighter in comparison with nearshore POM (5.9 \pm 1.3 \permil). We applied a stochastic method to estimate
contributions of four organic endmembers which are identifiable by \delta$^{13}$C and \delta$^{15}$N. The result implied that
seagrasses could be a major source for sedimentary organic matter as 42 \pm 5 \% at Ko Muk area, while 36 \pm 5 \% at Ko
Talibong. Contribution of marine POM to sediment was estimated to be only 13 \% in Ko Muk and 19 \% at Ko Talibong. Mangrove
could be about 23 \% contribution in both areas and there were not obvious difference between these two areas. It was
concluded that the influence of seagrasses could be an important source for sedimentary organic matters in a coastal region
at the western Thailand.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/057cffbb-af3d-4dd5-a34d-3444f4e144dc"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Field measurements are being carried out at Barrow, Alaska to investigate differences in the energy budget and the mass
balance of major surface types in the coastal Arctic The objective is to clarify land-ice interactions in such regions.
Measurements were made from November 1999 through June 2000 at four sites including tundra, a fresh water lake, a sea water
lagoon, and shorefast sea ice. Extensive snow surveys conducted in early April indicated that snow was deepest on the tundra
and thinnest on the fresh water lake. Snow drifting and dune formation were considerably greater at the sea water lagoon
site. Because of the snow cover, albedos at all four sites were similar prior to the onset of the melt season. Lake and
tundra area-averaged albedos were slightly smaller due to thin spots in the snow cover. As melt progressed, albedos decreased
at all sites. Average albedos were largest for the lake site due to the formation of a drained surface layer, while tundra
albedos were smallest once the snow cover melted and exposed the underlying vegetation. Spectral albedos for snow and ice
were largest at visible wavelengths, while tundra values reached a maximum in the near infrared. The ice growth season lasted
from early October to late May. During this time, there was 1.54 (+/-0.05) m of sea ice growth and 1.49 (+/- 0.04) m of
lagoon ice growth, and  1.92 (+/-0.04) m of fresh ice growth. The upper 0.2 m of the lagoon ice was sediment-laden, resulting
in rapid melt and extensive melt pond formation. Sediment collecting on the bottom of these ponds further hastened melt.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/05850413-5cb7-402b-988f-25885cee659e"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We report the results of a series of numerical calculations relating ridge properties to the surface displacement caused by a
simulated dike intrusion event.  The model ridge is defined in terms of the geometry of the elastic near-surface layer, the
size and viscosity of a magma lens and the viscosity of the crust and mantle under such a lens.  Dike intrusion is simulated
in several ways, including as an impulse of displacement.  We also consider a sudden change in boundary condition along a
vertical line at the axis.  In this scheme we assume no horizontal displacement at the axis during a period of stretching of
several model years, followed by a short duration (days long) fixed hydrostatic pressure in a column of assumed intruded
magma.
Potentially measurable differences in short term (hours to days) and long term (years to 10&#39;s of years) surface responses
are predicted between cases with different viscous and elastic structures.  For a high viscosity (andgt;1016 Pa s) magma chambers
the short-term response is mainly confined to a distance from the axis equal to the brittle layer thickness.  For a lower
viscosity magma lens the short-term horizontal displacements are spread over regions wider than the lens.  The crustal
viscosity and the strength structure control the pattern of longer-term displacements.  Constraints that may be obtained from
suitably designed geodetic experiments on ridges might allow discrimination between competing models for axial highs, since
those models depend on the strength and viscosity structure of the axial region of a fast spreading ridge.  Such constraints
would also shed light on how flow of melt and partial melt occur in the lower oceanic crust, and specifically whether crustal
crystallization and differentiation occur mainly in a shallow magma chamber.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/058c8f3b-c613-4aee-9b22-c343de54c178"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">It is not known whether large earthquakes can rupture the central and southern Calaveras fault. Extending from the Calaveras
Reservoir to south of Hollister, California, the fault creeps rapidly (7 to 15 mm/yr) and is seismically active, with many
microseismic events and moderate-sized earthquakes. Historically, however, this section of the Calaveras fault has not
ruptured in any large magnitude (M$andgt;$7) earthquakes. Understanding the affect of high creep rates on the mechanics of
faulting is necessary to better estimate the size and frequency of future earthquakes along the fault.
We are investigating the late Holocene geologic record of the southern section of the fault, as revealed in paleoseismic
trenches, in order to evaluate whether large slip events have occurred at the surface or whether all of the slip is
accommodated as creep and small offsets (due to moderate-magnitude earthquakes). Four new trenches were excavated 5 km south
of San Felipe Lake, at Costa Ranch, where the surface trace is expressed as a 12-m-wide releasing step-over with sag ponds
and east-facing scarps. These reveal multiple expressions of how fault slip has been accommodated in the past approximately
3000 years. One trench across a 1.5-m-high fault scarp shows that the vertical slip resulted from purely brittle deformation
across an 80 cm zone, while another trench 4 m away shows the same scarp formed by predominately non-brittle warping over a 6
m zone (adjacent to a sag pond). The other two trenches cross the entire step-over and reveal that the faulting, although
accommodated on multiple strands throughout the zone, is heavily concentrated along a main trace that projects diagonally
across the step-over. Even with all of the exposures at the site, there are no upward terminations and all of the faulting
extends to the surface, likely the result of continued creep.
A conclusion on whether this part of the Calaveras fault has experienced large coseismic surface ruptures remains ambiguous.
All deformation exposed in the trenches can be explained by aseismic creep. However a few isolated features on the eight
trench walls can alternatively be interpreted as products of coseismic surface offset. These features are: 1) A scarp-derived
slope-wash sediment package downslope of the fault, forming as creep continues to refresh the scarp, thatmay camouflage a
colluvial wedge; 2) an accumulation of organic, non-stratified sediment with vertical boundaries along the main fault,
interpreted as either sediment accumulation in a creep-formed depression or as a fissure formed during ground rupture; and 3)
a knickpoint in the sediment at the main trace combined with sediment from mixed sources on the down-faulted side.  This may
be due to either a combination of bioturbation, down-faulting, and secondary shearing, or a fissure and an eroded scarp
free-face formed from coseismic rupture. If the Calaveras fault&#39;s slip is accommodated entirely by creep and
small-to-moderate magnitude earthquakes, then the potential for large damaging events may be currently overestimated.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0591918f-3e92-4084-b8fd-ff90f20859af"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We analyzed the $b$ value of the frequency-magnitude distribution ($log N = a-bM$) for the Landers and the Hector Mine
aftershock sequences in map view and in cross-section along the slip plane. In both sequences the b value maps exhibit a
general trend of higher $b$ values within the rupture zone and lower $b$ values in the areas adjacent to the ends of the slip
zone. In the northernmost 40km of the Landers sequence $b \approx 1.5$, whereas south of the southern end of the rupture
zone $b andlt; 0.8$.  The Hector Mine sequence reveals a similar pattern with $b andgt; 1.3$ in the rupture zone south of the main
shock and $b andlt; 0.9$ north of the northern tip of the rupture zone. No strong dependency of $b$ on depth is observed.
We explored several hypotheses that explain the observed systematic spatial $b$ value variations.  One possibility is that of
stress transfer from the area of slip to its surrounding areas, causing an increase in mean earthquake size in the areas of
increased stress. Volumes that experienced large slip would be depleted of stress and consequently display high $b$ values.
Preliminary results from stress tensor inversions of focal mechanism on nodes spaced 2 km apart support this interpretation.
Another possibility is significant fracturing or an increase in pore pressure caused by the mainshock, which causes a
reduction in earthquake size by limiting slip length. Directivity of the rupture seems to play an important role in
controlling areas of decreased $b$ value.
These spatial $b$ value maps can be translated into probabilistic aftershock hazard maps. We build upon the Reasenberg and
Jones (1989) aftershock model, which is based on the Gutenberg-Richter relationship and the modified Omori law. Parameters
$a, b, p$ and $c$ are allowed to vary spatially. The time dependent probabilistic aftershock hazard maps forecast peak ground
accelerations for a given probability level and period.  For both sequences we find that that the hazard of strong ground
shaking ($andgt; 10\%  $g) is concentrated, throughout time, south of the Landers hypocenter and north of the Hector hypocenter.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/05960d6c-62dc-42dc-894b-a10220366da2"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Trace element zonation in phenocrysts will diffusively decay to a stable &quot;equilibrium&quot; profile. Since this diffusion is
quenched by eruption, it is possible to derive a crystal residence time.  One of the problems of this technique is accounting
for changes in the partitioning of the trace element due to bulk composition variation.  This work widens the approach of
Zellmer et al. (1999), where plagioclase residence times were obtained from relaxation toward a square-wave bulk composition
variation.  The revised methodology will allow diffusive dating of continuously, compositionally zoned clinopyroxene
crystals.  A computer program is used to determine partitioning behaviour using the algorithm of Wood and Blundy (1997).  The
program iterates time backwards, in small steps, until the distribution of the diffusing species reaches a set criterion of
stability.  At this point a time is output for the crystal zone in question.  The model can cope with elements with
diffusivity values ranging from 10$^{-14}$ m$^{2}$s$^{-1}$ (Sr) to 10$^{-21}$ m$^{2}$s$^{-1}$ (Th), allowing age
determination over a wide range of timescales.  Furthermore, partitioning behaviours in amphibole and garnet are similar to
those in clinopyroxene (Liu et al., 1992) allowing similar models for these two mineral groups.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/05b834e7-7a63-43d5-a64e-ab8dca4411fa"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We study the effects on fluid flow and the magnetic field in a planetary ocean due to heterogeneous tidal heating in a mantle
below, by solving for convection in a liquid using the Navier-Stokes equations with full inertial terms.  A simple model
using cartesian coordinates solves for temperature, velocity, and magnetic field utilizing both Fourier and Chebyshev
numerical methods with the Boussinesq approximation. The combination of the orbital eccentricity and proximity of a satellite
to a giant planet, like Jupiter, continually deforms the mantle, producing internal heating.  The heat flux at the oceanic
floor is prescribed based on small-scale thermal instabilities predicted by recent 3D spherical mantle convection
calculations for Gallelian satellites.  These preliminary oceanic convection model calculations provide insight into the
types of general circulations and magnetic field structures that may exist within Europa&#39;s speculated ocean.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/05cfaa5c-c371-4ffc-bae5-fbba69afd984"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Deforestation in humid tropical regions of the world has led to speculation about the hydrologic impact of land cover change
on streams draining tropical rainforest watersheds.  Empirical evidence and analytical rainfall-runoff calculations indicate
that topography has a significant effect on the timing of runoff response to rainfall events in four topographically diverse,
1,000 km2 basins in the state of Rondonia, Brazil.  Thus, to understand the impact of deforestation on the hydrologic
response of these basins, it is necessary to also control for the influence of topographic ruggedness and how topography
interacts with land cover change.  Field evidence indicates that runoff from hillslopes is dominated by flow through the
unconfined soil-saprolite aquifer and by saturation overland flow during large, wet-season, storm events.  A groundwater flow
model (MODFLOW) is utilized to explicitly describe the influences of topography and land use at the hillslope scale but
finds that measured values of saturated hydraulic conductivity (Ksat) are insufficient to convey input.  Field observations
of typical depths to the water table in the wet season (2-4 m) indicate that the large volumes of saturation overland flow
implied by the model results do not occur.  To better simulate the observed flow regime, the presence of macropores as an
observed mechanism for conveying excess flow is explored.  Results indicate that steeper slopes have faster runoff response
and greater dry season flows. Areas with lower angle hillslopes saturate over more of the hillslope.  Deforestation is
simulated by reducing evapotranspiration using available micrometeorological data and by reducing near-surface Ksat using
measured values from pasture sites in Rondonia.  Deforestation causes discharge from the aquifer to the river to increase
throughout the year and saturation overland flow volumes to increase as well.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/05d975e6-ce9f-49d7-8e91-03132bce7b3b"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We have analyzed diverse suites of lower crustal xenoliths in order to characterize the Lu-Hf isotopic composition of the
lower crust and to constrain coupled Hf-Nd isotopic evolution within this reservoir.  We do so for three important reasons.
First, the Hf-Nd isotopic character of the lower crust is largely unknown.  Second, the lower crust has frequently been
suggested to be a hidden reservoir with characteristics that solve various geochemical mass balance problems (e.g., the Pb
paradox) within the Earth.  One point of interest in the Hf-Nd isotopic composition of the lower crust has been in its
relationship to the upper crust, mantle, and the bulk silicate Earth (BSE) reference.  It has been argued (Blichert-Toft and
Albarede, EPSL, 1997) that the BSE lies significantly below the terrestrial array and requires a hidden reservoir somewhere
in the Earth to balance terrestrial compositions.  Third, the lower crust may have been substantially reworked by melting and
intracrustal differentiation and may contain significant amounts of residual garnet which could impart an elevated Lu/Hf
ratio to the bulk rock.  These residual garnet-bearing assemblages could evolve to highly radiogenic and distinctive Hf
isotopic compositions over time.  Such radiogenic Hf character, however, would be the opposite of what is needed to balance
the terrestrial array with BSE.
Our main observations based on the xenolith Hf-Nd data are: (1) the measured lower crustal Hf-Nd isotopic compositions span
the range of compositions seen in upper crustal granitoids derived from lower crust, and are likely to be reasonably
representative of the lower crust; (2) these samples have a wide variety of parent/daughter ratios and evolve along somewhat
divergent Hf-Nd isotopic evolutionary paths; (3) in spite of this compositional diversity, all samples have evolved to
present-day Hf-Nd isotopic compositions within the terrestrial array.  Thus, Lu/Hf and Sm/Nd ratios are not greatly
fractionated by lower crustal processes nor does the lower crust appear to be a reservoir with a long-lived isotopic history
distinct from the upper crust.  A general feature of the lower crust seems to be that it does not function as a hidden
reservoir critical to terrestrial chemical mass balance.  Instead, it is merely a less incompatible-element enriched
equivalent of upper crust.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06041bfb-c0b3-401e-a8dc-7d503d14e676"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Site 1166 (Prydz Bay, Antarctica) recovered upper Pliocene-Holocene diamictons and diatomaceous claystones directly above
upper Eocene-lower Oligocene diatomaceous claystones with interbedded sand and lonestones. Below are, matrix-supported
fluvio-deltaic sands. Marine dinoflagellates, spores and pollen support a late Eocene (33.9 - 39.1 Ma) age for the
lonestone-bearing mudstones and underlying deltaic sediment. Lonestones suggest that the sediments were deposited during
transition from pre-glacial to glacial conditions. Spores and pollen assemblages also indicate the existence of a stunted
&quot;rainforest scrub&quot; flora analogous to parts of modern Tasmania and Patagonia in the Prydz Bay region at that time.
Carbonaceous siltstone and mudstone lie below the deltaic sediment and contain Late Cretaceous (Turonian)palynomorphs. Site
1167, on the Prydz Channel trough-mouth fan, recovered Pleistocene debrites composed of thick homogeneous packages of
gravel-rich sediments with sandy-silt matrix, separated by thin mudstone.  The debrites mark glacial phases with high
sediment input from grounding of the Lambert Glacier at the shelf break, and the hemipelagic mudstones represent phases with
contour-current deposition and the ice grounded inshore from the shelf break. Preliminary dating of the section indicates
that most construction of the trough mouth fan and hence most advances of the Lambert Glacier to the shelf edge took place
prior to 500 ka. This suggests a major change in behaviour of the East Antarctic Ice Sheet at this time.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/060792bf-2789-4808-b7c5-ab73f442b130"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Austral islands are characterized by some geophysical anomalies: unusually shallow sea floor, high concentration of
seamounts, low rate of subsidence, lineated gravity anomalies and elastic thickness lower than expected, considering the age
of the seafloor. The difficulty in sorting out the spatial and temporal pattern of the volcanism is that there are only six
emergent islands and most of the studies were concentrated at these islands until recently. Thanks to recent cruises, the
knowledge of the seafloor has increased considerably allowing some detailed studies to be carried out. In 1997, McNutt et al.
studied the southernmost part of this area and show the existence of three distinct volcanic chains: Macdonald, Taukina and
Ngatemato, from bathymetric data and dating of dredged rocks. In 1999, the ZEPOLYF2 cruise (see other papers in
this session), conducted a geophysical survey over the northern part of the Austral archipelago. More than 30 seamounts have
been completely mapped (multibeam bathymetry, gravity and magnetism) as well as the slopes of the islands. Three-dimensional
modelling based on this accurate seafloor topography allows us to compute the geoid anomaly due to the elastic response of
the lithosphere under each of the volcanic loads. The effective elastic thickness $T_e$ that minimizes the difference between
this computed geoid anomaly and the observed one is kept as a significant value for the lithosphere under the considered
volcanic feature, at the time of loading. We thus obtain 34 new estimates of $T_e$ in this zone.
The main result is the clear identification of two groups of seamounts presenting rather homogeneous value of $T_e$.
Relatively low values, $andlt;$ 9 km, to the south under two main seamount alignments and higher values, $andgt;$12 km, to the North
(including the Austral islands) corresponding roughly to the MacDonald line. Since $T_e$ is mainly related to the age of the
lithosphere at the time of loading, we can infer that the southern seamount group was created on an relatively young
seafloor, which is in agreement with the guyot shape of the more voluminous seamounts, and the northern one on a old
seafloor, during the last 10 My. The thermal and mechanical state of the lithosphere and the interactions between these
closely related areas showing such different values of $T_e$ will be addressed. Radiometric ages, soon available on most of
the studied seamounts, will be taken into account.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06115900-d7d6-4c98-a11a-ea55f8843736"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The large solar flare and coronal mass ejection on July 14 was important scientifically and programmatically.  The solar
events, the output of solar wind and energetic particles, and the terrestrial response to the enhanced electromagnetic
radiation, solar wind, and particles were well observed by numerous instruments.  It was a major event observed from &quot;cradle
to grave.&quot;  The solar energetic particles from the event affected, and in at least one case damaged, systems on a number of
spacecraft.  The increased atmospheric drag
produced by upper atmospheric heating led to a loss of control of a research satellite and possibly a permanent loss of its
scientific capabilities.  Effects were also detected in the electric power grid. Understanding and ulitimately predicting
this type of event is one of the goals of the Living With a Star initiative.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/061da65e-2cda-4516-8f40-3ea656d4624f"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Two telemetered tiltmeters installed in September, 1999 on the flanks of Guagua Pichincha, at 2.5 and 5 km from the dome show
inflationary/deflationary cycles that correlate with intercaldera seismicity and repetitive dome growth and destruction.
From September 1999 until present, data derived principally from the closest tilt sensor show that usually an inflationary
stage coincides with high seismic energy release and dome formation.  Dome destruction, accompanied by minor pumice ashfalls
and pyroclastic flows, was accompanied by deflation and then a return to near background tilt values.
From 18 December 1999 through 23 July 2000 dome no. 8 grew slowly and despite having the largest volume (3 million m3),
generated a deflationary trend in  mid cycle.  Starting, 05 July a constant inflationary trend is seen in both tilt sensors,
resulting in a  40 microradian change.  Previously tilt changes of only 15 microradian were observed which were associated
with dome volumes of  1 million m3.  The actual inflationary stage is of longer duration and of greater changes compared to
earlier tilt phases.
Four GPS campaigns made since Nov. 1998, employing double frequency receivers two km from  the dome and in the outer caldera
area, show minimal horizontal displacements and questionable vertical uplift.  Neither has SAR imagery of the caldera taken
in 1997 and 1999 shown changes.  Presently we conclude that the deformation detected by tiltmeters was of low magnitude and
short duration, likely associated with gas pressurization or magma ascent beneath the dome which relaxes after its
destruction by gravitational collapse.  Given this hypothesis, neither GPS or INSAR may be expected to show changes related
to oscillating small volume dome growth and destruction, since the changes are short term and may be within the range of
error.
Preliminary results from a GPS campaign made in late Aug. 2000 suggests uplift of  5-8 cm at proximal sites compared to data
collected in January, 2000.  As these GPS results correlate with the present inflationary trend observed in the two tilt
sensors and with a period of active dome growth we consider them more credible.  To date our preliminary model of the present
eruptive phase is of a small volume of degassed magma arriving at the surface after weeks or months of seismic unrest from a
shallow reservoir  that is located at about 3 km depth below the caldera floor.  Given the small volume of magma and shallow
depths both tilt and GPS results have shown only small changes. Still the present inflationary phase, being of longer
duration and greater magnitude may portend a larger volume of ascending magma.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06298d4b-ccfe-4b41-b7c9-9904873837d1"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We extend our previous analysis of convection including
a historical effect of yielding whose magnitude varies
horizontally [{\it Honda et al.,} 2000] to a flow in a box
with a large aspect ratio (= 8: previous case = 4).
Side boundary condition is periodic (previous case: symmetric) and
the maximum contrast of temperature-dependent viscosity is increased to $10^5$
(previous case: $10^4$). Thus, the
present study allows for much freedom.
The simulation begins with obtaining a statistically steady solution of
the convection without yielding. Then, we set a thin yielding area, where
yielding occurs, on the top surface. Historical
effect of yielding is considered by tracing once yielded segments as described
in {\it Honda et al.} [2000].
To mimic a
possible horizontal variation of yield stress, we set a non-yielding
area (NY area) with a thickness comparable to that of the NY area
in the middle of
the convection box. The
NY area changes its shape by the flow.
The obtained flow shows a considerable variation with time, that is,
the alternative
appearance of
plate-like regime and stagnant reime. Despite this, in the plate-like regime,
the place of the
sinking is restricted to the edge of NY-area where the yield stress changes.
This confirms our previous results.
Clear one-sided subduction occurs in this regime because the
effective viscosity of the ``overlying lithosphere&#39;&#39; is too high to bend.
The case without NY-area also shows a considerable variation with time.
However, the place of sinking changes its place.
Thus, the flow is strongly controlled by the horizontal variation of yield
stress.
The viscosity below the NY-area is higher than that of the surrounding area,
since the temperature below it
is low. Because of this, the case with NY-area preserves the memory of the
subduction at the edge of NY-area.
Since the flow of plate-like regime is
characterized by the long wavelength, the flow may &#39;feel&#39; the lateral variation
of yield stress. A combination of lateral variation of yield stress and the memo
ry of
past weak zone may play an important role in the style of mantle convection.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/062d25d6-f20b-4d26-b38d-456805bbc674"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Crevasse patterns are the writings in a glacier&#39;s history book --- the movement, strain and deformation frozen in ice.
Therefore by analysis of crevasse patterns we can learn about the ice dynamic processes which the glacier has experienced.
Direct measurement of ice movement and deformation is time consuming and costly, in particular for large glaciers; typically,
observations are lacking when sudden changes occur. Analysis of crevasse patterns provides a means to reconstruct past and
ongoing deformation processes quantitatively.
Geostatistical crevasse pattern analysis is a subset of geostatistical surface classification, a method designed on the
background of the theory of regionalized variables. In a generalization of the well-known variogram, the structure function
most commonly used in geostatistics, first and higher order variograms, experimental ``ordinary&quot; and residual variograms are
defined. Crevasse patterns are organized in classes according to principles of structural
geology, examples of classes are one-directional, two-directional, rhombic, en \&#39;echelon, chaos (geometric classification),
extensional, compressional, and shear types (kinematic classification). Parameters extracted from
generalized variograms are combined into feature vectors, and it can be shown that specific feature vectors are
characteristic of each of the crevasse classes.
Data types that may be used with geostatistical crevasse pattern analysis include satellite data (Synthetic Aperture Radar
(SAR) and satellite image data) and videographic data.
Association between geostatistic parameters and feature vectors and crevasse patterns is achieved using deterministic
decision rules or neural networks, and the reconstruction of the dynamics is then clear from the structural glaciology design
of the crevasse classes.
Notably, this is a method to reconstruct ice dynamics without the use of differential equations. The theory of physical
glaciology that is commonly
used today builds on the principles of continuum mechanics. A goal is to combine the algebraic-geostatistical and the
differential equations-continuum mechanics approaches in order to simulate ice dynamic processes resulting in given,
theoretical or observed, crevasse patterns. Basic ideas of the new theory will be given.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/062fbf8c-2af2-45c2-a675-3e93b3559d9d"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The TRMM-LBA field campaign was conducted in the southwestern portions of the Amazon in January-February 1999. The purposes
of the field program were to validate both TRMM satellite algorithms and TRMM cloud models.  Many convective systems were
sampled with a suite of instruments, including both remote and in situ sensors.  In this study we focus on the 17 February
1999 case, and examine dual-Doppler, polarimetric and airborne data in this mesoscale convective system.  Radar data were
obtained from the TOGA C-band radar, and the NCAR S-pol 10 cm radar.  The latter system also collected a full suite of
polarimetric variables including differential reflectivity and differential phase.  These variables yield information on
hydrometeor types and mixing ratios in regions that are difficult to sample with the in-situ aircraft.  We also analyze data
collected by the NASA ER-2 aircraft, in particular data from EDOP (X-band Doppler radar) and AMPR (Airborne Microwave
Profiling Radiometer).  This easterly case had strong convective cells exceeding 45 dBz.  Polarimetric results showed that
some convective cores contained  large, oblate raindrops, however, differential phase data indicated that considerable
precipitation mass exited in smaller drop sizes.  The dual-Doppler wind field revealed some intriguing low-level vortices
that influenced the flow up to 6 km AGL.  Near the end of this system&#39;s lifetime, a prominent radar bright band was evident
along with classic melting signatures in the polarimetric variables.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06341138-7201-4c0a-a1d0-df3652138db3"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Closure of fractures created during seismic slip along crustal fault zones has been indirectly observed in active seismic
regions such as the Nojima fault in Japan. Healing rates along faults at seismogenic depths at elevated stresses and
temperature must be strongly dictated by the kinetics of mineral-fluid reactions, but the operation of these at relevant
conditions is not well understood. We have conducted drained, triaxial, saw-cut, hold-slide-hold tests with the objective of
investigating evolution of strength and fault gouge texture in a series of 1-2 month-long experiments using gypsum as the
fault gouge. Though gypsum is weak and ductile the rapid kinetics of gypsum-water reactions at room temperatures makes it
amenable to such studies over a laboratory time frame. We also argue that the time-dependent deformational behavior of gypsum
at room temperatures is phenomenologically similar to that of quartz and feldspar at crustal conditions.
Stress-strain data for both dry and wet experiments are initially linear followed by quasi-stable sliding along the saw-cuts.
There is a similar increase in residual sliding friction strength in dry and wet experiments as a function of hold time.
However, wet tests are distinct in that they exhibit a peak strength, suggesting development of cohesion within the gouge.
The peak strength also increases as a function of hold time with unequivocal cohesion being observed for hold durations of 10
days or more. The increase in strength as a function of hold time appears to have a power-law relationship. In comparison,
experiments with wet calcite gouge over similar times behave in a manner analogous to dry gypsum. Petrographic observations
using optical and scanning electron microscopy indicate differences in the nature of deformation between dry and wet samples.
Samples deformed dry exhibit strong brittle signatures such as fracturing, cleavage development, and kink bands. Wet samples
lack these features. They on the other hand show an abundance of sutured grain contacts and triple junctions. Intergranular
porosity in the wet samples is nearly obliterated while it is preserved in the dry experiments. Permeability measured during
wet experiments show a greater reduction during the hold periods suggesting that time-dependent mechanisms such as pressure
solution and cementation are dominant means by which intergranular porosity is destroyed. These observations imply that
faults not only regain strength during interseismic periods but ubiquitous mechanisms such as pressure solution and
associated precipitation of cements are the cause of this strengthening.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0639fd7a-faa4-464f-8ae3-113f0c555dfd"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Magnetospheric Constellation is the NASA Solar Terrestrial Probe designed to reveal the global dynamics of the structured
magnetotail, the region that controls the complex energy flows of the magnetosphere. Previous observations and simulations
indicate that the magnetotail is structured on scales $\geq$ 1 R$_E$ in the magnetotail, and is dynamic on time scales $\geq$
10 sec during  geospace storms. To resolve the dynamic response and coupling of the entire magnetotail on such scales, an
observatory of 50 to 100 spacecraft will be deployed, each of mass 10-20 kg, making  independent vector field, plasma flow
and energetic particle measurements. The spacecraft will have nested eccentric orbits with common perigees near 3 R$_E$ and
apogees ranging from 7 to 40 R$_E$, yielding a relatively uniform mean spacing of 1-2 R$_E$ within the outermost orbit.
Deployment of this observatory will set a new precedent for the number of scientific spacecraft, instruments  and data
streams to be developed and managed for Sun-Earth Connection research. The Magnetospheric Constellation will be developed
using new methods for designing, producing, and calibrating scientific instruments for spaceflight, yielding an integrated
&quot;sciencecraft&quot; that can be reproduced inexpensively, yet with acceptable variations in calibration. The result will be the
capability needed to remove the ambiguity and controversy that have long plagued magnetotail research.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0647243b-807a-4812-a022-b292ad8e07d6"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Far from being a passive transmission line, the M-I coupling
medium is highly dynamic.  In mediating the flow of
magnetospheric energy into the ionosphere, it can adjust its
own structure, and can convert electromagnetic energy into
electron kinetic energy and vice versa.  For example, the
creation of auroral electron beams is commonly seen as a
response of the coupling medium to current injected by a
magnetospheric generator.  Similarly, the distinct structure
of the aurora may be more indicative of the properties of
the M-I couping medium than those of the auroral generator.
This presentation will explore possible reasons the M-I
coupling medium is structured into sheets, layers and
filaments, and will also address the factors that determine
the relative roles of electromagnetic and kinetic energy
flux.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0648ce7e-48b2-46f7-bbda-ebff4b91f38a"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We derived the rupture history of the August 17, 1999, Izmit ($M_{w}=7.4$) and the November 12, 1999, D\&quot;uzce ($M_{w}=7.1$)
earthquakes in Turkey from teleseismic body waves using broadband data of the Global Seismograph Network, aftershock
locations and mapped surface breaks. The centroid solutions indicate strike-slip mechanisms for both events. The Izmit
earthquake was characterized by rupture propagating predominantly eastwards. It consisted of a main rupture lasting about 25
s followed within 1 minute by two more events having $M_{w}=6.9$ and $M_{w}=7.0$, respectively. With the teleseismic data, we
could not resolve westward extent of rupture into the Marmara Sea. However, an upper bound of the seismic moment release
west of the epicentre of the Izmit event is estimated to be $1.9\times 10^{19}$ Nm corresponding to $M_w = 6.8$. The D\&quot;uzce
earthquake lasted about 14 s and was characterized by bilateral mode of rupture, in excellent agreement with mapped surface
breaks and
aftershock locations.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/064bb89e-b8b3-4ace-a324-c846f2339bfb"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Seasonal changes in the flux of individual planktonic foraminiferal species to the deep ocean have been studied in the
western equatorial Pacific, using material collected by sediment traps over a period of one year (1991-1992). Mean total
foraminiferal fluxes (TFFs) were 171 and 97 shells m-2day-1 at Sites N1 and N2, respectively. Seasonal change in the TFFs was
much higher than that in the organic matter (OM) and carbonate fluxes. The major species of planktonic foraminifera were
Pulleniatina obliquiloculata, Neogloboquadrina dutertrei, Globigerinoides sacculifer, Globigerinella aequilateralis and
Globigerinoides ruber. The foraminiferal assemblage was significantly different from that observed in the eastern equatorial
Pacific. Although food availability is generally important for foraminiferal production, TFFs were poorly correlated with OM
fluxes at Sites N1 and N2. In contrast, the OM fluxes were more positively correlated with andlt;63 ?m carbonate fraction, mainly
composed of coccoliths (r=0.87), which suggests that OM fluxes were more affected by phytoplankton productivity.
Symbiont-bearing species and N. dutertrei were predominant in the western equatorial Pacific. Fluxes in P. obliquiloculata
and N. dutertrei peaked in late August. In addition to high OM fluxes, stronger upwelling and/or thickening of the mixed
layer possibly occurred during Period 2 (August to October) relative to Period 4 (late December to April). However, the d18O
values indicate cooler surface water during Period 4 than Period 2. G. sacculifer showed a lunar reproduction cycle, which
was outstanding during July to October at Site N1. A similar profile was evident in the case of G. ruber, Orbulina universa
and G. aequilateralis. These four species are generally predominant in low latitude. Therefore, the lunar reproduction cycle
may play an important role in carbonate production in the equatorial ocean.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/064f1a9d-ac23-4c83-81f9-d9ab5a2e60da"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We collect PKP data recorded at a distance range between  130-142 degrees
from the Global Seismic Network (GSN) and many regional  seismic networks
to study the seismic velocity and Q structures at the top of  the inner core.
Because PKiKP and PKIKP have almost identical raypaths in
the mantle at this distance range, the differential travel  time and waveform of these two phases provide an ideal
opportunity to explore seismic velocity
and Q structures at the top of the inner core.
In a companion abstract (Niu and Wen, this meeting),
we show that global PKIKP-PKiKP differential travel times  clearly reveal a difference of seismic structure between
the &quot;western hemisphere&quot; (40$^{0}$E - 180$^{0}$E) and the  &quot;eastern hemisphere&quot; (180$^{0}$W-40$^{0}$E)
at the top of the inner core, with those in the &quot;western  hemisphere&quot; being slower than those in the &quot;eastern hemisphere&quot;.
In this abstract, we present detailed seismic velocity and
Q structures of these two &quot;hemispheres&quot; at the top of the  inner core from waveform modeling of the broadband PKP data
recorded in dense regional seismic networks.
Waveform modeling is performed in different frequency bands  to explore
seismic velocity structures at different length scales and  the frequency
dependence of seismic attenuation at the top of the inner core.
Preliminary results indicate that the &quot;eastern hemisphere&quot;  has faster isotropic velocities (about 0.8\% faster) and higher
attenuation compared to the &quot;western hemisphere&quot;. We will discuss in detail about the transitions of seismic  structure
across the inner-outer
core boundary, trade-offs among different models and how
our results at the top of inner core will affect
conclusions about the deep structures.
We will also discuss possible physical mechanisms to
produce these different seismic structures at the top of
the inner core.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/066af85d-8036-4491-b930-219b26e6e9e2"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Natural spinels are solid solutions of various end members including magnesioferrite (MgFe$_{2}$O$_{4}$), chromite
(MgCr$_{2}$O$_{4}$), spinel (MgAl$_{2}$O$_{4}$) and quandilite (Mg$_{2}$TiO$_{4}$).  Platinum group elements are known to be
compatible in some spinels, such as chromite or magnetite, but we lack a comprehensive understanding of the compatibility of
specific elements in spinels.   In order to investigate the effect of crystal chemistry on Ru and Ir compatibility, a series
of experiments was carried out with four magnesian spinel end members.  Each spinel was welded into a Pt capsule, together
with RuO$_{2}$, IrO$_{2}$ and Ir metal, and equilibrated for 24 to 48 hours in a piston cylinder apparatus.  Oxygen fugacity,
temperature and pressure were fixed at the Ir-IrO$_{2}$ buffer, 1500 ï¿½C and 10 kb, respectively.   Run products were mounted
in epoxy, cut and polished and analyzed with an electron microprobe.   The results show that Ir and Ru have the least
affinity for MgAl$_{2}$O$_{4}$, with $andlt;$1 wt.\% IrO$_{2}$ and RuO$_{2}$ dissolving into this spinel.  However, the solubility
of both oxides increases in the end members in the order MgCr$_{2}$O$_{4}$, MgFe$_{2}$O$_{4}$ to Mg$_{2}$TiO$_{4}$, with the
latter having $andgt;$20 wt.\% RuO$_{2}$+IrO$_{2}$.  These elements may prefer the Mg$_{2}$TiO$_{4}$ spinels due to their having
the same valence as Ti, but there is also an overall correlation with solubility and unit cell dimension of the end member
spinel.  In a general sense, these results show that Ir and Ru are unlikely to be hosted by spinel in peridotites, yet are
likely to be hosted by chromites, magnetites or titanomagnetites, especially in sulfide undersaturated basic magmas.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0684d55f-85a2-425b-974b-6a542ce2eabd"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Polar Ionospheric X-ray Imaging Experiment (PIXIE) has provided for the first time global conductivity maps derived from
space based auroral X-ray measurements.
In this study, we focus on an isolated substorm event occurring on July 31, 1997. From the PIXIE X-ray measurements, the
energy spectrum for the precipitating electrons can be derived. By then using a model of the upper atmosphere, the resulting
conductivity values are generated.
We present global maps of how the Hall and Pedersen conductivities vary during this isolated substorm with a time
resolution of 15 minutes.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/068a3e05-a459-4fcc-9f0d-cf75c4e0417a"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">High deposition rate marine cores recovered from the Indonesian region as part of the IMAGES IV project have provided
planktonic foraminiferal stable oxygen isotope records of sea surface temperature variability that reveal millennial scale
periods of SST cooling followed by abrupt warming episodes. These SST changes within the Pacific warm pool correlate well
with the Dansgaard/Oeschger cycles in Greenland Ice cores. Sea surface temperatures in the western tropical Pacific varied by
as much as 4-5oC during Marine Isotope stages 3 and 4. Subsurface temperatures recorded by thermocline-dwelling planktonic
foraminiferal species record similar temperature changes suggesting large changes in the thermal structure of the warm pool.
The large millennial scale changes observed in the Indo-Pacific region are analogous to warm and cool phases of the ENSO
cycle. As the distribution of heat within the tropics affects heat and vapor flux to the atmosphere, changes in the location
of tropical convection and perhaps changes in tropical cloud cover during MIS 3 and 4 may provide an additional clue that
millennial-scale climate variability recorded in ice cores and other high latitude sequences was a response to forcing that
originated in the tropics.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06a6393f-68b5-481a-9ea0-a9ee09efcbfb"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Cobb, Brown Bear and Axial Seamounts, located along and to the west of the Juan de Fuca Ridge (JdFR), are the most recent
volcanic expressions of the Cobb Hotspot.  In March 2000, the NOAA Ship Ronald H. Brown conducted a full-coverage SeaBeam
2112 survey of Cobb and Brown Bear to complete coverage between these seamounts and the JdFR axis. Cobb (3.3 Ma) is a
circular volcanic edifice that rises from ~2800 m at its base to within 34 m of the sea surface. The summit of Cobb was
extensively surveyed in 1956 using a single-beam echosounder, underwater video/photography and SCUBA diving observations.
The new multibeam data from Cobb clearly shows the distribution of wave-cut terraces along the summit, as well as landslide
scars along the flanks of the volcano.  Brown Bear Seamount (0.5-1.5 Ma) has an elongated (N-S) shape, which indicates an
interaction of the hotspot plume with the extensional-stress field of the JdF spreading center. Furthermore, Brown Bear has
two large (~4 km wide) summit calderas similar to Axial Seamount.  The calderas likely formed due to collapse of the summit
once plate motion moved Brown Bear Seamount west of the magma source. The composite bathymetry may give new insights into
patterns of magma supply, seamount subsidence, sea level rise/fall, and plate-motion rates.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06bd95f4-ec87-45d2-b92b-c2a6521cd77d"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The 15 June 1991 Pinatubo eruption is the largest eruption of the satellite remote sensing era. This study aims to compare
satellite observations of the eruption with the numerical model ATHAM (Active Tracer High Resolution Atmospheric Model),
which was developed at the Max-Planck Institute for Meteorology and German Climate Computing Center. The  basic parameters
(volcanic shape and height, vent geometry, eruption rate, atmospheric profile) of the model are assembled  from observations
and previous studies. Preliminary results show that the spreading rate of the umbrella cloud, possibly driven by gravity
currents produced by the axis-symmetric model version of ATHAM, is comparable to the velocity of gravity currents observed by
Holasek et al (JGR, Vol. 101, No. B12, 1996 ). It also shows that hydrometeors (cloud water, rain, cloud ice, and graupel)
in the plume come from both magmatic H${_2}$O and water vapor entrained from ambient air, and that most hydrometeors are
frozen to form ice or  ice-particle aggregates in the upper part of the plume (above 14-15 km). Also no significant
separation of gas and ash in the plume in the first 20 minutes after eruption is observed. But gas-ash separation is expected
with the starting of ash or ash-hydrometeors sedimentation and with the release of SO$_{2}$ from ice sublimation in the
stratosphere due to low relative humidity. The model shows a significant amount of SO$_{2}$ was trapped by ice particles in
the plume. The comparison of SO${_2}$ and ash separation from the model results to the remote sensing observations by UV and
IR sensors will be one of our research thrusts.  Since concentrations of SO${_2}$ in different hydrometeors  as well as gas
phase SO${_2}$ can be estimated from the model, our other research  objective is to use ATHAM to understand the second day
increase of SO${_2}$ that was observed by NASA TOMS (Total Ozone Mapping Spectrometer) satellite. The uncertainties caused by
a missing orbit of TOMS and the oxidation of H${_2}$O to SO${_2}$ by OH, which might be enhanced in the plume by the large
amounts of H${_2}$O, will also be considered.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06dc36d6-ed76-4fda-99fd-56d4af989492"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Total organic bromine and chlorine will be presented from samples collected by the NCAR Whole Air Sampler on the ER-2 during
the NASA SOLVE campaign.  We will provide a detailed analysis of the partitioning of both individual compounds and groups of
compounds that contribute to total organic bromine and chlorine.  We will also examine correlations of various compounds with
N2O and compare these with previous campaigns to assess temporal changes in mixing ratios.  Our measurements of total
organic bromine will be used in conjunction with age calculations and tropospheric temporal trends to calculate total
inorganic bromine, which we will compare with our calculations from previous missions as well as published estimates from
northern latitudes.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06ed79a9-0d8f-454d-b55b-196c0c0c4877"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Rainstorms in December 1999 induced thousands of landslides along the north coast mountain range in the state of Vargas,
Venezuela. Rainfall accumulation of 293 mm during the first two weeks of December was followed by an additional 911 mm of
rainfall on December 14 through 16. The landslides and floods inundated coastal communities resulting in a catastrophic death
toll estimated at between 15,000 and 30,000 people.
Debris flow damage to houses, buildings, and infrastructure in the narrow coastal zone was severe. Flash floods also
contributed to the general destruction on alluvial fans at the mouths of rivers draining the coastal mountain range. In time
scales spanning decades to centuries, the alluvial fans along this Caribbean coastline are dynamic. Because most of the
coastal zone in Vargas consists of steep mountain fronts that rise directly from the Caribbean Sea, the alluvial fans provide
the only flat areas upon which to build. Rebuilding and reoccupation of these areas requires careful determination of hazard
zones to avoid future loss of life and property. A limited assessment of the distribution and character of landslides is
currently in progress by the U.S. Geological Survey in cooperation with the Venezuelan Ministry of Environment and Natural
Resources.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06ee2232-c72f-4703-bd17-08fe5ddc7e4f"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">HIRDLS is one of four instruments that will fly onboard the NASA/EOS AURA satellite providing key scientific observations for
the study of chemistry and dynamics of the earth&#39;s atmosphere, with emphasis on the upper troposphere and lower
stratosphere. Temperature, the concentrations of several trace species, aerosol extinction, the locations of polar
stratospheric clouds and cloud-tops, and geopotential height gradients extending from the upper troposphere (under cloud-free
conditions) to the mesopause (i.e. 8-80 km) will be derived from HIRDLS infrared limb observations.As part of the
ground-data processing of measured limb radiance data, the presence of high cloud in the instrument field-of-view must be
detected and the cloud-top altitude determined, setting a lower altitude limit on the inversion of radiance data. We report
preliminary results from simulations of observed limb radiances in the presence of high clouds having limited horizontal
extent (e.g. ~ 110 km) along the line-of-sight (LOS) path.  The influence of cirrus clouds on observed limb radiance is
strongly dependent upon the location of the cloud along the ray path. A method for detecting the presence of cirrus using
optically thin spectral channels designated for aerosol determination will be discussed.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/06ee38f7-cc8e-4f1e-ac4d-feaba41ad176"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The lithospheric mantle has been recognized as excellent wave guide for high-frequency long-range P- and S-wave propagation
over thousands of kilometers. In a classical contribution to the emerging plate tectonics Molnar and Oliver (1968) mapped
travel paths of effective and ineffective Sn-propagation between source and single stations on a global scale. They found
that Sn is capable to cross continental and oceanic plates, passive margins, but will stop as soon as its travel paths
traverses a ridge or an active margin. Recent work on controlled source seismology data - the Russian Peaceful Nuclear
Explosion data - revealed that the wave guide is established by horizontally extended velocity fluctuations in the
lithospheric mantle, so-called &#39;schlieren&#39;. This has been proven by 1D and 2D modeling of elastic wave propagation, that
succeeded to explain the key features of long-range Pn observations: low apparent velocities (8.1 km/s), high-frequency
content, long coda. We argue that two features characterize the lithospheric &#39;schlieren&#39;: (1) a change in scale-length of
velocity fluctuations at Moho-level; (2) their disappearance at active plate boundaries. The generation and destruction of
the &#39;schlieren&#39; in oceanic and continental lithospheric mantle is not yet clear. Allegre and Turcotte (1986) suggested in a
Nature paper on marble-cake models a mechanism for the generation of fine structure in the Earth&#39;s mantle together with two
mechanisms of destruction. One of the destructive mechanisms is partial melting in the upwelling mantle convection at ocean
ridges. The blockage of the Sn-phase at ocean ridges may in fact be related to the destruction of fine structure at the
ridges and their gradual built up as the oceanic lithosphere moves away from the ridges. Thus the &#39;schlieren&#39; may be
gradually frozen in traces of plate motion and thus provide hints to the mechanical coupling of the convecting mantle to the
rigid plates.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/07016119-a0de-470f-b074-3e8d33fa3ba4"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We present long (8 ms) optical and radio frequency (RF) timeseries of intracloud lightning events observed with the FORTE
satellite in January 2000.  The optical lightcurves have 15~$\mu$s resolution, while the RF have 3~ns resolution (which we
bin to 2-15~$\mu$s in the interests of efficient data handling).  Each 8 ms record contains multiple RF and optical events,
with a very high event-for-event RF/optical correspondance.  We identify the general type of discharge for each event using
discrimination techniques reviewed here.  The rate of RF/optical coincidence depends on the type of discharge studied: 100\%
of VHF signals from initial, negative return strokes have an associated optical signal, while 50\% of diffuse in-cloud events
and 20\% of impulsive in-cloud events appear to have optical counterparts.  Whereas the RF signals from ground strokes
clearly coincide with optical signals in almost all cases, the intracloud lightning typically shows nearly continuous RF and
optical emissions which do not necessarily correlate with one another.  We see a large number of paired, impulsive events in
the RF, which are presumed to be weak transionospheric pulse pairs (TIPPs) and which allow us to study the heights within
clouds of several events.  The coincident RF and optical pulses do not show a well-defined relationship of intensities, for
any lightning type.  The observed delay between the RF and optical pulses we interpret as mainly an effect of the scattering
experienced by the light as it traverses the cloud.  For in-cloud lightning, we find no evidence of an intrnsic delay at the
source between the onset of the RF and optical signals, and in fact the optical light tends to precede the RF.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0708e6c7-5724-4a31-872a-d395d6b5a891"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">New evidence from piston core RC11-83 (42 deg. S.) demonstrates that direct correlatives of the North Atlantic&#39;s 1500-year
cycle are present in the South Atlantic.  The best evidence is from the interval between about 10 ka and 50 ka where the
cycles are defined by robust variations in the petrologic composition of ice rafted sediment.  The most distinct of those
variations is in the percentage of fresh volcanic glass that probably came from the active volcanic chain of the Scotia
Arc/West Antarctic Peninsula.  The ice that transported the volcanic material most likely originated in the Weddell Sea.
Cycles defined by variations in ice-rafted volcanic glass have been identified previously in the South Atlantic and linked to
the North Atlantic&#39;s Heinrich events.  Here we document for the first time a nearly one to one match of the volcanic
material with both the Heinrich events and the 1500-year cycle.  Combined with recent evidence that the Holocene part of the
1500-year cycle is present in the subtropical North Atlantic, it now seems clear that the cycle has at least a bi-hemispheric
imprint.
The relation between the 1500-year cycle in the South Atlantic and shifts in North Atlantic Deep Water (NADW) production, as
thought to be documented by variations in benthic carbon 13 at RC11-83, is complex.  Some of the South Atlantic cycles appear
to lead reductions in NADW while others appear to coincide with reductions in NADW.  Thus, it is unclear whether the
presence of the 1500-year cycle in the South Atlantic reflects the operation of a deep circulation mechanism or an ocean
surface-atmospheric process.  The absence of a clear and consistent relation between NADW shifts and the 1500-year cycle in
the South Atlantic, moreover, raises the possibility that its origin does not lie in a bipolar see-sawing of the deep
circulation.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/071652c1-c7a2-4265-b748-86ef9356c637"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We present Mars Global Surveyor observations of hot diamagnetic cavities upstream of the Martian bow shock. The events are
characterized by a region of turbulent magnetic field, high electron temperature, and densities at or below the ambient solar
wind density. The region of high temperature is flanked by layers of high magnetic field and electron density and is
accompanied by a rotation in the interplanetary magnetic field. We suggest that the events are generated by interplanetary
discontinuities interacting with the Martian bow shock, i.e., the events are the Martian counterpart to the hot flow
anomalies (HFAs) observed upstream of the Earth&#39;s bow shock. The existence of these events upstream of both the Martian and
terrestrial bow shock suggests that the two bow shocks have similar properties although the nature of the two solar wind
obstacles (one with and one without a magnetosphere) are quite different. The existence of HFAs upstream of both the
terrestrial and the Martian bow shock also eliminates the possibility that HFAs are generated at the planetary magnetopause,
since Mars does not have a magnetosphere. The observations presented here also imply that hot flow anomalies are present not
only upstream of the subsolar bow shock, as seen at Earth, but also at the flank where the shock is substantially weaker.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/071a669f-b6e6-4573-984e-366b535ffcdf"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The enhanced sequestration of C in soil is one way to reduce rising carbon dioxide concentrations in the atmosphere.
Previous work has focused on the enrichment of soil organic matter (SOM) in an effort to increase the productivity of
different land use systems.  The novelty of this new research is derived from an interest in optimizing C storage to reduce
greenhouse gas emissions without compromising land productivity.  We seek here to establish the role of soil microbial
structure and function in the metabolism of freshly added C in four soils of different origin.  The soils studied were
collected from agricultural, desert, grassland, and forest ecosystems and have well documented management histories. We have
selected paired samples from each site for study.  The paired samples represent contrasting management histories, location
along a climate gradient, or establishment age.  The relative contributions of fungal and bacterial metabolism of a model C
substrate were quantified.  As a group, fungi have greater C use efficiencies than bacteria and therefore are more likely to
govern long-term storage of C in soil. Thus, identifying the contributions of these two taxa of organisms in soils of
different origin may be a fundamental piece of information necessary to designing land management strategies for enhancing C
sequestration in soil.  In addition to classical biochemical techniques for measuring fungal and bacterial contributions to
soil respiration, we have also pursued alternate measures of the two groups: direct microscopy and phospholipid fatty acid
(PLFA) analysis.  This allows us to comment critically on these techniques and to draw more rigorous conclusions regarding
the biological activity of these soils. The comparison of soils of different ecosystems will aid in the identification of
terrestrial ecosystems best suited to being managed for the sequestration of C away from the atmosphere.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/072c54f5-c775-4a2e-a2e5-081d18492788"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We collected at least 10 separately oriented drill cores from 26 sites in the Snake River Plain volcanic field in
Idaho.  Step-wise AF and thermal demagnetization results in the isolation of a well defined characteristic directions in the
vast majority of specimens.  The NRM directions from 20 of these sites are well grouped prior to any demagnetization.  Of
these, 12 are normal and 8 are reversed.  All but one site yield well clustered data after demagnetization.     These data
will provide   a new data set that meets the strictest standards for inclusion in the data base for constraining the time
averaged field over the last 5 Myr.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/072e99cd-89ea-4ceb-9f6e-f96e95e3a30a"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Continental shelves have been identified as sites for cold seeps, where release of fluids and gases from sediments to oceanic
bottom water occurs as the result of tectonic compression of sediments.   Frequently, these seeps are characterized by
elevated levels of methane.  The quantification of microbial oxidation of methane at seeps is important in constraining the
oceanic methane budget and the amount of methane that may eventually be released to the atmosphere, where it may contribute
to global warming.  The control of microbial methane oxidation rates at seep sites is not well-understood.  In order to gain
a better understanding of the role of methane oxidation in determining methane fluxes, we examined microbial methane
oxidation activity in sediments and near-bottom water at: 1) cold seep sites devoid of gas hydrate (San Clemente escarpment);
2) on seep sites overlying gas hydrate bearing sediments (Hydrate Ridge); and 3) non-seep sites in East basin and San
Clemente basin.
Specific methane oxidation rates (equal to the fraction of available methane removed per unit time) within surface
sediments of seep sites was orders of magnitude higher than values measured at background stations. At all sites, methane
oxidation rates decreased with depth in the sediments. Within the seeps, the highest specific methane oxidation rates were
recorded at the San Clemente seeps, reaching values as high as 0.31 per day. These sites were characterized by the presence
of tube worm and clam colonies.  On Hydrate Ridge, specific methane oxidation rates in surface sediments were significantly
higher at clam sites (up to 0.30 per day) relative to bacterial mat sites (up to 0.014 per day).  Lower methane activity at
mat sites may be due to lower levels of available methane for methane-oxidizing bacteria due to higher flow rates and the
predominance of gaseous methane (as opposed to dissolved methane) produced by large-scale decomposition of hydrates.
Alternatively, methane oxidation rates at clam sites may be positively affected by different  pore water chemistry resulting
from the biopumping action of clams, which generates a downflow of seawater, rich in electron acceptors such as oxygen and
sulfate into the upper sections of the sediment.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0738e042-6e24-4a3b-b3dd-8eb669cd4d81"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We investigate the onsets of energetic (0.1-3 MeV) ions in impulsive Solar Particle Events (SPE) using the Geotail/EPIC and
ACE/EPAM high-resolution particle instruments.  Geotail, in a 10x30 Re equatorial orbit, samples the near-Earth region and
ACE, stationed 230 Re upstream of Earth at L1 in the solar wind, monitors approaching solar flux.  SPE ion flux ranges from
being very anisotropic and field aligned during some onsets to being somewhat isotropic during other onsets. Abrupt
anisotropic onsets are useful diagnostics for studying the entry of SPE ions into the magnetosphere.  Since 1997 these two
spacecraft have often measured SPE ions while both spacecraft were in interplanetary space and the occurrence frequency of
SPEs has remained high during the current phase of maximum solar activity, thus providing a large sample of impulsive events
for possible analysis.  Energetic SPE ions frequently accompany the shocks produced by Coronal Mass Ejections CMEs.  By
intercomparing the ion flux variations in interplanetary space, both with and without compensation for field line convection
delay, we can both determine the range of 200-Re scale spatial and temporal variations that are presented to the
magnetosphere when SPE ions approach Earth and gain more understanding of the large-scale structure of CMEs.  Analysis of
several intervals will be presented.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/07445801-7793-472f-ae79-9ce6ac8d00b3"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">An idealized two-dimensional model is presented for the
interaction of the solar wind with the Earth&#39;s magnetosphere.
The model is based on a resistive MHD description of
the solar wind and magnotespheric plasma.  The result is
a set of five coupled nonlinear partial equations for five
unknowns.  The equations considered are the convection
and diffusion of a magnetic stream function (the vector
potential), the continuity equation two conservation
equations for momentum and the energy conservation
equation.  All transport equations are discretized with
finite elements on an unstructured triangular mesh.  The
Earth stream function is approximated as a 2D dipole
field and the incoming solar wind density and magnetic
field are treated as given.
Preliminary results are presented for a fixed
(non-adaptive) and unaligned mesh.  An assessment is
also made of the use of mesh adaptivity, to better resolve
regions with strong spatial variations, as well as mesh
alignment, to better resolve strong transport anisotropies.
The extension of the model to three spatial dimensions
is also discussed.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0746871a-7c7b-475c-95ea-b5ad36d89fb5"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">This study investigates the shallow subtropical/tropical overturning
circulation (STC) in the Pacific using the hydrographic data formed
by the combination of NODC98 and PMEL data sets covering the last
50 years. The inclusion of PMEL data, in particular the CTD surveys
during the service of TAO moorings, significantly improved the data
coverage of the 1990s in the tropical region. The subtropical/tropical
communication pathway is delineated by the spatial patterns of the
salinity, potential vorticity (PV), and geostrophic flows on isopycnal
surfaces. In the northern hemisphere, the mean interior pathway takes
a zigzag detour around the high PV associated with the ITCZ, with a
mean equatorward transport of 7 Sv. The interior communication between
the tropical and subtropical gyre is more direct in the South Pacific,
with a mean equatorward transport of 14 Sv. The data allow for an
examination of decadal time scale variations about the mean.  The most
significant change occurred in the northern hemisphere STC in the 1990s
when the interior flow was reduced by one half in the thermocline. These
results are consistent with Sverdrup solutions forced by various wind
products from COADS, FSU, ECMWF, NCEP, and satellite measurements.
The period of the 1990s was one of unusual warmth in the tropical
Pacific, suggesting that decadal changes in the shallow thermocline
circulation may play a role in regulating the background state of
the ocean in this region.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0746ac98-11be-4fae-878c-20efa438c78c"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">During the G8 encounter of Galileo with Ganymede, the spacecraft penetrated the region of closed magnetic field lines at the
upstream side (with respect to the corotational flow) of the moon with closest approach at $\sim 1600$ km. {\em Volwerk et
al.}\ [JGR 104, 14,729, 1999] found that during three minutes around closest approach, the spacecraft remained close to
$L=2$, and identified a harmonic spectrum of transverse waves that agreed well with the spectrum of poloidal field line
resonances for a dipole, as calculated by {\em Cummings et al.}\ [JGR 74, 778, 1969]. The inferred mass density at Ganymede&#39;s
equator and $L=2$ was 2 AMU cm$^{-3}$.
On 20 May 2000 Galileo returned to Ganymede (G28), again on the upstream side of the moon, this time at an altitude of $\sim
900$ km, penetrating much deeper into the closed field line region than on G8. Around closest approach the spacecraft remains
approximately on an L-shell of 1.4.
We will present magnetometer data from the region of closed magnetic field lines and show that once again an harmonic
spectrum is present in the transverse components of the magnetic field that correspond to field line resonances. We will
discuss the inferred density and how it relates to the density that was deduced in the G8 flyby. {\em Volwerk et al.}\
considered a convective flow that transported H$^+$ from Ganymede&#39;s ionosphere to its magnetosphere, requiring an ionospheric
ion density of 200 cm$^{-3}$. We will relate this model to the data for the G28 flyby.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/074e40e7-f3e8-4291-ab4d-aa2891311f4b"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">When the IMAGE spacecraft is in the low altitude trough region just
outside the plasmasphere, the Radio Plasma Imager excites plasma waves in
both the ordinary and extraordinary modes at LF and MF frequencies. A
subset of these waves propagate towards the Earth, are reflected at lower
altitudes, and return to IMAGE, where their time delay and dispersion are
measured. In the present work we show how the properties of the reflected
plasma waves can be used to determine the distribution of the cold plasma
below the spacecraft.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/07532ab4-4bc2-41a8-8c00-1981db9c2cd5"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We present paleomagnetic and rock magnetic results from Ocean Drilling Program (ODP) Leg 178 Site 1101 ($64\deg 22&#39;$S,
$70\deg 16&#39;$W, 3509 m water depth). The site is located centrally within a large sediment drift situated on the continental
rise west of the Antarctic Peninsula. Shipboard paleomagnetic measurements showed that Site 1101 was characterized by the
most complete Matuyama sequence cored during ODP Leg 178. Our study covers the interval 0.7-2.1 Ma, encompassing the
Brunhes/Matuyama geomagnetic reversal, the Jaramillo Subchron, and the Olduvai Subchron. Directions and intensity of the
natural remanent magnetization (NRM), the anhysteretic remanent magnetization (ARM), and isothermal remanent magnetization
(IRM) were determined using measurements made on u-channel samples. Over the interval investigated, the magnetic mineralogy
is dominated by pseudo-single-domain magnetite. Continuous measurements of low field magnetic susceptibility (k) and karm/k
yielded a marked cyclicity matching variations in terrigenous and biogenic intervals. This property was used to improve the
age scale initially derived form the magnetostratigraphy, through correlation of the magnetic susceptibility to a
$\delta^{18}O$ target curve, in our case the benthic record from ODP Site 677.
Inclination values show the clear alternation of magnetic polarities initially depicted by the shipboard data. Two short
intervals with anomalous inclination values are observed close to the Jaramillo Subchron, at (1.09-1.1) Ma and (1.15-1.17)
Ma. This pattern is similar to what was observed at ODP Site 1021 in the Pacific ocean, where those directional changes were
tentatively attributed to the Punaruu and Cobb Mountain events. A record of relative paleointensity was obtained through
normalization of the natural remanent magnetization (NRM) by the magnetic susceptibility, the anhysteretic remanent
magnetization (ARM), and the isothermal remanent magnetization at saturation (SIRM). Comparison of the relative
paleointensity results with other marine records shows the existence of correlative features which are interpreted as a
recording of global-scale field variations.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/07585ba9-8508-400c-81bb-bf9bf79cbf10"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">There are some active volcanoes and frequent earthquake swarms along a narrow zone near the summit of the mountain range.
There are several high mountains of more than 3000m in the range. There occurred, however, no large earthquakes along the
central part of the mountain range but some large events occurred only at the foot of it. According to some reports there are
low gravity and low velocity anomalies and high attenuation of seismic waves along the mountain range. We estimated
three-dimensional P- and S-wave velocity structure in the Hida mountain region, to derive detailed inhomogeneous structure
concerning to volcanoes and large inland earthquakes. We applied three-dimensional seismic wave inversion technique to
determine velocity structures. We used two datasets; one  from newly installed dense station networks in the area from June
1996 to November 1998 including earthquake swarm data in August 1998 , and the other from temporary joint observation by
universities ({it GROUPS96}) from June to October in 1996. We used  P and S-wave travel times of 4666 events selected on
several conditions. The total number of both P and S wave data is 76,624 at 99 stations. Our study area is an area of
$35.50\deg$N - $36.50\deg$N in latitude by $137.00\deg$E -$138.00\deg$E in longitude with grid spacing of 5km in space and
3km in depth.  Several low velocity zones are found along the Hida mountain range. The lowest P-wave velocity zone of about
12\% is located at between most active volcanoes Mt. Yake and Mt.Norikura. Another low velocity area of 5-6\% is in the east
of an active volcano, Mt. Ontake at 6km depth. These changes in velocity are nearly the same at depth of 9km. The low
velocity zones are roughly correlated with shallow cutoff depths of the seismogenic layer. Therefore, the low velocity zones
seem to correspond to the areas of high temperature.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0777bd64-4d7e-4a06-94af-a2d29c420680"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Acquiring information on the type of singularity that occurs at the
upper mantle $\mathrm{410\,km}$-discontinuity is important for
understanding the mantle dynamics and mineralogy at this transition.
New developments in monoscale data analysis make precise
determination of the singularity order possible from mode converted
seismic data. Application of this new method to characterize the
sharpness of the 410 discontinuity has shown (i) the 410 to behave
as a fractional order transition with order $\alpha=0.24$; but (ii)
there is no evidence of a characteristic length scale.  Both
observations have important implications for models of elastic
and/or mineralogical changes that take place at this transition.
The non-integer value of the transition&#39;s singularity and the lack
of a characteristic length scale are both indications of non-trivial
critical behavior, which can not be explained by the current
discontinuity models. So far, these models are defined by linear
and/or cubic functions, which describe the mineralogical and/or
elastic behavior across the transition zone, whose sharpness is
parameterized by the characteristic length scale [Gaherty
\textit{et.~al.},\textsc{grl},&#39;99]. In our approach the sharpness is
characterized by the singularity order, extending the trivial order
one singularity to which both the transition models and yield
function for the high pressure phase are limited
[Stixrude,\textsc{jgr},&#39;97].
As suggested by the $P410s$-waveform data, seismic observations call
for a non-trivial order singularity in the elastic parameters. Such
a singular behavior is related to a critical phenomenon.  This
critical behavior is either the result of a non-linear relationship
between gradual mineralogical composition changes and the elasticity
or could be caused by a thermodynamical phase transition in one of
the constitutive minerals. In the first case a percolation threshold
could be responsible for the non-linear dependence of the elastic
parameters on the mineralogical composition.  Anomalous
compressibility of free silicate provides another explanation for
the observed asymmetric $P410s$-waveform. In both cases, the elastic
parameters contain a fractional order singularity in the
depth-dependent elastic parameters. We present the new analysis
method and preliminary results of its application to broadband data.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0778f5b9-bbdb-4fe2-828b-c84c1ee1d088"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Observations of shear-wave splitting in plume regions may give important observational insight into the kinematics of
upper-mantle flow and lithosphere/asthenosphere interaction.  The &quot;parabolic flow model&quot; predicts the geometry of flow above
a mantle upwelling.  Specifically, it predicts a characteristic spatial pattern of anisotropy, which allows us to constrain
volumetric flow rate and relative motion of the plate with respect to the plume source.  Shear-wave splitting and anisotropy
can also help constrain rheological material behavior since it distinguishes regions of dislocation creep from those of other
deformation mechanisms.
In general, dislocation creep controls deformation in the lithospheric mantle.  However, in regions of elevated mantle
temperature, the zone of dislocation creep may deflect into the asthenospheric mantle.  In regions around mantle plumes
therefore, upper-mantle shear-wave splitting may provide information about the kinematics (and also the dynamics) of the
interaction between the lithosphere and asthenosphere.  This presentation will focus on testing kinematic models of the
interaction between the lithosphere and asthenosphere by comparing teleseismic splitting measurements of SKS, SKKS, S, and
ScS with those predicted for the various models.  We use previously published shear-wave splitting measurements from five
stations on the
Hawaiian Islands (PELENET and KIP), and augment them with measurements from an ocean-borehole seismometer located 220 km SW
of Hawaii (OSN-1 pilot study) and an ocean-bottom seismometer located between Hawaii and California (H2O).  Waveforms from
these stations are very important, as they provide well-constrained measurements that are not influenced by the Molokai
fracture zone, which possibly influences
splitting measurements beneath nearby island stations.  In addition, the high quality of these data and the splitting
measurements document the importance of ocean-bottom seismometers.
Preliminary data suggest that splitting beneath H2O can be explained by a single-layer model with an ENE fast polarization
direction that is consistent with a fossilized lattice preferred orientation of olivine parallel to the spreading direction.
Preliminary data from OSN-1 can also be explained by a single layer of anisotropy, but
with an ESE fast polarization direction and a relatively large delay time.  We cautiously interpret this splitting and the
splitting beneath the islands as due to upper-mantle anisotropy predicted by the &quot;parabolic flow model&quot;.  This implies that
basal tractions under Hawaii resist plate motion rather than drive it, as it indicates the relative motion between
lithosphere and underlying mantle.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0787ef3e-3018-4dfe-9964-28540d95df1d"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Analysis of computerized images, such as those constructed during this research, will hopefully aid in the verification of
current conceptual, numerical, and analytical models that attempt to explain and predict fracture growth in compression.  The
authors are currently not aware of existing techniques to provide direct visualization of fracture networks in
(non-transparent) rock prior to ultimate failure.  While a variety of excellent indirect methods exist for fracture
visualization (e.g., acoustic emissions, CAT-scans, and fracture mapping after ultimate failure), this work is an attempt to
use perhaps the most robust approach, which is rather direct and simple (at least, conceptually), yet allows new insight into
the mechanisms, behavior, and growth of brittle fracture in rock in compression.
The results for Granitic Gneiss samples are presented herein.  The samples used for this study were clearly lineated and had
grain sizes up to a few millimeters.  The angles between the planes of lineation and the sample axes were 70-80 degrees.
Cylindrical samples, nominally 47.5 mm in diameter and 115 mm tall, were subjected to unconfined uniaxial loading to
near-failure conditions.  Using strain-controlled loading, tests were stopped prior to any visible, external failure of the
specimens.  At the completion of the compressive tests, samples were coated with clear epoxy and sliced into 3-mm thick
discs.  Each disc was then coated with epoxy and polished.  A digital imaging technique was developed to produce a
three-dimensional computerized replication of the fracture network that evolved in the rock specimen during compressive
loading.  This imaging technique was applied to each of the polished discs in order to map fractures at various points
throughout the specimen.  When the fracture maps for each disc were combined, a three-dimensional digital replication of the
fracture structure for the entire sample was produced.
Analysis of the digital replications of the tested samples indicated that the developed fracture networks typically consisted
of one major fracture spawning many other smaller cracks.  Fracture propagation was generally observed to be parallel to the
sample lineation and the loading direction (i.e., the sample axis).  Further, the roughness of the fractures had a recurring
pattern with a wavelength of approximately 10 mm.  These results provide an insight into possible fracture mechanisms and
branching in compression.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0788861d-9c7e-4f11-abd6-0ec8d1ecabc1"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Data from a realistic ocean GCM forced with observed winds
are analyzed to examine the structure of the Southern
Oscillation. Mean and perturbed wind, zonal current and
temperature fields are studied with a special emphasis on
the propagation of subsurface anomalies. The main focus of
the study is the differences between distinct El Ni\~{n}o events,
including changes in the properties of the Southern
Oscillation since the late 1970&#39;s.
Each El Ni\~{n}o event in the model displays the characteristic
eastward propagation of the subsurface temperature anomalies
associated with the vertical motion of the thermocline.
However, the manifestation of this signal at the surface - important
for ocean-atmosphere interactions - appears to be different before and after the 1980&#39;s. A typical warm subsurface anomaly in
the 1960&#39;s and 1970&#39;s was localized vertically near the thermocline and did not
affect the surface before reaching the eastern Pacific.
In contrast, subsurface anomalies in the 1980&#39;s and 1990&#39;s
had a significantly larger vertical extent, so that their
effect on the surface was felt already in the central
Pacific. As a result, an SST anomaly typically had a westward
phase propagation along the equator in the earlier period,
and a slightly eastward propagation afterwards.
The differences in the properties of El Ni\~{n}o may be related
to decadal changes of the background state of the tropical
ocean-atmosphere system, particularly to the gradual
relaxation of the easterly winds along the equator since the
1970&#39;s, and the associated warming of sea surface
temperatures in the eastern Pacific ocean - a result
consistent with earlier work of Fedorov and Philander (2000).
Other factors that are likely to contribute significantly
to the difference between one El Ni\~{n}o event and the next
are the influence of westerly wind bursts, and the asymmetry
in duration and intensity of the successive El Ni\~{n}o and La
Ni\~{n}a phases of the Southern Oscillation.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/07d7a20e-daa3-4dea-a31e-a4868674daa9"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Kimberlites, K-rich ultramafic rocks are too enriched in incompatible trace elements (ITE) to represent low percent melts of
asthenospheric or depleted lithospheric mantle. They are thus interpreted to form by melting of strongly enriched carbonated
peridotite in the upper mantle or the mantle transition zone. However, diamond inclusions (DI&#39;s) from lower mantle (LM) show
that some kimberlites are derived from lower mantle sources.
Melting of LM Ca perovskite (CaSiPvk - the main reservoir of ITE), MgSiPvk and Mg wustite (MW) would yield liquids with
highly variable ITE contents, depending on the mode and melting sequence.  CaSiPvk DI&#39;s from S\~{a}o  Luiz exhibit variable
and generally high ITE $^{1}$. ITE budget of LM can be calculated from S\~{a}o Luiz DI data, assuming pyrolite LM with 69\%
MgSiPvk, 22\% MW and 9\% CaSiPvk. Even using this conservative estimate of CaSiPvk abundance, LM shows significant ITE
enrichment (Sr-Y 3$\times$; LREEs 10$\times$; HREEs 2-5$\times$; Pb 4$\times$) relative to primitive mantle (PM).  S\~{a}o
Luiz CaSiPvk is also strongly depleted in Th and U relative to PM, indicating the existence of additional LM phase
(baddeleyite?). Thus if kimberlites are LM melts, the melting assemblage would involve CaSiPvk (enriched by earlier melt
extraction) and MW (source of Mg-Cr-Ni) and a Th-U bearing phase. In this model, observed chemical and isotopic differences
in kimberlites reflect variation in degree of melting of a modally variable LM source.
To evaluate this model, an extensive new ICPMS trace element data-base for aphanitic hypabyssal facies kimberlites from
different tectonic environments has been developed. Kimberlites have variable ITE enrichment, with carbonate-rich kimberlites
showing the highest levels. Degree of enrichment appears to be coupled to the degree of fractionation of elements with
similar compatibilities.  HFSE pairs Zr-Nb and Nb-Ta are more strongly fractionated in kimberlites than in any other mantle
melts (Nb/Ta ranging from 9.26 to 26.4: Zr/Nb ranging from 0.15 to 4.56). Furthermore, Y/Ho ratios of kimberlites and
carbonatites (23.1$\pm$2.1) and CaSiPvk (20.8$\pm$0.6) are non-chondritic. Y fractionation appears to be correlated with
Ce/Pb and Ce/Sr.  Fractionation of these elements mainly reflects carbonate partitioning. Ta/U (pmn) and Nb/Th (pmn) ratios
for most kimberlite are 1.52$\pm$0.76 and 1.53$\pm$0.73. It is significant that ITE depleted diamondiferous kimberlites from
Kamchatka show the effect of contamination from the mantle wedge; viz., Ta/U (pmn) = 0.04$\pm$0.01, Nb/Th (pmn) =
0.09$\pm$0.01 Ce/Pb = 8.85$\pm$5.36 and have chondritic Y/Ho = 26.4$\pm$0.25.
Carbonate partitioning has previously been interpreted to be a shallow mantle feature, but recent primordial noble gas and N
isotopic data suggests that carbonate (magnesite?) plays an important role in the LM.  Further strong support for a LM source
of kimberlite is also given by the occurrence of CaSiPvk, MgSiPvk, ferropericlase-enstatite, MW and majorite DI&#39;s, and by
the recent discovery of diamondiferous majorite-bearing xenoliths. The relationship between emplacement of kimberlites and
plume magmatism, for which there is tomographic and Os-He isotopic evidence, suggests that kimberlite (and carbonatite
magmas) both form by thermal plume induced melting in the LM. Chemical variability within and between individual kimberlite
provinces is interpreted to reflect changes in LM melting assemblage and variable upper mantle contamination. Kimberlite
Sr-Nd-Pb isotopic data do not support an enrichment signature inherited from sediment subduction or from an early magma
ocean.
$^{1}$Hutchison, M.T. (1997) PhD Thesis Univ. Edinburgh.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/07e0831b-d82e-4b09-b1cd-0aa9b6eeb88a"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Between 1991 and 1996, carbon measurements were made on twenty-four WOCE/JGOFS/OACES survey cruises in the Pacific Ocean.
This combined database will be used to determine the distribution and inventory of carbon parameters in the Pacific Ocean,
and will enhance our understanding of the ocean carbon cycle, including estimates of the anthropogenic CO2 uptake by the
oceans.
Of the four measurable carbon parameters, (dissolved inorganic carbon (DIC), total alkalinity (TAlk), fugacity of CO2 (fCO2),
and pH), DIC was measured on all the cruises, and TAlk was the second most measured.  We examined the data where cruises
overlapped using several independent techniques, and have proposed adjustments that will result in a consistent, unified
dataset. Based on our results, we recommend DIC adjustments for P16N, P17N, and P2, and TAlk adjustments for P8S, P17C, P17N,
P2 and P31.  Details of our proposed adjustments are posted at http://cdiac.esd.ornl.gov/oceans/glodap/index.html.
This CO2 dataset represents the most comprehensive high-precision survey of carbon distributions in the Pacific Ocean since
the GEOSECS expedition, and provides an order of magnitude improvement in the quality and quantity of Pacific DIC and Talk
data.  With the proposed adjustments, DIC and TAlk have an estimated overall accuracy of ~3 and 5 umol kg-1 respectively.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/07e23d42-920d-4024-9345-980ef1d85375"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Woodlark Basin in the SW Pacific is a result of the rifting of
continental lithosphere of the Papuan Peninsula and the westward
propagation of seafloor spreading. It is conjectured that during rifting,
low-angle normal faulting can accommodate large amounts of extensional
strain. Although this hypothesis violates classical theories of frictional
slip on faults, focal mechanisms for a number of moderate-sized earthquakes
in the area do permit normal slip on planes dipping at low-angles (25-35
degrees). One of these earthquakes is located on or near a fault plane that
has been imaged by seismic reflection data as a shallow north-dipping fault continuous
with the northern flank of the continental Moresby Seamount. To investigate
further the dynamics in the transition region between continental rifting
and seafloor spreading, and to help resolve the low-angle normal faulting
paradox, an onshore/offshore seismic experiment was conducted in 1999-2000
utilizing both passive and active sources. As a part of this experiment, an
array of 20 OBS/Hs were deployed over an area of 30 km X 30 km just north
of the Moresby Seamount and west of the spreading tip. This array recorded
microseismicity for 6 months, but at the beginning of the deployment also
recorded airgun blasts from an MCS survey. We present the results of a
tomographic inversion of first arrival traveltimes from the airgun blasts
recorded at the 14 functioning OBS/Hs. Our results represent the first
detailed look at 3-D crustal velocity variations in the area. The data
constrain velocities within a 80 km X 80 km region to maximum depths of ~12
km, although resolution degrades outside of the OBS/H array. A narrow
high-velocity region at the SE corner of the array marks the westernmost
spreading segment. Low velocities are associated with the fault-bounded
rift basin north of Moresby Seamount. Further north, an ophiolitic crustal
slice at least 10 km thick and with velocities up to 7.5 km/s dips
10-15 degrees north. There is little E-W velocity variation except in the
poorly resolved region west of the array, where mantle-type velocities are
present at 8 km depth, and beneath Moresby Seamount, where 7 km/s
velocities occur only 3 km below sea level. Future work will focus on
analyzing the microseismicity: locating the earthquakes within the 3-D
velocity model to illuminate active structures, determining fault
planes and focal mechanisms, and thereby deducing if active low-angle
normal faulting is indeed occurring in the Woodlark Basin.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/07f4167d-5673-45fc-a43f-2c2539fa196c"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Records of skeletal $\delta^{18}$O and Sr/Ca ratios from a Bermuda coral ({ \it Diploria labyrinthiformis}) were used to
reconstruct the variability of sea surface temperatures (SSTs) for the time period 1520-1603 ( $\pm$ 15 years) A.D. within
the Little Ice Age (LIA). The temporal resolution of the proxy records is approximately monthly for $\delta^{18}$O and the
most recent decades of Sr/Ca ratios. The reconstructed interannual temperature variability is the same as today (1 standard
deviation=0.4 $\deg$C), but phases of anomalous SST occurred more frequently during the LIA. Singular spectrum analysis
indicates three oscillatory modes with periods of $\sim$30, 16, and 7.8 years, which contribute 14, 8, and 6\% to the
interannual time-series variance, respectively. Only the last periodicity is evident in modern instrumental SSTs and reflects
the persistent influence of the North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO). Seasonally, the cycle of coral skeletal Sr/Ca ratios leads
the cycle of $\delta^{18}$O by one month. In contrast, there is no clear phase shift in two modern Bermuda corals from the
same location. Since the potential influence of salinity on the coral $\delta^{18}$O is small compared with temperature, a
possible explanation is a seasonal cycle in surface water Sr/Ca ratios due to depletion in Sr during summer and advection of
Sr-enriched deeper water in winter.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0802c784-cfcb-4614-97ed-fc343b29e5c8"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We report observations of neutral atoms from the solar wind in the Earth&#39;s vicinity made by the Low Energy Neutral Atom
(LENA) imager on the IMAGE spacecraft. This instrument, unlike most other neutral atom imagers, was specifically designed to
be capable of looking at and in the direction of the sun. We deduce that observed enhancements in the hydrogen count rate in
about the solar direction are due to neutral atoms from the solar wind. Based on the presence of sputtered oxygen observed in
the tof spectra, the energy of these neutral particles exceeds $\sim$1~keV, consistent with solar wind-like energies. In
addition, the sputtered oxygen abundance tracks the solar wind speed even when IMAGE resides deep inside the magnetosphere.
The enhancements are correlated with neither solar ultraviolet emission nor suprathermal particles. We consider various
sources of these neutral particles including solar wind charge exchange with dust grains and interstellar neutrals,
undisturbed solar wind and magnetosheath charge exchange with the Earth&#39;s geocorona and solar wind charge exchange with the
Earth&#39;s geocorona in the cusp region.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08151095-c887-4442-a5a0-4d52fb0e7989"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Using up to nine years of data from a global network of Global Positioning System (GPS)
stations, including 11 sites well distributed across the Pacific Plate, we derive
present-day Euler vectors for the Pacific Plate more precisely than has previously been
possible from space geodetic data.
The fit of the 11 Pacific Plate (PA) site velocities to the best fitting PA Euler vector
is significantly worse than the fit of 11 Australian Plate (AU) site velocities to the AU Euler vector
or 8 North American Plate (NA) sites to the NA Euler vector.
If three southernmost Pacific sites,
all located on the submerged continental crust south and east of New Zealand, are omitted, the fit improves
significantly to about the level of the fits to the other plates.  We show that the
International GPS Service site at Chatham Island (CHAT) is moving 2-3 $\pm$ 1 mm/yr relative to the rigid interior
of the Pacific Plate.
Based on the velocity of this and other nearby stations we
consider the possibility that sites located on the
New Zealand continental shelf define a ``Chatham Block&#39;&#39; moving independently of the Pacific Plate,
but there is neither sufficient resolution in our data to confirm this suggestion nor any
independent evidence in support of it.
Our best-fitting PA/AU relative Euler vector is located significantly southeast of the NUVEL-1A
pole but very close ($\sim$1$\deg$ in position and $\sim$2\% in rate) to a pole derived from transform
faults identified from satellite altimetry, suggesting that the vector has changed insignificantly over
the past 3 Ma.  Our relative Euler vector is also consistent with all known geological and
geodetic evidence concerning the AU/PA plate boundary through New Zealand.
The GPS sites offshore of central and southern California are presently moving 3-6 $\pm$ 1 mm/yr
relative to predicted Pacific
velocity, with their residual velocities in the opposite direction to PA/NA relative motion.
Likewise, the easternmost sites in South Island, New Zealand, are
moving $\sim$6 $\pm$ 1 mm/yr relative to predicted Pacific velocity
(or $\sim$3 $\pm$ 1 mm/yr relative to the possible Chatham Block),
with their residuals in the opposite direction to PA/AU relative motion.
The velocity residuals are
in the same sense as predicted by elastic strain accumulation on known plate boundary faults,
but of a significantly higher magnitude in both Southern California and New Zealand.
Because the velocity residuals off California are nearly orthogonal to those off New Zealand
there is no rotation of the Pacific Plate that can significantly reduce both sets of residuals,
implying that the plate boundary zones in both regions are
wider than previously believed.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08188784-a916-4a90-bdf4-8480a765d56c"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The present study is an attempt to characterize the seismic sources of the Ometepec, Guerrero earthquakes in southern Mexico,
a region of high seismic potential. Earthquakes in this region frequently occur by pairs (called {\it doublet} in
Seismology). Earthquake doublets appearance in other regions has been related to the presence of isolated large asperities
that is, zones of high stress concentration on the fault plane. In the present paper we propose a relation of Ometepec,
Guerrero doublets generation to local geological feature which has not been proposed before. The aftershock sequence
following the major earthquake doublet of June 7, 1982 is used in the discussion. The spatial distribution of 113 aftershocks
suggests the presence of a Cocos subducting plate disruption at the Ometepec, Guerrero area which may be causally related to
the doublet genesis.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0824ec8f-7224-4e44-906e-220561684075"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Based on repeat ground track elevation comparisons, a new methodology for calculating ice sheet elevation changes is being
developed. Altimeter satellites maintain  repeat tracks for large portions of their missions. The repeat tracks are kept
within a specified distance from a reference orbit (nominally 1 - 2 km). If the cross-track slopes are known, all elevations
measured along repeat ground tracks can be compared by geolocating them to the reference ground track. Geolocation requires
correcting the elevation for cross-track and along-track misalignment with a pre-determined reference orbit. Along-track
alignment can be done by linearly interpolating between two consecutively measured elevations. Two methods have been used to
determine the cross-track slopes. The first one is to use a Digital Elevation Model (DEM). The second one is to align the
data along the track and then fit a straight line to the elevations in the cross-track direction. These algorithms were
tested using ERS2 data from May 1995 to March 1999 over a small region of Greenland.  The results were compared to elevation
changes calculated by crossover analysis. The methodology will be very useful at the lower latitudes where the crossovers are
sparse and for ICESAT/GLAS data where the altimeter footprint will be maintained to within 30 m of the reference track over
the ice sheets.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/082bfbe3-464a-41d1-b0e4-4bb87f987195"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Black Butte (BB) is a dacitic dome complex located 10 km SW of Mt. Shasta, Ca.  The dacite domes of Shastina (SH), a cone on
the main Shasta edifice, erupted contemporaneously with the BB extrusions 9-10 kbp.  Major and trace element analyses of
fused powders of BB and SH indicate the rocks are compositionally identical and were almost certainly derived from a common
parent magma.  Despite their spatial proximity, eruptive simultaneity, and compositional affinity, the two lavas exhibit
significant phase assemblage differences thought to be the result of variations in pre-eruptive storage or transport
conditions.  The BB phase assemblage consists of plagioclase (plag), hornblende (hb), and Fe-oxide phenocrysts in a high-Si
microcrystalline (50-100 um) groundmass.  The SH phase assemblage contains the above phases with the addition of both clino-
and orthopyroxene (cpx, opx) in a similar groundmass.  Plag phenocrysts in both lavas have homogeneous An$_{75-80}$ cores
with 10-20 um An$_{60}$ rims.  Hb phenocrysts are unzoned, with average Mg \#&#39;s of 58.5 (BB) and 69.5 (SH).  SH cpxs are
essentially unzoned Wo$_{44}$En$_{48}$Fs$_{8}$, while two populations of opx exist: zoned phenocrysts
(Wo$_{2}$En$_{81}$Fs$_{17}$ cores, Wo$_{2}$En$_{72}$Fs$_{26}$ rims) and unzoned microphenocrysts identical in composition to
the phenocryst rims.
Phase equilibrium experiments performed on a natural BB sample over a range in pressure (1.1-2.5 kbar) and temperature
(825-950C) at fO2=NNO constrain the pressure of both the BB and SH magma storage regions to $andlt;$2 kbar.  Testing and
additional calibration of the Holland and Blundy [1994] hb-plag thermometer yields crystallization temperatures of 860-885
and 833-858C for BB phenocryst core and rim compositions, respectively.  SH pyroclastic debris indicates markedly lower core
and rim temperatures of 800 and 782-798C, respectively.  Two-pyroxene thermometry [Wood and Banno, 1973; Wells, 1977] was
applied to one SH sample which contained cpx, but not hb, returning temperatures of 994 (core) and 1016C (rim).  This data
suggests that the SH storage region was temperature stratified.  In contrast, the BB melt appears to have experienced near
constant temperature until ascent.  In the absence of variations in ascent rate or storage pressure, preliminary evidence
indicates melt water content, storage temperature, or degree of crystallization are likely responsible for the observed phase
assemblage differences.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/082d90bd-7fc1-48c4-99be-976c4fbb10cf"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">As part of the ONR/NASA-sponsored study of the circulation and the wind-forcing of the Japan-East Sea, MINIMET drifters were
deployed to measure mixed layer currents and surface winds. Wind speed was determined from the acoustic power recoreded in
the 0.5-2.0 KHz band ambient sound signal from a hydrophone at 10m depth. Wind direction was determined from conditional
sampling of a small vane on the drifter float. The MINIMET observations compare well with the NASA QuikSCAT scatterometer
data (rms differences of +/- 1.5 m/sec; 15 deg). A model was developed for the local wind-driven currents as a function of
the MINIMET observed winds and was subtracted from the total current observations. North of the sub-polar front, the time
dependent upper ocean currents were largely locally wind driven, with occasional long lived (70-210 day  residence) warm
eddies that spin off the warm water sphere. The geostrophic eddy energy was compared to eddy energy estimated from combined
ERS/TOPEX/Poseidon altimeter data in which both the patterns and amplitudes agreed, provided the altimeter energies were
increased by a nearly a factor of two. Drifter tracks indicated that the South-East Korean Warm Current reversed seasonally.
No eastward branching of this current was observed, beyond the drift that was caused by the influence of a random eddy field.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08310628-6484-4ace-927f-1cc0a0574e18"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">\\As plants close their stomata, intercellular pCO$_{2}$ decreases, enriching intercellular CO$_{2}$ in $^{13}$C; thus high
\delta$^{13}$C values are a sign of water stress.  Stomatal closure can occur in plants growing in high salinity levels;
although adapted to high salinity, mangroves show this same response to salt stress.  One source of salt stress for
terrestrial plants is proximity to the ocean.
\\\Palynological evidence suggests that the buttonwood ({\it Conocarpus erectus}) evolved from a common ancestor of white
mangrove ({\it Laguncularia racemosa}) during the Quaternary (Graham 1995), implying that differences between the
\delta$^{13}$C values of these trees reflect different environmental conditions rather than genetic effects. We determined
\delta$^{13}$C in stem and leaf material from these two species of the tree family {\it Combretaceae}.  11 individuals of
white mangrove and 11 individuals of buttonwood were sampled on two separate excursions to the Florida Keys during Spring and
Fall of 1999. The white mangrove trees were growing submerged in the brackish swamp, while the buttonwood trees were
terrestrial, and were sampled along a transect 0 to 160 meters away from the water&#39;s edge.  Water potential of the buttonwood
stems ranges from -1.99 MPa up to -1.05 MPa, and is only weakly correlated with absolute distance from shore.  At large
distances from shore (e.g., 80 m), the variability in water potential is maximal  (=1 MPa) indicating that with progression
onto land, water availability becomes more &quot;patchy.&quot;  Stem and leaf samples were both collected at four locations on each of
the 22 trees during Spring and Fall.  Regarding the total data set (n = 88), \delta$^{13}$C values for white mangrove stem
show a mean value of -25.9 \permil (range = -26.4 to -25.3 \permil ).  In contrast, buttonwood stem values showed a larger
range (-27.9 to -24.1 \permil ) with a mean value of -26.2 \permil .  \delta$^{13}$C values for white mangrove leaf show a
mean value of -26.6 \permil  (range = -26.8 to -24.2 \permil ) while buttonwood leaf showed a mean value of -27.2 \permil
(range = -28.9 to -25.5 \permil ).  We observed a negative correlation between buttonwood stem \delta$^{13}$C and distance to
the ocean&#39;s edge (y = -24.6 - 0.02x; R$^{2}$ = 0.7; n = 10) as well as between buttonwood leaf \delta$^{13}$C and distance
to shore (y = -26.2 - 0.01x; R$^{2}$ = 0.5; n = 10).   Over a distance of 160 meters, total variability in \delta$^{13}$C of
buttonwood stem tissue increases systematically from 0.4 \permil  up to 2.6 \permil , while total variability in leaf tissue
remains unchanged.  The total variability in \delta$^{13}$C value of white mangrove stem (averaged across all individuals)
was = 0.69 \permil , of leaf was = 0.34 \permil  and did not change significantly over the course of the growing season.  In
contrast, the total variability in \delta$^{13}$C value of buttonwood stem was = 0.88 \permil  in the Spring and = 1.32
\permil  in the Fall, while for leaves, total variability was = 0.88 \permil  in the Spring and = 0.50 \permil  in the Fall.
This indicates that over the course of the growing season, buttonwood leaves became more homogeneous, while stems became
more heterogeneous, with respect to \delta$^{13}$C value.  Variability associated with immature vs. mature status of tissues
was smaller in all cases than total variability within individual tree.
\\\We will use these observations of modern mangrove communities to reconstruct salt stress in paleocommunities.  Future work
involves mangrove fossil isolation from the upper Miocene Paraje Solo Formation of Veracruz, Mexico.
\\\Graham, A. (1995). {\bf Biotropica}  27: 20-27.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/083f9210-2718-412a-ad9b-7cb9e8047c8e"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">An understanding of the transport behavior of dense non-aqueous phase organic liquids (DNAPLs) in the subsurface is a
prerequisite for the accurate assessment of chemical exposures and the design of effective remediation strategies.  Although
a number of studies have investigated the effects of physical heterogeneity on the fate of DNAPLs, few studies have been
undertaken to explore the influence of wettability variations on contaminant behavior.  Results of on-going experimental
studies are presented that are designed to quantify multiphase flow properties and residual DNAPL saturations in fractionally
wet media; to explore the use of column scale data to simulate behavior in larger scale heterogeneous systems; and to
develop improved modeling approaches for DNAPL migration in heterogeneous media.
For these experiments, tetrachloroethylene (PCE) was selected as the model DNAPL.  Organic wet sands were created by treating
various Ottawa sand size fractions with octadecyltrichlorosilane (OTS).  These OTS treated sands were then mixed with
untreated sands in different mass proportions to obtain fractionally wet media.  Two-phase (water-organic) capillary
pressure-saturation relationships and organic residual saturations were measured for several different organic wet sand size
fractions using an apparatus similar to a standard Tempe pressure cell.  A similar apparatus was also employed to conduct
two-phase outflow experiments.  Column outflow data were then used, in conjunction with a one-dimensional numerical simulator
and a coupled non-linear parameter estimation algorithm, to estimate relative permeability relationships for the
fractionally wet soils.
A two-dimensional sand tank experiment was then undertaken to examine multiphase flow behavior and model predictive
capability in mixed wettability systems.  Within the tank (30.6 cm $\times$ 38.4 cm $\times$ 1.7 cm), organic wet lenses of
various size fractions were embedded in a coarser matrix of water-wet sand.  PCE was released at a known rate and permitted
to migrate within the heterogeneous medium.  The column-measured constitutive relationships were employed in a
two-dimensional multiphase flow code to simulate the PCE migration and entrapment.  Visual observations of PCE migration in
the sand tank revealed that fractional wettability can have a dramatic effect on DNAPL behavior.  The P$_{c}$-S data, when
coupled with a conceptual relative permeability model, were found to adequately model the observed DNAPL behavior, indicating
that the column-scale measurements scale well to heterogeneous bench scale situations.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0846d880-4545-49cb-b423-1ac4fcb241cb"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We present an extension to the catchment average soil moisture monitoring (CASMM) concept of Grayson and Western (1998,
J.Hydrol. 207, 68-82) in which the analysis of a series of surface soil moisture data sets indicated that certain locations
within a catchment consistently exhibit average moisture conditions.  While this early analysis was encouraging, it did not
indicate whether such locations could be determined a priori.
Further field studies were undertaken on pasture sites within the 50km$^{2}$ Mahurangi catchment of northern New Zealand in
1998 and 1999.  This work forms one part of a comprehensive field hydrology study known as MARVEX.  Additional field sites
were monitored in south eastern Australia.
Spatial measurement of soil moisture in the Mahurangi catchment has shown a more variable distribution through the year than
was encountered at other locations.  This variability makes the a priori selection of points with a moisture content
representative of the catchment difficult.  We demonstrate how the idea of a &quot;CASMM point&quot; can be extended to &quot;representative
landscape or CASMM zone&quot;.  Landscape zones may be delineated via DEM, as hillslopes of varying aspect, or by slope.
Our pasture field sites differ in climate and in, soil and topographic composition, yet representative landscape units within
these sites can be defined and offer a convenient method of monitoring in the future.  There are differences in the
characteristics of the &quot;CASMM zones&quot; between the sites but these can be explained by differences on soils, topography and
climate.
This finding indicates that given basic information about a catchment (including a DEM, soil type, climate type, and
vegetation) it should be possible to determine optimal locations for soil moisture instruments that provide measurements of
catchment average conditions and can be interpreted in a spatial context.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0859f093-ec9f-4fae-a0ae-87869b4c2630"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A convincing body of evidence shows that fluids play an important, if
not dominant, role in the earthquake process. However, most models
ignore the role of fluids because the range of timescales bewteen
diffusion processes and wave propagation processes are computationally
restrictive. The dominant mechanical effects of fluids within fault
zones can be included if the permeability is approximated by a
co-seismic switch from low to high permeability. This simplification
approximates the fault valve model for earthquakes, and is supported
by substantial evidence of fault zone hydraulics. This switch provides
a strong non-linearity with very important conequences on the fault
zone stress state, rupture properties, and long-term fault strength.
The most important effect of treating the dynamical aspect of fault
zone pore pressure within a deforming elastic solid is that it
provides an additional degree of freedom in stress space. This
additional degree of freedom ensures a high degree of stress state
disorder, and a continuous self-organization of zones of incipient
failure along the fault plane. We present model results of stress state
evolution along a vertical strike-slip fault, and along faults in a coupled blind-thrust/strike slip
fault system.  We show that the stress state approaches an attractor,
resulting in &#39;effective&#39; properties of the fault system over long-time
scales. The attractor is controlled by the ratio of pore pressure
buildup to plate velocity. The fault zone pore pressure state is
another mechanism for establishing long-range correlations because of the co-seismic linking of isolated hydraulic networks.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08691b31-b31e-4e06-8c58-8e0724849c0d"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The exothermic reaction of atomic hydrogen with ozone in the mesosphere produces vibrationally excited hydroxyl radicals,
primarily in the $\upsilon$ = 6-9 levels.  Collision of OH($\upsilon$) with other atmospheric constituents results in
vibration-to-translation energy transfer that significantly influences the heat budget in the 80 to 100 km altitude range.
The radiative decay of OH($\upsilon$) dominates the visible and infrared nightglow in this region, and thus provides a window
into the chemical energy deposition rates.
The SABER (Sounding of the Atmosphere using Broadband Emission Radiometry) experiment on NASA&#39;s TIMED
(Thermosphere-Ionosphere-Mesosphere Energetics and Dynamics) mission will measure Meinel band emission from the $\upsilon$ =
7, 8, and 9 vibrational levels of OH.  Atmospheric chemistry and radiation models will then be used to calculate energy
release and hydrogen atom concentration profiles.  The accuracy of this approach depends sensitively on the kinetic rates for
collisional removal of OH($\upsilon$ = 7-9).  Mlynczak et al.$^1$ have laid out the accuracy requirements: total loss rate
coefficients accurate to 15\% and removal rate constants for collisions with oxygen atoms accurate to better than 50\%.
Our experimental approach is inspired by the work of Greenblatt and Wiesenfeld$^2$ who photodissociated a small fraction of
ozone in an O$_3$/H$_2$/He mixture to determine the ozone removal rate of OH($\upsilon$ = 9) by monitoring the temporal
evolution of the $\Delta \upsilon$ = 6 Meinel band emission.  We use a pulsed excimer laser at 248 nm to photodissociate
ozone in an O$_3$/H$_2$/N$_2$ mixture and probe the transient population of specific vibrationally excited OH states using
laser induced fluorescence spectroscopy.  By adjusting the composition of the O$_3$/H$_2$/N$_2$ mixture and by varying the
248 nm laser fluence to control the ozone dissociation fraction, the dominant relaxation partner can be varied systematically
from ozone to atomic oxygen.  Model calculations of the chemical evolution in the photolyzed gas mixture will be presented,
along with results of preliminary experiments.
This work is supported by the Aeronomy Program of the National Science Foundation.
$^1$Mlynczak, M. G., D., K., Zhou, and S. M. Alder-Golden, {\it Geophys. Res. Lett.}, {\bf 25}, 647-650, 1998.
$^2$Greenblatt, G. D., and J. R. Wiesenfeld, { \it J. Geophys. Res.}, {\bf 87}, 11145-11152, 1982.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/086eedf0-a9e2-4c37-b7fa-0b826ed12600"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A manual calibration procedure for the distributed hydrological model HYDROTEL [1] was developed.  The proposed calibration
procedure which is based on a systematic adjustment of model parameters ordered in specific groups represents a major
improvement over the traditional trial and error approach.
The grouping of parameters reflects modelled hydrological processes (i.e., governing equations) and well defined calibration
targets. Five targets, associated with five distinct groups of parameters, were defined.  They are:  (1) drought recession
behaviors, (2) seasonal volumes, (3) summer and fall floods, (4) spring floods, and (5) peak flow synchronization.
Calibration of the five groups of parameters is done sequentially.  Since changes on a particular group of parameters can
slightly affect more than one calibration target, some parameter values may need to be readjusted all along the calibration
exercise.  To guide these adjustments, analytical and/or empirical relations between model parameters and target values were
developed.
The proposed calibration procedure approach which has been applied successfully to a 2000-km2 watershed in Quebec, Canada,
will serve as the basic frame for the development of an automated calibration of HYDROTEL.
[1]  Fortin, J.-P., R. Turcotte, S. Massicotte, R. Moussa, and J. Fitzback.  2000.  A distributed watershed model compatible
with remote sensing and GIS data, part 1:  description of the model.  Accepted for publication in:  J. Hydrol. Eng., ASCE.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0872dbd2-93bd-4c49-bbe4-0cef2fdf11ee"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">There is a considerable debate about the mechanism of denitrification and the atmospheric conditions that support it.  Here
we report data from the January 2000 deployment of SOLVE in an attempt to understand denitrification.  During the SOLVE
mission, the Multiangle Aerosol Spectrometer Probe (MASP) was flown aboard the ER-2 sampling the Arctic polar vortex. On
several of these flights, the ER-2 flew directly through PSCs.  We will present the changes in aerosol size distributions
determined by the MASP as a function of altitude during the flights on January 20, 2000 and February 3, 2000.  For these
flights, we observed three size modes,  (andlt;1 um, 1-2 um, and andgt;2 um diameters), with the mass being dominated by the largest
particles only at the lowest altitude.  The MASP size distributions will be used in conjunction with a one-dimensional model
run along trajectories to probe the mechanism of particle growth and denitrification.   Based on the size distributions, we
may be able to identify what type of particles are responsible for denitrification.  Specifically, we want to determine if
nitric acid trihydrate is solely responsible for denitrification or if supercooled ternary solutions or nitric acid dihydrate
are also present.   Finally, comparing the data with the model may lead to some understanding of the nucleation of solids in
the atmosphere.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0889565c-9970-4a76-8cf7-7e0b1f2c3bc4"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">An assessment was done of the capability of a regional climate model (RegCM) into simulate cold season precipitation and
snow water equivalence (SWE) in the western United States by comparing model simulations with observations. The observed data
were gridded from high-elevation SNOTEL measurements and lower elevation rainfall measurements.  This work has advanced
previous studies of the possible use of regional climate modeling for water resources applications in three ways: i) the
analysis was conduced over a longer period, ii) the RegCM employed a finer resolution, and iii) the SWE data were gridded for
quantitative comparison. The analysis focused on the 1990-1995 winter seasons (January through March). For comparison with
the model results, the observed total precipitation for a ï¿½ degree grid cell was calculated by using weighted sum of the
SNOTEL and NCDC precipitation data, and then the combined product bilinearly interpolated to the 36-km regional model grid.
The weighted sum method reduced bias for evaluating the model-simulated precipitation at high elevation.  However, there is
a significant sensitivity to the method used for combing high and low elevation station data.  In order to explore the
accuracy of the method, geostatistically interpolated spatial SWE for the Western US at a 1 km grid for the study period was
introduced, which provided a valuable resource for qualitative verification of the model snow process.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/089b557b-7ff6-42b3-8f11-6ccb4a844de6"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The Hector Mine October 16, 1999 (Mw=7.1) and Landers June 28, 1992 (Mw=7.3) earthquakes occurred close to each other both in
time and space.  Both the Landers and Hector Mine ruptures took advantage of multiple faults and triggered deformation over
a wide area.  Gravity and aeromagnetic data reveal that the aftershocks of the two temblors bracket a high-density, magnetic
body lying between the two ruptures.  The aftershocks avoid the interior of the body.  Two- and three-dimensional modeling of
the potential-field anomalies indicates that the source, here named the Emerson Lake body (ELB), extends to a depth of
approximately 15 km.  Two-dimensional gravity models indicate that the ELB has a density of about 2.76 g/cc.  The ELB most
likely is composed of Jurassic hornblende diorite; exposures of these rocks coincide with the apex of both gravity and
magnetic anomalies west of Emerson Lake.
Seismicity before the Landers earthquake also tended to avoid the ELB, suggesting that the ELB affects the distribution of
stress in this part of the Mojave Desert.  Even though the 1975 Galway Lake earthquakes (M5.4) overlap the body in plan view,
the earthquakes were shallow (less than 5 km) and tend to occur above the ELB.  The 1979 Homestead Valley earthquakes (M5.3)
were also shallow, apparently activating a set of conjugate faults.  The Homestead Valley aftershocks occur within an
embayment in the western margin of the ELB.
The ELB may help explain the changes in strike along both the Landers and Hector Mine ruptures and differences between the
Landers aftershock patterns north and south of the Pinto Mountain fault.  The Landers and Hector Mine mainshocks apparently
did not originate along the immediate margin of the ELB.  The Landers earthquake ruptured unilaterally to north (Wald and
others, 1992) and follows the western edge of the ELB.  As in the Hector Mine earthquake, both aftershocks and mapped surface
rupture change strike to parallel a margin of the ELB.  Even the east-west trending aftershock zones near the northern end
of the Landers rupture occur at the northern margin of the ELB.  The maximum amount of Landers slip occurred near the
step-over from the Homestead Valley to the Camp Rock/Emerson faults (Wald and Heaton, 1994), both of which clearly bound the
ELB.
Landers seismicity and surface rupture also extended south of the Pinto Mountain fault, a left-lateral fault with Holocene
displacement.  The southern aftershock zone is twice as wide as that to the north.  The ELB is present only north of the
Pinto Mountain fault and thus may contribute to the different character of the seismicity and faulting north and south of the
Pinto Mountain fault.  Slip may preferentially occur on faults at the margins of the ELB, rather than within the more mafic
and stronger ELB, resulting in fewer, but more clearly defined faults with perhaps greater cumulative slip north of the Pinto
Mountain fault.
Almost all Coulomb stress change calculations reported for the Landers earthquake assume an elastic half space with uniform
physical properties.  Such calculations indicate that the Landers event only mildly increased the static stress at the Hector
Mine hypocenter (e.g., Parsons and Dreger, 2000).  The correspondence of seismicity and potential-field anomalies strongly
argues that this mafic crustal heterogeneity may provide a spatial link between the Landers earthquake and the Hector Mine
hypocenter that might permit a more efficient transfer of stress either by stress concentration at its boundaries or by slip
on its bounding faults.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08a610db-f421-4d64-8229-6134c57a568e"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Sediment transport under current, waves, waves and current together, and under soliton forcing has been investigated through
velocity measurements from rigid structures on the bottom for a little more than 30 years, approximately the period spanning
the career of Joe Kravitz.  First in a wave-free deep-sea environment in HEBBLE, later in a wave-dominated shelf environment
in CODE and STRESS, and more recently in mixed wave and current plus soliton environments such as Stratiform and CMO, tripods
have supported current sensors and particle analyzers.  Electromagnetic and mechanical sensors have given way to acoustic
travel-time and Doppler velocity sensors and optical transmissometers have been augmented by light backscatter sensors and
optical particle sizers.  Four-meter tall tripods have grown to 8 meters and soon will reach 18 meters but small tripods in
clusters or isolated single rigid sensors in large arrays may change the future of observation of sediment transport under
bottom current and wave stress patterns in benthic storms.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08c7ebbf-fb62-4dee-b3e0-ec1f35607a09"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Density ($\rho$) and S-wave velocity (Vs) of the Earth&#39;s mantle are both related to temperature, composition and pressure. We
perform joint inversions of geodetic and seismological data for the scaling factor $\zeta$, which is defined as the ratio of
the relative density perturbations to the relative Vs-anomalies. Gravity and global Vs-anomalies are provided by the recent
models egm96 (Lemoine et al., 1996) and s16rlbm (Woodhouse and Trampert, 1995), respectively.
We have computed a whole range of possible values for $\zeta$. We introduced a-priori errors in the seismological model,
which leads to an estimate of the variance in $\zeta$. The gravity kernels account for possible radial viscosity variations
(Forte and Peltier, 1991). Calculations are made separetely for oceanic and continental regions, and data are filtered for
spherical harmonics degrees L=11 to L=16. The sub-continental and sub-oceanic models of $\zeta$ are significantly different:
$\zeta$ has positive values until z=220 km (z=140 km) below continents (oceans), with a typical value of 0.04. This value is
small compared to experimental mineralogy estimates. At depth greater than 350 km, $\zeta$ is not well-constrained.
We then used these results to constrain lateral variations of temperature (dT), mineralogy (dX), and iron-content (dFe) in
the shallow mantle. Accounting for anelasticity reconciles our inverted model of $\zeta$ and the mineralogical estimates.
Pure temperature variations do not explain low values of $\zeta$. Simultaneous variations of temperature (dT~$\pm$100 K) and
iron-content (dFe~$\pm$1\%) are consistent with our model of $\zeta$. Variations of the fraction of olivine has less
influence. Finally, the ocean-continent difference is discussed, using the inferred dT, dX and dFe.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08cb3a91-97f8-4d90-8ee6-beb7595f9174"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">For over a decade it has not been understood how Pc 1 waves generated
in the equatorial magnetosphere could be observed on the ground so
reliably.  We present solutions of the coupled full wave equations for
ion cyclotron waves propagating along auroral field lines and predict
Poynting fluxes, wave amplitudes, and polarizations along a field
aligned coordinate.  Our results suggest that even for significant
concentrations of oxygen and helium substantial wave power can reach
low altitudes due to mode conversion and tunneling.  Because the waves
reach ionospheric altitudes, they can contribute to ion heating and
conic formation.  In order to understand how much energy the waves can
provide to heavy ions, it is necessary to also include a proper
ionospheric model because significant ion cyclotron absorption occurs
for the reflected wave.  The wavelength of the waves is the order of
the ionospheric height so it is necessary to include the altitude
dependence of ion-neutral, electron-neutral, and electron-ion
collision frequencies as well as variation of densities for electrons
and ions in the E and F region.  The model includes $\rm H^+, He^+,
O^+$ and $\rm NO^+$.  Significant dissipation can occur in the
ionosphere due to collisions.  We compare the amount of wave power
dissipated by collisions in the ionosphere with that power absorbed by
ion cyclotron resonant heating.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08ef6d8a-90b8-4706-b422-ce56c944d206"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The existence of precariously balanced rocks within 20 kilometers of the San Andreas Fault might be explained solely by the
shallow shear-wave velocity structure under the rocks. We present amplitude spectra of LARSE-II blasts and Hector Mine
aftershocks, shallow shear velocities from microtremor analyses, and p-wave velocities from hammer refraction experiments
from ten locations near the Mojave
section of the San Andreas Fault. This section of the fault has ruptured numerous times in the last thousand years, most
recently in 1857. Three of the ten sites are locations of precarious or semi-precarious rocks. Amplitude spectra of the
horizontal components of seismograms recorded at the precarious-rock sites exhibit significant de-amplification relative to a
site at Mill Creek Summit (location of the USC strong ground-motion station MCS) in the 1 to 6 Hz frequency range.
Approximately 2/3 of the de-amplification observed in spectral ratios can be accounted for by velocity differences alone. The
precariously balanced rock sites have 30-meter-average shear velocities 2.5 to 3 times higher than at MCS.  Average 30-meter
shear velocities at precariously balanced rock sites range from approximately 1000 m/s to 1500 m/s. Velocity measurements
made at Mill Creek Summit, Newhall, and Palmdale, all previously noted as engineering class &quot;A&quot; rock sites ($andgt;$760 m/s
30-meter-average shear velocity), fall below the 760 m/s velocity criterion used in creating predictive ground-motion maps.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08f038ef-6b95-4bff-9bb5-90c4baee5f18"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">NOx (NO+NO$_{2}$) was measured at two heights above the snow surface continuously between Feb. 11 (DOY 42) and May 5 (DOY
126) as part of the Polar Sunrise Experiment (PSE) 2000 at Alert, Nunavut, using the MTU NOx/NOy analyzer.  The hourly
3$\sigma$ detection limits of this instrument were 1.3, 2.1, and 1.1 pmol/mol for NO, NO$_{2}$, and NOy, respectively.  A
constant NO artifact of 1.5 pmol/mol was subtracted from the data.
Median NO and NO$_{2}$ mixing ratios in background air during darkness were 0 and 7.5 pmol/mol, respectively.  During the
light period NO displayed a clear diurnal cycle, as expected, with noontime maximum mixing ratios of up to 15 pmol/mol.
NO$_{2}$ also displayed a diurnal cycle with amplitudes of up to 20 pmol/mol, reaching a noontime maximum of ca. 30 pmol/mol
in background air.  Our NO$_{2}$ measurements allow us to determine the importance of the BrO + NO$_{2}$ reaction as an
active-bromine sink.
Starting on April 26 (DOY 117) we observed 10 uninterrupted days of complete or near-complete boundary layer ozone depletion
at Alert.  As ozone was depleting, NOx was spiky and reached several 100 pmol/mol.  However, once ozone reached zero
nmol/mol, NO mixing ratios were reduced.  In some instances during the beginning of this 10-day period the noontime maximum
was diminished to 5 pmol/mol.  Equally NO$_{2}$ was reduced to below 10 pmol/mol.  The NOx gradient changed sign during
periods of ozone depletion, indicating that the snow surface no longer acted as a source of NOx when O$_{3}$ was depleted.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08f1648c-b6a5-434c-9a8a-6867565cb121"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We have determined high-resolution hypocentral locations for over 45,000 earthquakes that occurred from 1980-2000 in the
greater Long Valley area using a double difference earthquake relocation algorithm and routinely determined arrival time
readings. Relocations reveal numerous discrete fault planes in the south moat of the caldera and Sierran block to its south
with roughly 100 m accuracy. To resolve driving stresses on these newly imaged faults, we performed stress inversions using
focal mechanisms constrained by 50 or more first motions for events within 5 km x 5 km square bins throughout the region of
relocated seismicity.
Activity beneath the south moat of the caldera is dominated by a right-lateral $275\deg$ striking fault zone that dips
approximately $65\deg$ north. Many small, planar faults of widely varying orientations are also active within the caldera
itself, providing a variety of slip orientations that enable us to obtain well-constrained stress inversion results. These
smaller structures tend to release stress in brief, intense seismic swarms. Three roughly $20\deg$ striking, steeply-dipping,
subparallel faults host the majority of seismicity in the Sierran basement rock immediately to the south of the caldera.
Slip on these faults is principally left-lateral with a normal component. The center fault of these three hosted the May
1999, {\bf M} 5.3 event, whose aftershocks clearly delineate the slip patch for the main shock as a ring of seismicity around
its center of moment release. These three faults are truncated by the $275\deg$ south moat fault mentioned above.
Stress inversions reveal that the direction of minimum compressive stress systematically rotates across the studied region,
from a NE-SW relative extension direction in the south moat of the caldera, to an E-W relative extension direction in Round
Valley, 25 km SE of the caldera. Since E-W extension is characteristic of the western boundary of the Basin and Range
province, the stress rotation in the vicinity of the caldera appears to reflect a stress perturbation, presumably of volcanic
origin.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08f69603-b2b9-4580-a5e4-5d3e0f281842"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We use extensive Fabry-Perot interferometer measurements of Doppler shifts in the OI 630 mm nightglow from Millstone Hill to
study the storm-time dependent response of midlatitude thermospheric winds to magnetospheric disturbances.  Our results show
large wind perturbations developing shortly after enhancements in the high-latitude current systems, and decreasing with a
time constant of a few hours after magnetic quieting.  The zonal disturbance winds are mostly westward, and have largest
values in the premidnight sector.  The meridional disturbance winds are equatorward for up to about 5-6 hours after the
increase in magnetic activity when they decrease, and even become poleward, due to the arrival of the wind surge from the
conjugate ionosphere.  The zonal disturbance winds do not change much with the seasons, but the meridional perturbation winds
show the largest conjugate ionosphere surge effects during the December solstice.  We will also compare the time-dependent
response of these perturbation winds with disturbance plasma drifts measured by the Millstone Hill radar.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/08f6991c-5ad2-4699-9d62-0c97b1016f63"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A groundwater aquifer in a moraine geological environment in the Eastern part of Denmark has been subject to investigations
of travel pathways and age distribution (including residence time) of pesticides. The residence times in the different redox
zones have a large impact on the fate of the pesticides and reliable predictions of the occurrence of pesticides in an
aquifer and in water supply wells thus require a detailed resolution of the geological facies. Three geological models of
different complexity and based on different geological interpretations were defined and introduced in a 3-D numerical
groundwater model to predict the spatial distribution of groundwater age.
The basis for defining the three geological models were in all cases an extensive well log archive with typical
hydrogeological information. The simplest geological representation is an aquitard/aquifer model based on simple
interpolation of data from the well log archive and using a zonation approach. The model of intermediate complexity is a
multi-layered aquifer based on regional trends and soft information on depositional history. In the most complex model, the
geological interpretation includes identification of different geological facies based on studies of local geomorphic
features.
Numerical simulations of groundwater age were carried out using a particle tracking model that was based on a groundwater
flow model calibrated against head measurements. CFC and tritium concentrations were compared with model simulated
groundwater ages for all three geological models.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0900bb82-ecad-475a-972a-7f828cbb4e2c"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Plutonium is a reactive element, which is adsorbed by particles in seawater and scavenged from the water column.  Plutonium
isotopes, $^{239}$Pu (half-life=2.44 x 10$^{4}$ year) and $^{240}$Pu (half-life=6.58 x 10$^{3}$ year), have been added to the
surface oceans mainly as a consequence of atmospheric nuclear weapon testing, which took place mostly during the 1950s and
early 1960s.  It is important to investigate the transport process of plutonium for a highly productive coastal sea.  The
aims of this study were to measure the activities of $^{239+240}$Pu in settling particles collected in the East China Sea
continental slope by using time-series sediment traps and to discuss the transport process of plutonium on the East China Sea
continental margin.  Three mooring of sediment traps were deployed on the continental margin of the East China Sea from 26
October 1995 to 4 November 1995.  Two types of traps were used, cylindrical traps and conical time-series traps.  Two
time-series sediment traps were deployed at depths of 502 m and 30 m above the bottom (574 m).  The activities of
$^{239+240}$Pu were determined with alpha spectrometers equipped with passivated ion implanted silicon and a multichannel
analyzer.  There was little variation in $^{239+240}$Pu activities in spite of large variations of the total mass fluxes.
The $^{239+240}$Pu activities ranged from 2.8 to 3.7 mBq/g at 502 m depth and 2.9 to 4.2 mBq/g at 574 m depth and were higher
than that from the shelf edge.   The $^{239+240}$Pu fluxes showed large variations, which ranged from 4.2 to 37.4
mBq/m$^{2}$/day at 502 m depth and 3.6 to 44.9 mBq/m$^{2}$/day at 574 m depth, similar to the trend of the total mass fluxes.
Furthermore, a significant strong correlation (r=0.973) was observed between $^{239+240}$Pu flux and total mass flux.  This
is due to the fact that the $^{239+240}$Pu flux variation is largely controlled by mass flux variations.  The large fluxes
of $^{239+240}$Pu may be attributable to lateral transport of particles that slide down on the continental slope nepheloid
layer.@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
    <DataPropertyAssertion>
        <DataProperty IRI="#hasAbstract"/>
        <NamedIndividual IRI="http://data.geolink.org/id/publication/agu/0906bc98-f8d8-4ebe-9429-aeb6936ac748"/>
        <Literal datatypeIRI="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Snow covered sea ice plays an integral role in the Arctic&#39;s energy balance, owing to its influence over surface albedo,
sensible and latent heat exchange, and due to its considerable spatial and temporal variability.  The importance of the snow
cover is particularly emphasized during the snowmelt period, when surface albedos decrease rapidly and energy absorption
increases.  Monitoring spatial and temporal variations in snowmelt onset dates provides researchers with a proxy climate
indicator of past climate conditions in the Arctic.  However, in order to predict the response of snowmelt onset to future
Arctic climate conditions, where general circulation models suggest near surface warming will be rapid and pronounced, it is
necessary to associate variations in snowmelt onset to atmospheric conditions.  Accordingly, the objective of this paper is
to examine statistical and synoptic links between snowmelt onset and the Arctic Oscillation.  The Arctic Oscillation is used
to represent atmospheric conditions because it is considered the dominant form of low-frequency variability in the Northern
Hemisphere, and because previous studies of atmosphere-sea ice associations noted variations in sea ice conditions closely
resemble variations in the AO.  Monthly averaged AO values are compared with passive microwave-derived snowmelt onset dates
over first-year and multiyear Arctic sea ice from 1979-1998.  Correlation analyses indicate positive (negative) AO phases are
related to earlier (later) than average snowmelt onset along much of the Siberian coast, extending north in to the Arctic
Ocean.  Positive (negative) AO phases are also related to later (earlier) than average snowmelt onset near Baffin Island.
Composite synoptic conditions illustrate earlier (later) snowmelt onset occur when near surface air temperatures are greater
(lower) than average, mean sea level pressure is below (above) normal, and cyclonic activity is enhanced (diminished).@en</Literal>
    </DataPropertyAssertion>
